@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

This chapter explains the purpose of agent-cat and the form in which it
presents a workflow.  It also explains the practical consequences of a
mathematical meaning that is given to the workflow before the workflow runs.

@section What the project provides

@cindex workflow
@cindex authoring surface
agent-cat treats a workflow as an ordinary Haskell value.  An author writes a
program through @code{Agentic.Workflow}.  The library constructs a typed plan
from that program, records a first-order form of it for conformance testing,
and makes the same value available to the tools that describe, price, and
execute it.  The production command @command{agentic-run} can therefore answer
useful questions about a program before any addressee receives a prompt.

Three facilities belong together in this design.  The Haskell authoring surface
provides readable programs and a practical runner.  The Lean formalization gives
the represented program a precise meaning and proves the analyses that follow
from its structure.  The conformance suite compares the two realizations through
committed observations and live differential tests.  Each facility has a
separate responsibility, and the boundary between them is part of the design.

A workflow in this system is built from explicit questions, bindings, finite
branches, reusable functions, and bounded revision.  Earlier answers can appear
in later prompts, and branches can depend on flags or verdicts.  The set of
possible paths nevertheless remains finite for every program that the authoring
surface accepts.  The system can therefore compute a static cost tree before
execution.

@section Why meaning comes first

@cindex denotation
A @dfn{denotation} is the mathematical meaning assigned to a representation.
For agent-cat, that meaning describes how a workflow consults a total answer
sheet and produces a result together with an ordered semantic transcript.  This
definition fixes the subject of the proofs.  It also prevents transport policy,
memo tables, process management, and source syntax from quietly becoming part
of question identity.

The arrangement has visible consequences for an author.  A question that is
asked twice obtains the same semantic answer, and a different draw denotes a
different question.  A change to the prompt text can change the identity of a
question while the shape and the static cost of the program stay the same.  A
branch changes the set of possible paths.  An effect remains visible as an
authored request occurrence even though its semantic answer is unit.  Later
chapters derive each of these statements from the relevant representation.

@section When the design is useful

The approach suits work whose questions and finite control structure deserve
inspection before execution.  Review ladders, approval gates, bounded
draft-and-revise loops, panels, routed consultations, structured answers, and
repeatable operational checks all fit this form.  The static report shows how
many request nodes were written and which answer kinds occur on a straight
path.  It also shows the range of request counts that the finite branches
permit.

The authoring surface is Haskell rather than a separate workflow file format.
This choice brings ordinary bindings, lexical scope, modules, type checking,
pattern matching, and host-language functions to workflow construction.  It
also brings the usual cost of a typed Haskell library.  Authoring modules
enable a small set of language extensions, compiler errors are part of the
feedback loop, and runnable programs enter an explicit registry.

A program whose future structure must be computed from unrestricted answer text
falls at the dynamic rung of the underlying Plan language.  The public workflow
surface does not construct that rung.  An author can still use ordinary Haskell
inputs to choose which finite program to build before analysis begins.  Once
the program exists, its Plan remains within the analyzable fragment.

@section How to use this manual

@ref{Architecture and Guarantees} gives the boundary map and the evidence
ceiling.  The next three chapters explain the mathematical, representational,
and operational layers in that order.  @ref{Conformance Boundary} specifies the
wire format that holds the Haskell implementation to the Lean model.  A reader
who wants to write a program immediately can proceed to @ref{First Workflow}
and return to the foundations when a question about cost, sharing, or
execution requires them.

The guide chapters move from ordinary authoring to functions, inputs, schemas,
inspection, and execution.  The reference chapters collect source names, usage
forms, constructors, command-line options, defaults, and restrictions.  Each
term with a specialized meaning has one canonical definition in
@ref{Glossary}.

@node Architecture and Guarantees
@chapter Architecture and Guarantees

This chapter maps the live architecture and names the evidence that is carried
at each boundary.  It also states what a successful proof or conformance check
permits a reader to conclude.

@section Two implementations and one boundary

@cindex verification spine
@cindex production runner
Lean is the verification spine of the project.  The root module
@file{Agentic.lean} assembles the schema and question spaces, worlds,
dialogues, the typed Plan representation, denotation, the static analyses, the
commuting theorems, and the flagship workload.  The Lean checker accepts a
first-order @code{RawProgram} and returns either a diagnostic or an
intrinsically typed @code{Plan [] Unit}.  The type of a successful result
carries the basic well-formedness statement.

Haskell is the authoring and operational half.  @code{Agentic.Workflow} lowers
ordinary Haskell constructions through @code{Agentic.Builder}.  The resulting
@code{Program} contains two values that are built together.  The first value,
@code{progRawOut :: RawProgram}, is printable data that the guards, the routing
analysis, and the conformance suite use.  The second value,
@code{progPlan :: Plan '[] ()}, is the typed plan that the Haskell runner
analyzes and executes.

@smallexample
Haskell source
  -> Program
       |-> agentic-run
       |     reads RawProgram for guards and routes
       |     schedules and executes the Haskell Plan   [production]
       `-> RawProgram -> Lean checkProgram -> Lean Plan
                         |-> denote -> Dlg -> trace under Ω
                         `-> static and annotated observations
                                           [oracle/corpus testing]
@end smallexample

A production run follows the first branch and does not start Lean.  The second
branch belongs to testing.  @file{bisim/lean/Conformance.lean} implements a
line-delimited oracle.  The Haskell conformance programs compare their own
printed program and their Plan-derived observations with the replies of the
oracle.

@section The representation tower

@cindex representation tower
The architecture descends through representations of decreasing operational
detail.  Haskell source is the form an author edits.  @code{RawProgram} is a
first-order tree that crosses the language boundary.  The typed Plan retains
binders, question shapes, answer kinds, finite branches, and execution intent.
Denotation erases execution intent and interprets the Plan as a dialogue.
Running that dialogue against a world yields the mathematical result and
semantic transcript.

Execution descends along a related path.  The Haskell interpreter reads the
annotated Plan, obtains or reuses answers, and records an execution event for
each authored occurrence.  When the intent and the answer-source annotation of
an event are erased, a semantic event remains.  The theorems and the
conformance suite check this erasure.  The selected backend and the physical
behavior of an adapter stay below it.

@section What is established

The project carries several kinds of evidence.  They answer different
questions, and each must be read at its own level.

@multitable @columnfractions .23 .35 .42
@headitem Evidence @tab What it establishes @tab Principal source
@item Lean type checking
@tab Definitions and theorem statements elaborate without an admitted
placeholder in the checked modules.
@tab @file{model/Agentic/Core/} and the Lean build
@item Kernel theorems
@tab The stated equality, bound, law, or witness follows from the definitions
under the theorem's recorded axiom footprint.
@tab Named declarations in @file{model/Agentic/Core/}
@item Frozen corpus replay
@tab Haskell reproduces all 193 committed request/reply observations without
Lean in the test loop.
@tab @file{bisim/corpus/} and @command{tier0}
@item Curated surface comparison
@tab Checked Haskell programs print and observe as their frozen counterparts
specify, including folds, traces, and bills.
@tab @command{tier1} over committed cases
@item Live differential testing
@tab Generated Haskell programs and worlds agree with a prebuilt Lean oracle on
the declared versioned observation surface.
@tab @command{bisim} and conformance version 3
@item Deterministic transport gates
@tab The Haskell runner behaves as expected against local ACP and agent-deck
fixture doubles.
@tab @file{engine/acp/ci/acp.sh} and @file{engine/agent-deck/ci/deck.sh}
@end multitable

Program observations exist at three versions.  Version 2 is the frozen
bare-question semantics, and every corpus entry except the three typed-result
entries has this version.  Version 3 adds intent-bearing execution events and a
@code{semanticTrace} that is obtained by erasure, and the live differential
test uses it.  Version 4 adds a typed program result beside the unchanged
program, and the three newest corpus entries have this version.  A pass at
version 3 establishes representation parity for the annotations.  It does not
make intent part of semantic answer identity.  @ref{Conformance Boundary}
gives the wire format.

@section The evidence ceiling

@cindex evidence ceiling
A proof about denotation concerns the questions, answers, result, and semantic
transcript described by that denotation.  A theorem about a Plan fold concerns
the represented term.  A conformance result concerns fields carried by the
versioned wire record.  These statements provide no direct observation of an
external file, process, account, or service.

The @code{effect} intent authorizes and orders an occurrence according to the
runtime policy, and its semantic answer is unit.  A completed adapter turn
establishes that the adapter reported completion.  Evidence of the desired
state change requires an independent check.  Likewise, the behavior of an
observed command and the fidelity of its report require operational
verification.

Differences between transports remain operational rather than semantic.  ACP
exposes a stop reason, so the runner can require a completed turn for effects
and for questions that are addressed to a person.  The agent-deck command line
exposes no corresponding stop reason.  The manual states this distinction
wherever an operator chooses an engine, because a theorem about the common Plan
cannot erase a fact about the transport.

@section Where the documentation lives

@cindex documentation map
This manual is the reference for the authoring surface, the runner, and the
boundaries between the Lean model, the Haskell implementation, and the
conformance suite.  The root @file{README.md} orients a reader who meets the
repository for the first time.  @file{doc/meaning-and-representation.md}
gives the denotational argument at length.  It explains what a workflow means,
what is written in its place, and the theorems that bind the two.  Each source
directory carries a @file{README.md} that states its purpose, its public
modules, its dependencies, and its commands.  Each directory also carries an
@file{AGENTS.md} that states the conventions that a maintainer follows there.

@file{doc/research/} holds the design records and research dossiers, indexed
by its own @file{README.md}.  Current source, tests, and this manual govern
present behavior, and executable evidence takes precedence where prose has
drifted.

@cindex authoring language
The authoring language is the Haskell surface.  Lean receives a program only
as the first-order @code{RawProgram} that Haskell emits, which
@file{model/Agentic/Core/Dsl/Check.lean} checks and elaborates.

@node Mathematical Meaning
@chapter Mathematical Meaning

This chapter defines the mathematical object that a workflow denotes.  The
order of the sections is deliberate.  Answers and questions come first,
dialogues organize their interaction, and the Plan representation receives its
meaning from that space.

@section Answer kinds and values

@cindex answer kind
@cindex structured answer
@code{Code} is the closed universe of answer kinds.  Four of its constructors
are built in, and they are text, verdict, flag, and receipt.  A fifth
constructor carries a structural schema.  The family @code{El} interprets each
code as its value type.  Text is a string, a verdict is a value of the verdict
algebra, a flag is a Boolean, a receipt is unit, and a structured code is the
value that its schema describes.

A schema is built from null, Boolean, integer, exact rational, string, array,
and named product fields.  Its semantic value is ordinary algebraic data.  JSON
is one boundary representation for that value, and @code{Agentic.Schema.Json}
supplies its validation and its exact rational encoding.  The semantic domain
itself does not depend on JSON.

@section Questions and worlds

@cindex question identity
@cindex world
A question @code{Q c} records the addressee, scope, prompt, and draw for an
answer of code @code{c}.  The addressee is a model, tool, person, or executable
tool target.  Scope carries authored policy such as the serving-model axis.
The prompt contains the words put to the addressee.  The draw distinguishes
deliberate resampling from reuse.

A @dfn{world} is a total answer sheet:

@example
Ω = (c : Code) -> Q c -> El c
@end example

For every possible typed question, a world supplies one answer.  Equal
questions therefore have equal answers in every world.  A repeated occurrence
of the same question remains a repeated event in the transcript, although a
runtime can obtain the answer only once.  An independent draw changes the
question, and the changed question can receive another answer.

The total function is a mathematical device rather than an instruction to
compute every answer in advance.  A runtime constructs a finite table as a run
proceeds.  @file{model/Agentic/Core/World.lean} relates that table to a total world
through extension and counterfactual pinning.

@section Dialogues

@cindex dialogue
A @dfn{dialogue} is a question-and-answer tree with two forms.  It either
returns a value or asks one typed question and chooses its continuation from the
answer:

@example
Dlg A = done A
      | ask (c : Code) (q : Q c) (El c -> Dlg A)
@end example

The operation @code{run} follows a dialogue through a world and returns its
value.  The operation @code{trace} follows the same path and records an ordered
list of semantic events, each containing the code, question, and answer.  The
meaning used by the project is the coherent pair of these observations:

@example
meaning p ω = (run ω p, trace ω p)
@end example

Both observations respect monadic sequencing.  The theorems @code{run_bind}
and @code{trace_bind} state that observing a composed dialogue agrees with
composing the observations in the prescribed way.  This is the first place
where the Haskell shape of dependent questions receives a mathematical law
rather than an implementation convention.

@section Equality of meaning

@cindex observational equality
Two dialogues are observationally equal when every world gives them the same
result and semantic transcript.  The relation is named @code{Obs} in
@file{model/Agentic/Core/Dlg.lean}.  It is an equivalence and a congruence for bind,
which permits laws proved through denotation to descend to represented
operations.

The semantic function is not injective.  @code{Dlg.not_forcing} exhibits two
structurally different dialogues that agree in every world.  Structural
equality of dialogue trees is therefore stronger than equality of workflow
meaning.  The general forcing claim is recorded as false, and a repeat-free
fragment supports a narrower theorem.

@section Verdicts and panels

@cindex verdict algebra
Verdicts combine in reviewer order.  An approval leaves another verdict
unchanged, and a refusal determines the combined result.  Two objections
combine into one objection whose reason list is the concatenation of their
ordered reason lists.  The order records the order in which the reviewers
spoke.

A verdict panel asks every member and applies these rules to the answers in list
order.  Every member must approve for the panel to approve.  A refusal carries
no objection list, so rendering the resulting verdict as text yields an empty
reason string.  This behavior deserves attention when a revision prompt expects
an explanation.

A text panel uses the same fan-out with another result algebra.  Each answer is
placed in a named block, and blocks are concatenated in member order.  A
member's own closing tag is escaped inside its body so that the body cannot
forge the end of its block.

@section Plan denotation

@cindex Plan denotation
The typed Plan is a representation of a dialogue.  Its denotation has the
following type:

@example
denote : Plan Γ A -> Env Γ -> Dlg A
@end example

The context @code{Γ} records the kinds of answers in scope, and @code{Env Γ}
holds those answers.  A closed ask becomes a dialogue ask immediately.  An
open ask evaluates its prompt expression in the environment, forms the full
question, and then asks it.  A finite case selects the denotation of one arm.
The dynamic form computes and denotes a plan from an unrestricted answer.

Execution intent is absent from this meaning.  A represented request is a
question that is paired with an intent, and denotation projects the question.
The theorems @code{denote_askC_intent_irrel} and
@code{denote_ask_intent_irrel} state that a change to the intent alone leaves
the dialogue unchanged.  This placement preserves bare-question answer
identity, and it lets the runtime interpret consultation, observation, and
effect differently.

@section Commuting operations

@cindex commuting theorem
A commuting equation justifies each operation on Plans.  To build a Plan and
then denote it must agree with the application of the corresponding operation
to the denoted dialogues.  The five Plan formers provide the defining
equations.  Substitution, scope changes, grafting, panels, and bounded revision
have derived equations in @file{model/Agentic/Core/Morphism.lean} and
@file{model/Agentic/Core/Denote.lean}.

Several familiar laws follow through these equations.  A graft denotes a
dialogue bind, and it carries the unit and associativity laws.  Mapping and
pairing inherit their functorial and applicative laws.  A scope acts by a
reindexing of the world for both values and transcripts.  A panel combines the
answers of its members with the answer algebra, and a bounded revision denotes
its finite check-and-revise unrolling.

The five Plan forms also determine every fold that obeys their equations.
@code{PlanAlg} names those five operations, and @code{PlanAlg.fold_unique}
states the uniqueness property.  Denotation, the level fold, substitution, the
graft operation, and most structural reports use this common recursion
scheme.

@section The flagship meaning

@cindex flagship workflow
The flagship workflow asks for a draft and reviews it through a three-member
panel under at most two amendments.  Two reviewers and every amendment quote a
house style guide that the source defines.  The workflow then asks a person for
consent, and it performs an apply effect only on the consenting branch.  Its
meaning is written directly as a dialogue in
@file{model/Agentic/Core/HardenPatch.lean}, and the same module represents it
as a Plan.

The named theorems establish five principal facts.  Refusal of consent excludes
the apply receipt from the semantic trace.  The Plan lies at the branch rung.
At most three drafts are requested.  Its cost tree has nine leaves with a
folded minimum of four and maximum of fourteen request occurrences.  The
minimum leaf is unattainable, and every actual world produces one of seven
bills: 5, 6, 9, 10, 12, 13, or 14.  The dialogue run is total.  The guide is a
define and is consulted by no question, so the property that a shared reading
costs one event is proved instead on the two-reviewer example of
@file{model/Agentic/Core/Denote.lean}.

@file{model/Agentic/Core/DslFlagship.lean} checks the first-order flagship
term.  It proves that the transcript of the term agrees with the transcript of
the hand-written Plan in four named worlds, and one of these worlds follows the
longest demonstrated path.  The file states plainly that a transcript
agreement for all worlds is not proved.  This distinction keeps the evidence
about the flagship commensurate with its theorem statements.

@node Representation and Analysis
@chapter Representation and Analysis

This chapter describes the typed Plan, the first-order program emitted beside
it, and the analyses obtained by folding over the finished term.

@section Contexts, variables, and expressions

@cindex de Bruijn index
A Plan is indexed by a context @code{Γ}, represented as a list of answer codes.
An environment @code{Env Γ} contains one value of the corresponding type for
each entry.  Variables are de Bruijn membership proofs, so a reference identifies
a binding by its position in the context rather than by a source-level string.
Substitutions rename or supply these variables while preserving their types.

The Haskell authoring layer hides this machinery behind ordinary lexical
variables.  A binding hands a typed handle to the remainder of its @code{W.do}
block.  Prompt holes and later constructions consume that handle.  The handle
carries the printed name, which is generated or selected by the author,
together with the typed variable that reads its value.  A misspelled Haskell
variable is consequently a normal compiler error.

Expressions are pure functions of an environment.  They hold constants, read
variables, and combine earlier answers into later prompt text.  The shape of a
question stays outside the expression.  This design fact permits question
shapes and costs to remain static while the words depend on answers.

@section The five Plan forms

@cindex Plan form
The core representation has five forms.  Haskell prefixes their constructors
with @code{P}, and Lean presents the same family through @code{PlanF}.

@table @code
@item ret
Return an expression.  This is a leaf of the represented workflow.

@item askC
Ask a closed request whose prompt is already part of the term, bind the answer,
and continue.

@item ask
Compute prompt words from the current environment, combine them with a fixed
request shape, bind the answer, and continue.

@item case
Choose one of a finite, fully represented set of arms from a Boolean, verdict
tag, or three-way revision ending.

@item dyn
Compute a Plan from an unrestricted answer.  This form marks the dynamic rung
at which no general finite cost tree exists.
@end table

Substitution, sequencing, mapping, pairing, panels, and revision are derived
operations rather than extra forms.  @code{graft} replaces every return leaf
with a context-polymorphic continuation, and it provides sequencing without
@code{dyn}.  General value-dependent Plan construction through @code{bindP} is
the operation that reaches the dynamic form.

@section RawProgram and elaboration

@cindex RawProgram
@code{RawProgram} is a first-order syntax tree containing a function table and
a main block.  Its nodes record asks, bindings, effects, finite cases, calls,
known-context assertions, and both forms of bounded revision.  It contains no
Haskell closures and can therefore be encoded as JSON for the conformance
boundary.

The Haskell builder emits a @code{RawProgram} and a typed Haskell Plan from the
same combinator application.  The Lean function @code{checkProgram}
independently checks the first-order tree and elaborates it to a Lean Plan.
The checker resolves names into de Bruijn expressions and fixes answer kinds
from their ground uses.  It keeps a revision result pending until its required
case.  It chooses a closed ask exactly when every prompt component is literal
after the defines are expanded.

A successful checker result is already a closed, well-typed Plan.  The theorem
@code{Dsl.checkProgram_level_le} adds the substantive global fact that every
accepted program lies at or below the branch rung.  The Haskell authoring
surface enforces many refusals through its types, or it makes the invalid state
impossible to represent.  The frozen corpus retains the Lean refusal contract
for the first-order boundary.

@section Validation contract and authoring hazards

@cindex checker contract
The checker advertises a concrete validation contract.  Every prompt hole names
a live binder or an earlier define at a compatible kind.  Every finite branch
uses a flag, verdict, or revision ending and supplies all arms in the required
order.  Every binding agrees with the kind imposed by its source and first
ground use.  Live names remain distinct from one another and from defines.
A @code{knownHere} assertion names exactly the current context.

The Haskell surface moves most of this contract into types and lexical scope.
An arm exports no binding to a later join, because each arm is the rest of the
workflow, and because the Plan context contains answer codes rather than sum
values.  Place a shared tail in a function and call it from each arm.  The
explicit duplication makes its cost visible in the source.

The answer kind belongs to the represented question shape.  A define such as a
flag-format instruction helps the addressee produce that kind, while the
decoder still follows the typed code.  If the prose instruction drifts from the
code, the type remains authoritative, and the answering service can require
recovery.  Live transports append their current answer specification for this
reason.

Given the same world, denotation follows the same dialogue path and produces the
same result and semantic transcript.  This determinism is a property of the
meaning function.  Runtime concurrency preserves Plan-order observations while
approximating the total answer sheet through actual services.

Editing a question's words creates a different key in the world and does not
preserve its answer.  The question shape, Plan level, node counts, and cost tree
remain unchanged when only prompt text changes.  Defines provide the same
edit-preservation for every prompt in which they are expanded.

Two review rules govern additions to the language.  Whether a former is enabled
depends only on state represented inside its own subterm, so local syntax remains
sufficient to understand control.  When a construction's cost cannot be read
from its existing notation, the notation must expose that cost rather than
leaving it to hidden elaboration.

A bounded revision is unrolled into distinct round questions, and memoization
does not collapse its iterations when the candidate or the review words differ.
A one-member verdict panel uses the same reducer as a larger panel, and that
reducer requires every member to approve.  A future reducer with an explicit
name can therefore be added without a change to the meaning of the existing
form.

@section The four analysis rungs

@cindex analysis level
@code{level} is a fold over a complete Plan.  It classifies the represented
term, and a branch node counts as a branch even when its arms behave alike.
The four values form the chain @code{batch <= pipeline <= branch <= dynamic}.

@multitable @columnfractions .16 .31 .53
@headitem Rung @tab Structural criterion @tab Available information
@item batch
@tab Closed asks and returns
@tab Exact question list and a world-independent bill.
@item pipeline
@tab Open asks permitted, and no case or dynamic form
@tab Exact count, answer-code sequence, and question-shape sequence.  The bill
is exact for prices that shape determines.
@item branch
@tab Finite cases permitted, and no dynamic form
@tab A finite multiset that contains the bill of every world, with sound folded
bounds.  The set of bills that worlds produce has an attained least and
greatest member, and these can differ from the folded extrema of the multiset.
@item dynamic
@tab A dynamic form occurs
@tab No general finite bill set.  The formalization includes an unbounded witness.
@end multitable

Closed questions add no rung.  An open ask joins in @code{pipeline}.  A finite
case joins in @code{branch} together with the levels of all arms.  A dynamic
node returns @code{dynamic}.  This fold describes terms and does not infer
behavior from traces, because an inference from traces loses the structural
premises that the cost theorems use.

The Haskell workflow surface emits no dynamic node.  Functions are open
straight-line Plans, and a call substitutes arguments and grafts the callee's
body at the call site.  Branches remain finite.  Bounded revision unrolls a
fixed number of rounds.  These constructions preserve the theorem that every
authored program has a finite cost tree.

@section Structural reports

@cindex static fold
The runner reports several pure folds from the Haskell Plan.  @code{level} names
the analysis rung.  @code{size} counts represented nodes across all arms.
@code{askNodes} counts request nodes written in the Plan, also across all arms.
@code{codes} gives the answer-code sequence for a pipeline and returns no single
sequence when a branch exists.  @code{schemaRequirements} collects the
structured schemas that a Plan can request.

These values describe the term before a world selects one path.  An ask-node
count is therefore distinct from a run's bill.  A branch contributes every arm
to @code{size} and @code{askNodes}, while one execution follows only the
selected arm.

@anchor{Cost trees and bills}
@section Cost trees and bills

@cindex cost tree
@cindex fresh bill
A price maps a semantic question to a value in a cost carrier.  Folding that
price over a semantic transcript gives @code{billFresh}.  Under a shape-based
price, the branch-rung analysis @code{costM} produces a finite multiset whose
leaves are the bills obtained by the syntactic branch combinations.  The
summary reports its minimum fold, maximum fold, and number of paths.

The bill of every world belongs to the finite multiset, so it lies between the
minimum and the maximum folds.  Separately, the bills that all worlds produce
have a least member and a greatest member, and a world attains each of them.
These actual extrema can differ from the extrema of the syntactic tree, because
separate cases can be correlated.  The flagship demonstrates the distinction.
Its tree contains a leaf at five requests, while its direct meaning theorem
proves that every actual world bills at least six.  A displayed minimum is
therefore the folded tree minimum, unless a reachability theorem says more.

@code{billMemo} belongs to execution rather than to principal meaning.  Consult
and observe occurrences can share one answer by bare question identity between
two acts, whereas effects are retained per occurrence.  The operational bill can consequently
fall below the fresh semantic bill when repeated reusable questions occur.
@ref{Operational Model} gives the policy that produces this difference.

@section Printed names and alpha comparison

A Haskell library cannot recover the spelling of a source binder.  The authoring
surface generates @code{b0}, @code{b1}, and later binding names from depth, and
uses @code{r0}, @code{r1}, and later names for revision results.  @code{named}
overrides a binding's printed name without changing its meaning.  Names of the
generated shapes are reserved so that an author cannot claim one at another
depth.

Prompt holes read the name carried by their handle, so a generated or selected
name remains consistent with every use.  The curated conformance cases compare
authoring-surface programs up to scope-aware alpha-renaming where generated
binders differ from the frozen spelling.  Function and parameter names are
written explicitly and remain exact.

@node Operational Model
@chapter Operational Model

This chapter explains how the Haskell Plan is interpreted during a run.  The
mathematical question remains the key for answers.  The execution intent
determines reuse, ordering, permission, completion checks, and event
annotation.

@anchor{Requests and intent}
@section Requests and intent

@cindex execution intent
@cindex intent, purpose of
A represented request has the form @code{Request c = Q c × Intent c}.  The
question determines semantic answer identity.  The intent describes how one
authored occurrence is to be realized.  This section explains why the
representation carries that second component, what it refines, and what the
runtime obtains from it.

@subsection Why the representation needs intent

The mathematical meaning identifies a question by four fields.  These fields
are the addressee, the scope, the words, and the draw.  A world supplies one
answer to each such question.  That identity is exactly what the theorems
need, and it says nothing about how an occurrence is carried out.  A runtime
that executes a Plan must make decisions that the meaning does not record.  It
must decide whether an occurrence can reuse an answer that was already
obtained for the same bare question.  It must decide whether an occurrence runs
every time it is reached, because the occurrence acts on the world.  It must
decide whether the addressee can receive tool permission, and in what order
the occurrence stands relative to other acts.  Neither the answer code nor the
addressee settles these questions.  A receipt-valued question to a tool can be
a harmless consultation or an act on the world.  The author therefore declares
the intent on each occurrence, and the runtime reads it.

Intent belongs to the representation and never to the question.  Worlds stay
indexed by @code{Q}, so the answer to a question cannot depend on the policy
under which the question is asked.  Two workflows that ask the same questions
in the same order and hear the same answers therefore have the same meaning
under every execution policy.  Intent refines the five-form Plan without a
sixth form, and denotation projects the question and forgets the intent.  The
theorems @code{denote_askC_intent_irrel} and @code{denote_ask_intent_irrel} in
@file{model/Agentic/Core/Denote.lean} state that a change to the intent of a
request alone leaves its dialogue unchanged.  Both theorems are proved by
reflexivity.

@subsection The three intents

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .23 .24 .35
@headitem Intent @tab Source position @tab Reuse policy @tab Operational role
@item consult
@tab Ordinary value question
@tab Reusable by bare question until the next act
@tab Obtain an answer from a model, tool, or person.
@item observe
@tab Executable tool in value position
@tab Reusable by bare question until the next act
@tab Run an argv to obtain a typed observation.
@item effect
@tab Statement-position act
@tab Execute per occurrence
@tab Reserve the effect lane and request permission.  The result kind is receipt.
@end multitable

The position of a request in the source determines its annotation, and no
answer can change it.  An ordinary @code{ask}, a @code{confirm}, a panel
member, or a revision question lowers to a consultation.  A tool that
@code{running} modifies lowers to an observation when its answer is bound.  The
same executable tool in statement position lowers to an effect.  The Lean
checker applies the same rule to the first-order program in the function
@code{valueIntent} of @file{model/Agentic/Core/Dsl/Check.lean}.  The
@code{effect} constructor exists only at the receipt code, so no Plan can
branch on an alleged result of an effect.  Intent lives in the shape of the
request rather than in the prompt words, so the computation of words from
earlier answers cannot alter it.

@subsection What intent provides during a run

The annotation gives the runtime five things that the bare question cannot
give.  Consult and observe occurrences share one memo table that is keyed by
the bare question, so a repeated question is answered once between two acts.
An act clears the table, because it may change what any later question
answers, so a question repeated after an act is asked again.  Effect
occurrences never read or populate that table.  The runtime dispatches every
effect and bills each one per occurrence, and this is the difference between
@code{billFresh} and @code{billMemo}.  Under the ACP transport, only an effect
receives tool permission, and the runtime denies permission to consultations
and observations in the words of the protocol.  Effects enter an ordered lane,
so a later act cannot overtake an earlier one.  Every other occurrence waits
behind that lane, so it starts only after each effect that precedes it in Plan
order has completed.  An effect in turn waits for every occurrence that
precedes it, so an act cannot change what an earlier question reads.  ACP requires a completed turn
before an effect is credited with a receipt.  Every execution event retains the
authored request beside its answer source.  A trace therefore states whether an
answer was reused, or which dispatched question produced it after a fail-over.

The theorem @code{Plan.execAnnotated_correct} in
@file{model/Agentic/Core/AnnotatedExec.lean} ties these policies back to the
meaning.  The annotated executor returns the semantic value and extends the
bare-question table.  It also produces an execution trace that erases, event by
event, to the semantic trace.  Conformance version 3 compares the annotated
trace between Lean and Haskell and returns the erased @code{semanticTrace}
beside it.  The placement is therefore checked at the boundary as well as in
the kernel.

@subsection What intent does not establish

The tags state authored policy and never physical outcome.  An @code{observe}
tag does not prove that a command is read-only.  An @code{effect} tag does not
prove that a change occurred, and a completed adapter turn establishes only
that the adapter reported completion.  Evidence about the external world
requires an independent observation.  A workflow can be written to include one,
as @ref{Permission and external effects} describes.

@anchor{Memoization and event identity}
@section Memoization and event identity

@cindex memoization
The reusable memo table is keyed by the bare typed question together with the
number of effects that precede the occurrence in Plan order.  Consult and
observe occurrences read and populate it.  Effect occurrences bypass it in both
directions, which preserves each authored effect and its operational charge,
and each effect starts a new stretch in which nothing earlier is reused.
Persisted answers carry the same key, so a resumed run and a lineage child
reuse an answer only within the stretch that produced it.  A
consultation and an observation of the same bare question can therefore share
one underlying answer, and they still produce two annotated execution events
with their respective intents.

Every Plan occurrence records an event.  The event retains the authored
request, the answer, and an answer source.  A reused source records memo reuse.
An asked source records the question that was actually dispatched after
fail-over relabeling.  When the source and the intent are erased, the authored
bare-question semantic event remains.  The version 3 conformance record
includes both the annotated trace and this @code{semanticTrace}.

The runner reports two counts after a run.  @code{billFresh} counts the request
occurrences reached on the selected path.  @code{billMemo} deduplicates reusable
questions and retains every effect occurrence.  Re-asks caused by an unreadable
answer belong to transport recovery and are narrated separately rather than
changing the represented Plan.

@section Dependency-driven scheduling

@cindex scheduler
The interpreter schedules a request when every variable used by its prompt
expression is available and every effect that precedes it in Plan order has
completed.  An effect also waits for every request that precedes it.  Independent siblings that no effect separates can overlap even when
their authored order is sequential in the Plan.  A finite branch schedules only
the selected arm.  Equal reusable requests racing for the same key share one
memo reservation.

Observed traces remain in Plan order.  Stateful ACP and agent-deck backends have
FIFO lanes, and effects use an intent-selected ordered lane.  Lane positions are
reserved during traversal, which prevents a later ready request from overtaking
an earlier blocked request on the same lane.  A consultation or an observation
waits behind the tail of the effect lane without taking a position in it.  It
therefore observes every earlier effect, and it does not delay the requests
that wait beside it.  An effect waits for every consultation and observation
reserved since the previous effect, so each act is ordered against everything
before it and everything after it.

This concurrency policy is a realization of the Plan's data dependencies.  The
semantic transcript remains the ordered authored observation, while wall-clock
overlap and backend conversation state belong to execution.

@section Executable tools

@cindex executable tool
@code{tool "gate" `running` ("nix", ["flake", "check"])} records an argv
without a shell.  The prompt is written to standard input.  The command and its
arguments are program text and have no interpolation syntax, which keeps prompt
data out of the argv.

The exit status is converted where the answer kind can express failure.  A
flag receives success or failure.  A verdict receives approval on success and
the first failing line on failure.  A result kind without such a representation
causes the run to abandon.  Statement position produces a receipt only through
the effect policy.  A command in value position that follows an act starts after
the act has completed, so it reads what the act wrote.  The act also clears the
memo table, so an observation repeated after it runs the command again.

The program-authored intent cannot establish the physical nature of an argv.
An observation tag does not make a command read-only.  An effect tag controls
the runner's permission and ordering decision, while verification of the
external result remains an operational responsibility.

@section In-process tools

@cindex in-process tool
A registry row can answer named tools with Haskell functions.  The field
@code{rowTools} of a @code{Row} maps a tool identifier to a @code{Tool}, and a
live run answers every question put to that identifier by calling the
function.  The registration leaves the workflow unchanged.  It still writes
@code{ask (tool "shout") [wf|@{capital@}|]}, and its printed Program, its
analyses, and its bills are those of any other tool question.

A @code{Tool} pairs one answer code with a function of the run's working
directory and the rendered prompt.  @code{textTool}, @code{flagTool}, and
@code{receiptTool} build the text, flag, and receipt forms.  The directory is
the one in which @code{running} commands execute, so a tool that writes a file
writes it beside the files that the acts of the run write.

@example
capitalTools :: [(Text, Tool)]
capitalTools =
  [ ("save", receiptTool (\dir words' -> TIO.writeFile (dir </> "capital.txt") words')),
    ("shout", textTool (\_ words' -> pure (T.toUpper words'))),
    ("announce", receiptTool (\dir words' -> TIO.writeFile (dir </> "announcement.txt") words'))
  ]
@end example

Intent follows the source position, as it does for every question.  A
registered tool whose answer is bound is a consultation, so its answer is
reused by bare question until the next act.  A registered tool in statement
position is an
effect.  It runs once per occurrence in the ordered effect lane, and every
later question waits for it to complete.

A registered tool answers at one code.  A Program that asks a registered tool
at another code is refused with exit status 1 before any backend starts.  A
question behind a dynamic continuation is invisible to that check, and it
abandons the run when it is reached.

An in-process tool is not engine-bound.  It takes no transport lane, it needs
no route, and routing-only coverage requires no pin for it.  The run header
names the tools that are answered in process and the directory in which they
run.  Scripted execution calls no registered tool, and its table answers those
questions in the same way as every other question.  The @code{capital} example
registers three tools and carries a model answer through them to two written
files.

@section Answer decoding and recovery

@cindex answer decoding
All answering services end at the same typed decode loop.  Text is accepted as
text.  Flags, verdicts, receipts, and structured values use their corresponding
decoders.  Structured replies are checked against the carried schema before
they enter the Plan environment.

An undecodable answer is re-asked once under the default settings, with the
unreadable words quoted back and the expected form stated again.  Transport
refusal and empty or protocol-invalid replies have separate gap classes and do
not receive that same retry.  Exhausted recovery abandons the run rather than
inventing a typed answer.

A @code{servedBy} chain can name alternate models through
@code{fallingBackTo}.  A gap can advance through that chain.  The authored
question remains fixed, while the dispatched target and the failed attempt are
operational facts.  A successful alternate is recorded as the answer source,
and the failed attempt is narrated on standard error.

@section Answering services

The runner can obtain answers from three kinds of service.

@table @asis
@item Scripted table
@code{--scripted} uses the registered program's canned replies.  It starts no
adapter, asks no person, executes no external tool, and calls no in-process
tool.  Replies still pass
through the normal decoder.

@item ACP adapter
@code{--engine acp} starts one or more adapter processes and owns their pipes.
By default, each question receives a new ACP session.  ACP reports a stop reason,
so the runner can require a completed turn for effects and person-addressed
questions.  Effect intent is the only intent granted tool permission.

@item agent-deck session
@code{--session} sends questions into a session that already exists.  The runner
takes a timestamp before each send, polls until the session is idle, and accepts
only output newer than the prior timestamp.  The command line reports no stop
reason, so this engine cannot perform ACP's completed-turn check.
@end table

All three services receive the same typed request and use the same decode and
recovery loop.  The live transports send the authored prompt with any required
reply-format instruction, without adding a bookkeeping header.  The scripted
table selects bytes directly from the authored prompt.  Session ownership,
permission channels, and completion evidence differ.  @ref{Running Workflows}
gives the corresponding operator commands and safety rules.

@section Routing and fail-over

@cindex routing
A serving-model pin is authored on a model question through @code{servedBy}.
The primary pin participates in the scope of the represented question.
Alternate pins are execution policy that is collected before the run.  An
added alternate changes the printed program, but it leaves the elaborated Plan,
the denotation, and the static cost unchanged.

With no explicit engine or session, a live run loads routing configuration and
requires every engine-bound question to carry a configured serving-model pin.
There is no default backend.  An explicit engine or session instead supplies a
default backend, and @code{--route NAME=BACKEND} can add serving-model routes.
Unrouted pins, unpinned model questions, tools,
and people use that explicit default.  Routing selects an answering service
after the program and its static analyses already exist.  It therefore changes
neither the shape of the Plan nor its price.

Schema version 2 adds an operational persona above the same resolver.  The user
file owns environment references, engines, catalogues, concrete model aliases,
persona allowlists, and profile chains.  A project file can select a persona and
replace whole profiles, but it cannot introduce those privileged objects.  The
selected persona is never a workflow input or a @code{run.*} fact.  Exact or
deterministically ordered-prefix selectors freeze one model identifier and its
non-secret provenance before an engine starts.

Fail-over occurs within the authored pin chain.  Routing can place different
pins in different ACP adapters or agent-deck sessions.  One chain can therefore
cross providers, and no backend identity enters the semantic question keys.
The run facts that @ref{Functions Inputs and Schemas} describes let a program
state and inspect the resolved operational arrangement in its prompts.

@anchor{Permission and external effects}
@section Permission and external effects

@cindex permission
Under ACP, permission follows intent.  Consultations and observations decline
tool permission, and effects can grant it within the configured scratch
directory.  The grant remains a policy decision that is communicated to the
adapter.  The adapter, the directory, the requested tool call, and the external
system determine what actually happens.

A scratch directory bounds where the ACP process starts and where an authorized
act is intended to write.  The default is a fresh temporary directory.  An
operator who intends to alter a working tree must name that tree deliberately
with @code{--scratch}.  This action carries consequences beyond the inspection
or the rehearsal of a workflow.

A run can finish successfully and produce no useful external change.  It can
also receive a plausible report from an addressee whose underlying action was
incomplete.  When the external result matters, the workflow must therefore
include explicit observations or independent checks.  The Plan and its proofs
can structure these checks, and the observations of the checks provide evidence
about the external result.

@node Conformance Boundary
@chapter Conformance Boundary

This chapter specifies the wire format through which the Haskell
implementation is held to the Lean model, and it states exactly what the frozen
corpus pins.  A reader who only writes and runs workflows can skip it.  A
reader who changes the elaboration, the cost algebra, the interpreter, or the
string layer must read it, because every such change is measured here.

@section The oracle process

@cindex conformance oracle
@command{lake exe conformance-oracle}, which is built in @file{bisim/}, is a
line-delimited JSON process.  It reads one request per line on standard input
and writes one reply per line on standard output.  When a request carries an
@code{id}, the reply echoes it verbatim.  End of file on standard input is the
clean shutdown.  The oracle imports the Lean model through a local Lake
dependency, and it deliberately excludes the expensive flagship module, so it
builds in seconds.

Every request can carry @code{budgetMs}, whose default is 30000.  When the
budget expires, the reply is @code{@{"timeout": @{"ms": n@}@}}.  A timeout is
an observation rather than an error.  The two implementations differ in their
asymptotic behavior, and an asymmetry must be recordable as an asymmetry.

The oracle answers three kinds of request, which are a program observation, a
string operation, and a liveness check.  A liveness request
@code{@{"id": @dots{}, "ping": true@}} receives @code{@{"id": @dots{}, "pong": true@}}.

@section Program observation

@cindex program observation
A program request has the form

@example
@{"id": @dots{}, "version": 2 | 3 | 4, "result": <Code>,
 "program": <RawProgram>, "worlds": [<WorldSpec>, @dots{}], "budgetMs": @dots{}@}
@end example

@code{RawProgram} is the Lean datatype @code{Agentic.Core.Dsl.RawProgram}
under the derived JSON encoding of Lean, which encodes constructors as
single-key objects and structures as field objects.  The corpus files are the
normative examples of that encoding.  The record is exactly @code{fns} and
@code{main}.  An omitted @code{version} means 2.  @code{worlds} defaults to the
single echo world.  A world can add exact structured answers as a list of
schema and value pairs, and every entry is validated while the request is
decoded.  A world that omits a schema that the checked Plan can query is
rejected before evaluation.  @code{result} is required only at version 4, where
it is imposed on every terminal of the program.  Versions 2 and 3 are
receipt-valued.

A reply is refused, checked, or timed out.  A refusal names one of seven
guards:

@example
@{"refused": @{"guard": "panelEmpty" | "revisionBound" | "questionBudget"
                    | "servedBy" | "dupFunction" | "deciderEmpty" | "other",
             "n": <Nat> | null,
             "pos": @{"line": @dots{}, "col": @dots{}@}, "excerpt": "@dots{}", "message": "@dots{}"@}@}
@end example

The guard is compared, and for the question budget the computed @code{n} is
compared too, because both implementations produce them.  The position, the
excerpt, and the message are functions of written characters and of the
wording of the checker.  The Haskell builder never has those, so these fields
are oracle-only.  The oracle assigns the guard by a match on the message text
of the checker.  @code{deciderEmpty} covers a decider with no needle and a
decider with an empty needle, because both are a test that is constantly true
or constantly false.  @code{panelEmpty} covers an empty verdict panel and an
empty text panel.  @code{other} covers every refusal outside the six term-level
guards, and those refusals are diagnostics rather than comparands.

A checked reply carries the static folds and one record per world:

@example
@{"result": <Code>,                            version 4 only
 "level": "batch" | "pipeline" | "branch" | "dynamic",
 "size": <Nat>, "askNodes": <Nat>,
 "codes": [<Code>, @dots{}] | null,           null on any branching program
 "costSummary": @{"minFold": <Nat>|null, "maxFold": <Nat>|null, "paths": <Nat>@},
 "blockAsks": <Nat>,
 "fnAsks": [["name", <Nat>], @dots{}],
 "worlds": [@{"world": <WorldSpec>,
             "trace": [<Event>, @dots{}],
             "semanticTrace": [<Event>, @dots{}],  versions 3 and 4
             "result": <answer>,                  version 4
             "billFresh": <Nat>, "billMemo": <Nat>@}, @dots{}]@}
@end example

@code{codes} is a pipeline-only comparand, and it is null whenever a case node
exists.  @code{costSummary} gives the folded minimum, the folded maximum, and
the leaf count of the cost tree.  @code{blockAsks} and @code{fnAsks} are the
first-order ask counts, and both sides can compute them without a Plan.  At
version 2, each trace event is a bare-question semantic event.  At versions 3
and 4, the @code{trace} holds annotated execution events, and
@code{semanticTrace} holds the same occurrences after intent erasure.  Version
4 also returns the program result, as a code at the top level and as a value in
each world.

An event is data and never a rendering:

@example
@{"code": <Code>,
 "intent": "consult" | "observe" | "effect",      versions 3 and 4
 "addressee": @{"model" | "tool" | "person": @{"id": "@dots{}"@}@}
            | @{"toolExec": @{"id": "@dots{}", "cmd": "@dots{}", "args": ["@dots{}", @dots{}]@}@},
 "scope": @{"model": "@dots{}" | null, "mode": "@dots{}" | null@},
 "prompt": "@dots{}", "draw": <Nat>,
 "answer": <string> | @{"tag": "approve"@} | @{"tag": "declined"@}
         | @{"tag": "object", "objections": ["@dots{}", @dots{}]@}
         | <bool> | null | <SchemaValue>@}
@end example

A code is one of the strings @code{text}, @code{verdict}, @code{flag}, and
@code{receipt}, or it is the structured family
@code{@{"json": @{"schema": <Schema>@}@}}.  The tag @code{json} is a wire
spelling only.  The semantic constructor is @code{Code.structured}, and
diagnostics say @code{structured}.  A schema has one of four forms.  It is one
of the five primitive strings @code{null}, @code{boolean}, @code{integer},
@code{number}, and @code{string}.  It is an array form
@code{@{"array": @{"items": <Schema>@}@}}.  It is the empty object
@code{"object"}.  Or it is a property form
@code{@{"property": @{"name": "@dots{}", "schema": <Schema>, "rest": <Schema>@}@}}.
A schema value is a total exact boundary representation.  Unit, booleans,
integers, and strings use their direct JSON forms.  Lists and records recurse.
Exact rationals use a pair of a numerator and a denominator.  This
representation is distinct from the user-facing JSON codec, and no two
semantic values collapse in it.

Both traces are compared in order and without normalization.  The identity of
a semantic event is the code together with the complete question.  Routes,
retries, timeouts, and selected backends are execution metadata, and they never
rewrite the authored question in @code{semanticTrace}.  Version 2 deduplicates
bare questions in its memo bill, and the version-2 corpus entries pin that
billing.  Versions 3 and 4 share consult and observe answers by bare question
between two effects and retain every effect occurrence.  The pure oracle itself executes no
command.

@section The string layer

@cindex string layer
A string request has the form
@code{@{"id": @dots{}, "string": @{"op": @dots{}, "text": "@dots{}", @dots{}@}@}} and is
answered with @code{@{"result": @dots{}@}}.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .30 .52
@headitem Operation @tab Extra request fields @tab Result
@item @code{norm} @tab none @tab the normalized string
@item @code{words} @tab none @tab the list of words
@item @code{decodeVerdict} @tab none @tab a verdict object
@item @code{decode} @tab @code{code} @tab @code{@{"answer": @dots{} | null@}}
@item @code{say} @tab @code{code} @tab the rendered answer specification
@item @code{bare} @tab none @tab the string with markup removed
@item @code{fields} @tab none @tab the list of fields
@item @code{headerPaths} @tab none @tab the paths read from diff headers
@item @code{matchGlob} @tab @code{pattern} @tab a Boolean
@item @code{decide} @tab @code{decider}, @code{needles} @tab a Boolean
@item @code{fence} @tab @code{name} @tab the fenced text block
@end multitable

An oracle that meets an unknown operation answers
@code{@{"error": "unknown string op @dots{}"@}}, so an extension of the table
fails loudly.  The @code{decider} field takes @code{lastNonEmptyLineIs},
@code{containsLine}, @code{anyLineStartsWith}, or @code{anyPathMatches}, which
is the same spelling that the first-order @code{decide} node carries.

This request kind exists because the string layer is the highest-ranked
divergence risk, and a program observation never reaches it.  On a boundary
that takes a program in and gives a world out, nothing calls the decoders.  The
four low-level operations @code{bare}, @code{fields}, @code{headerPaths}, and
@code{matchGlob} make a divergence localizable.  A @code{decide} mismatch while
all four agree is a composition defect.  A mismatch in one of them is a defect
in that function.  The known hazard that the vectors freeze is case folding.
The Lean normalizer lowercases ASCII only, whereas a naive Haskell
@code{toLower} is Unicode.  Every predicate in this layer follows the ASCII
rule.  The vectors pin the places where the two implementations can otherwise
drift.  These places include a carriage-return marker line, whitespace-only
input, and a non-ASCII capital through a needle.  They also include glob
patterns against paths with and without directories, an indented diff header,
and a fenced body that closes its own tag or the tag of a sibling.

@section The corpus

@cindex frozen corpus
@file{bisim/corpus/} holds one JSON file per case, of the form
@code{@{"name": "@dots{}", "request": <request>, "reply": <reply>, "oracleVersion": <Nat>@}}.
The last field records the oracle format under which the entry was frozen.
Each file was produced by one run of the oracle, and its output was committed.
The checkout holds 193 files, which are 95 checked replies, 54 refusals, and
44 string results.  The three typed-result entries are version 4, and every
other entry is version 2.  The flagship is the entry
@file{example-000-the-flagship-single-file}.  Its request is the same
@code{RawProgram} that @file{model/Agentic/Core/DslFlagship.lean} proves
accepted.

The corpus is frozen, and the requests in it are the specification.
@command{tier0} is the Haskell side, and it reproduces every reply from every
request with no Lean in the loop.  @command{lake exe corpus-gen} reads each
file and takes its request verbatim.  It puts the request back through the same
observation that the oracle serves, and it rewrites the file with the fresh
reply.  This regeneration must change nothing.  An empty
@code{git status --short bisim/corpus} afterwards states that the
elaboration, the cost algebra, the interpreter, and the trusted base still
agree with the frozen specification.  A diff is a change to the specification,
and it is reviewed as one.

@section What the corpus pins

The corpus pins three surfaces, and each is described below.

The first surface is the reply record.  A version-2 checked reply has exactly
eight keys, and version 4 adds @code{result} and a returned value in each world.
The keys @code{shapes} and @code{asks} appear in no file, so
@code{Cost.shapes} and @code{Cost.asks} can be reorganized without a corpus
regeneration.  Of that family, only @code{codes} is pinned.

The second surface is the printed @code{RawProgram}.  The request of each
vector is the Lean datatype under the JSON encoding of Lean, so the constructor
and field bytes are frozen beside the observations.  Position is the one field
that this surface does not compare.  The corpus stores real line and column
values, and the Haskell comparison zeroes the positions on both sides, because
the Haskell builder has no source position to record.

The third surface is exercised by the Haskell gates rather than by the corpus
alone.  @command{tier1} rebuilds the curated checked entries in the production
authoring surface, and it compares the printed program and the whole reply.
Two further checks are pinned outside the wire.  They are the two
@code{#guard_msgs} in @file{model/Agentic/Core/Certify.lean}, and one of them
states that @code{certify_sound} depends on no axioms.  They are build failures
rather than comments, so the discipline enforces itself.

@section What is never on the wire

Five things never cross the boundary.  These are a Plan, a dialogue, a world as
a function, a table that holds anything other than data, and source text of any
kind.  @code{RawProgram} is the complete first-order object.  There is no
parser on the Lean side, so the boundary is the only way that a program reaches
the oracle.  A transport failure between the two processes is not a
conformance divergence.

@node First Workflow
@chapter First Workflow

This chapter builds the smallest complete program, inspects it, and runs it
against a deterministic table.  The example is the same compiled value that the
project runner and its conformance tests use.

@section Install the runner

@cindex installation
@cindex getting started
The user build of @command{agentic-run} is the Nix package of the root flake.
From the root of a checkout, these commands install it into the Nix profile of
the account and check the installed version:

@example
nix profile add .#agentic-run
agentic-run --version
@end example

Nix places the runner in @file{~/.nix-profile/bin}, which the Nix installer
puts on @code{PATH}.  Nix releases that have no @code{nix profile add} command
give the same command the name @code{nix profile install}.  The command
@code{nix build .#agentic-run} builds the runner without an installation, and
the directory @file{result/bin} of the checkout then holds it.  The command
@code{nix run .#agentic-run -- list} runs one command with no installation.
The guide @file{doc/getting-started.md} follows these steps in full.  It also
starts the workflow manager, connects the TUI, Pi and Emacs, adds real
engines, and lists the daily commands of the operator.

The first local runs need no manager and reach no model.  These commands
work in any directory.  Each exits with status 0, and the last one opens the
terminal interface, which @kbd{q} in its browser closes with status 0:

@example
agentic-run list
agentic-run help hello
agentic-run plan hello
agentic-run run hello --scripted
agentic-run run harden --scripted
agentic-run run hello --engine acp --adapter stub
agentic-run --tui
@end example

The workflow manager is the same executable.  The first command below
creates a manager root.  The second command serves it in the foreground of a
second terminal, and the third command connects the TUI to it from the first
terminal:

@example
agentic-run --manager init --root "$HOME/agent-cat-manager"
agentic-run --manager serve --config "$HOME/agent-cat-manager/serve.json"
agentic-run --tui --service "$HOME/agent-cat-manager/client/profile.json"
@end example

@section Enter the Haskell development shell

An author who compiles a new workflow module uses the maintainer route, which
builds the repository with Cabal in the root Nix development shell.  The
checkout tracks no @file{.envrc} file.  Write an @file{.envrc} in the
repository root that holds the line @code{use flake}, and run
@code{direnv allow .} once.  Then run these commands from the repository
root:

@example
direnv exec . test/cabal.sh build all
"$(direnv exec . test/cabal.sh list-bin agentic-run)" list
@end example

The build covers the library, the compiled examples, and the conformance and
runner executables.  Nothing is installed globally.  A bare
@command{agentic-run} in later examples means the installed runner or the
executable that @code{test/cabal.sh list-bin agentic-run} names.

@section A complete authoring module

@cindex QualifiedDo
The introductory program needs @code{BlockArguments}, @code{QualifiedDo}, and
@code{QuasiQuotes}.  @code{OverloadedStrings} keeps prompt and identifier
literals concise.  The complete module below is compiled during the
verification of the manual, and it can be copied as one file:

@verbatiminclude examples/Hello.hs

@code{W.do} changes only the workflow block in which it appears, while ordinary
@code{do} keeps its usual meaning.  A module that writes a Haskell @code{if}
over a workflow flag also enables @code{RebindableSyntax}.  Under that
extension, import @code{Prelude} explicitly, and import
@code{Data.String (fromString)} for @code{OverloadedStrings}.

@code{workflow} closes a @code{W.do} block with no function table.  A bare
@code{ask} in binding position is a text question, so @code{subject} and
@code{greeting} are typed handles to text answers.  Their prompt holes refer to
ordinary Haskell variables in lexical scope.  The local @code{brief} binding is
text that the author supplies.  It is inserted as literal prompt data, and it
creates no answer binding and no Plan node.

The final @code{ask_} discards a receipt and ends the block.  It is exactly the
same represented term as @code{act (tool "say") ...} followed by @code{stop}.
The underscore follows the ordinary Haskell convention for an operation whose
result is ignored.

@anchor{Registration}
@section Registration

@cindex registry
A program becomes available to @command{agentic-run} through a @code{Registry}.
Each row carries an @code{Example}, a one-line description, a help page, a
scripted answer table, and the tools that the row answers in process.  @code{Fixed helloProgram} registers a complete Program.
@code{Needs parameterizedProgram} registers a program whose operator inputs are
supplied before the Program is built.

A complete one-row executable is included here.  It uses the same compiled
Program and gives the row its description, help page, and deterministic replies:

@verbatiminclude examples/Registry.hs

The documentation check compiles both modules with the local @code{agentic}
package.  Running the resulting executable with @code{run hello --scripted}
requires no credential or external process.

The library performs no global discovery.  A downstream executable constructs
its own registry and applies @code{Agentic.Cli.cliMain}.  The
@command{agentic-run} of this repository applies that function to
@code{examplesRegistry}.  The private workflow toolbox that the source mentions
uses the same CLI with another table.

Every registered row has a help page by type construction.  The deterministic
examples gate of the repository also pins the level, the size, the ask-node
count, the cost summary, and the scripted bills of each row.  A new row
therefore requires an explicit account of its static and observed cost.

@section Inspect before running

The registered name is @code{hello}.  Its Plan report is obtained without
asking or executing anything:

@example
$ agentic-run plan hello
hello, as elaborated:

  level     pipeline
  size      4
  askNodes  3
  codes     text, text, receipt
  cost      minFold 3, maxFold 3, over 1 path
  (--raw prints the program itself)
@end example

The two later prompts read earlier answers, which places the program at the
pipeline rung.  There is one path and three request occurrences, so the cost
range has one value.  @code{--raw} additionally prints the first-order program
that the Haskell builder emitted.

@section Run without an external addressee

The scripted runner uses the canned table registered beside @code{helloProgram}:

@example
agentic-run run hello --scripted
@end example

The table answers the three prompts with fixed bytes.  Those bytes pass through
the normal typed decoder.  The scheduler follows the same Plan that live runs
use, and the final report gives both the fresh and the memo bills.  No adapter,
agent-deck session, person, or executable tool is contacted.  This is the
preferred first run, and it is the repeatable form that the guide uses
throughout.

@node Authoring Workflows
@chapter Authoring Workflows

This chapter develops the authoring surface from prompts and questions through
statements, finite branches, panels, decisions, and bounded revision.

@section Blocks and terminals

@cindex workflow block
A @code{W.do} block is indexed by its incoming and outgoing stage.  The stage
tracks whether the block is an ordinary workflow, a review clause, an amendment
clause, or a function body.  Ordinary source code does not mention those
indices.  They appear in compiler messages when a construction is placed where
the grammar cannot represent it.

A workflow block ends in a terminal.  @code{stop} is the empty ending.
@code{ask_} performs one answer-discarding request and stops.  A two- or
three-way branch is terminal because each arm contains the remainder of the
workflow.  The type family @code{NoFollow} rejects a statement written after
such an ending with @samp{nothing follows a terminal}.

@code{act} is a statement rather than a complete block.  It can be followed by
another statement in a workflow or function body.  @code{knownHere} asserts the
currently live printed names and adds no Plan node.  It is useful when a
first-order program needs to carry and check an explicit account of its context.

@section Bindings and printed names

A bound answer is a typed handle that is handed to the remainder of the Haskell
block.  The surface generates the printed binder name from the depth of the
binding, and the first name is @code{b0}.  A revision result uses the
corresponding @code{r0} form.  Generated names make printing reproducible, and
they require no type-level copy of the spelling of the Haskell binder.

@code{named "guide" statement} selects the printed name for a binding.  It
changes neither the typed variable nor the Plan meaning, and prompt holes continue
to read the selected name from the handle.  Names of the generated @code{bN} and
@code{rN} shapes are reserved.  A statement that binds nothing ignores
@code{named}.

@section Prompt fences and holes

@cindex prompt fence
@code{[wf|...|]} produces prompt pieces in the current binding scope.  Blank
lines at the two edges are dropped, common indentation is removed, and the
result has no trailing newline.  A hole such as @code{@{subject@}} is resolved
as an ordinary Haskell expression at the splice site.

The class @code{Says} admits three useful sources.  A live handle contributes
an interpolation piece under the name that the handle carries.  A @code{Text}
value contributes literal words.  A fenced define contributes its existing
prompt pieces.  A handle must have a supported textual rendering, so flags and
receipts cannot be placed in prompt holes.

A verdict hole renders its objection text.  Approval and refusal both render as
an empty string under the verdict algebra.  When the difference matters, branch
on the verdict tag, because the rendered text cannot distinguish them.

@code{[wft|...|]} applies the same fence layout to a define, and it returns
text rather than scoped prompt pieces.  Its holes can name defines in Haskell
scope.  They cannot name live workflow handles, because the resulting text
carries no workflow scope in which such a handle is live.

Literal pieces on either side of a hole remain separate in the first-order
prompt.  This detail makes the authoring surface print the same left-associated
expression that the Lean checker and the frozen corpus expect.

@section Parties and question modifiers

A question names one of three parties through @code{model}, @code{tool}, or
@code{person}.  The identifier belongs to the authored question and appears in
run traces.  A model party can be modified by
@code{servedBy} and repeated @code{fallingBackTo} applications:

@example
model "reviewer" `servedBy` "deep" `fallingBackTo` "broad"
@end example

The primary serving model enters the scope of the represented question.
Alternates form an ordered execution chain.  Tools and people cannot carry a
model-serving chain, and the party kind makes such a construction a
compile-time error.

@code{drawing party n} sets the draw field.  Equal prompts at distinct draws
are distinct questions, and they occupy distinct memo keys.  @code{running}
attaches an argv to a tool party.  Its command and arguments are literal
program text, and the prompt travels separately on standard input.

@section Answer kinds

A bare @code{ask party prompt} receives its kind from the position in which it
stands.  Binding it in an ordinary block or function body selects text.
A panel or review position selects verdict.  An act selects receipt.  Other
kinds are stated with dedicated or explicit forms.

@table @code
@item confirm party prompt
Produce a flag source suitable for a Haskell @code{if}.
@item ask ... `answering` Verdict
Select an answer kind without printing an annotation in the first-order term.
@item ask ... `annotated` Verdict
Select the same kind and print the annotation.
@item structured @@HostType
Select a schema-indexed answer whose schema comes from @code{HasSchema}.
@end table

The @code{Answer} witnesses are @code{Text}, @code{Flag}, @code{Verdict},
@code{Receipt}, and @code{Structured schema}.  @code{answering} and
@code{annotated} accept any of them.  Structured answers are developed in
@ref{Functions Inputs and Schemas}.

@section Panels

@cindex panel
@code{panel} accepts a nonempty list of questions and yields a verdict.  The
members become independent Plan subterms, so the scheduler can overlap them
when their prompt dependencies permit it.  Their verdicts are multiplied in
list order, and all members must approve for the result to approve.

@code{panelText} accepts nonempty pairs of a label and a question.  It yields
text that is formed from one named block per member.  Labels are authored
document roles rather than addressee identifiers, and they must be valid and
distinct.  The body of a block cannot close its own fence, because that closing
tag is escaped.

An empty list is refused when the Program is forced.  The authoring surface
uses ordinary lists so that a panel reads naturally.  The value-level refusal
preserves the same language contract as the first-order checker.

@section Pure decisions

@cindex decider
@code{decide} classifies text already held in a handle and yields a flag without
asking anyone.  The vocabulary is closed:

@table @code
@item LastNonEmptyLineIs
The last nonblank line equals one of the needles.
@item ContainsLine
Some line equals one of the needles.
@item AnyLineStartsWith
Some line begins with one of the needles.
@item AnyPathMatches
A path read from a diff header matches one of the glob patterns.
@end table

The line deciders compare under the ASCII case-folding rules of the project.
Needles remain literal program text, and each needle must be nonempty.  The
list must contain at least one needle.  When a decision replaces an asked flag,
it removes one question from every path, and the path count and the analysis
rung stay the same.

@section Boolean and verdict branches

@cindex terminal branch
An authoring module with @code{RebindableSyntax} writes an ordinary Haskell
@code{if} over a flag handle.  The library's @code{ifThenElse} is the desugaring
target.  Both arms are @code{W.do} blocks and each contains the rest of the
workflow.

@code{when flag body} and @code{unless flag body} provide one-armed forms.  They
supply @code{stop} for the omitted arm and seal the body with its own terminal.
These combinators are terminal, unlike the continuing operations of the same
names in @code{Control.Monad}.

A verdict handle remains an ordinary value, and a workflow can splice it or act
past it.  @code{caseVerdict verdict approved objected declined} supplies its
three terminal arms in the Lean tag order.  There is no Haskell data
constructor to match here.  The branch is placed at the explicit
@code{caseVerdict} call, which can come many statements after the verdict was
bound.

@section Bounded revision

@cindex bounded revision
@code{revising subject (atMost n)} reviews a candidate and can amend it up to
@code{n} times.  The bound lies between zero and sixty-four.  Each round has
exactly two source clauses.  The first is a review that produces a verdict, and
the second is an @code{amend} at the answer kind of the candidate.  Additional
statements have no first-order place inside the round, and they are rejected.

The flagship's complete review clause is a compiled example:

@c SOURCE workflow/example/Harden.hs
@verbatim
    result <- revising draft (atMost 2) \patch -> W.do
        verdict <- panel
          [ ask (model "reviewer-correct") [wf|
              {guide}
              Is this patch correct?
              {patch}
              {verdictSpec}|],
            ask (model "reviewer-secure") [wf|
              {guide}
              Is this patch secure?
              {patch}
              {verdictSpec}|],
            ask (model "reviewer-simple") [wf|
              Could this patch be simpler?
              {patch}
              {verdictSpec}|]
          ]
        amend (ask (model "author" `servedBy` "deep") [wf|
            {guide}
            Revise this patch:
            {patch}
            {verdict}
            Reply with the revised diff only.|])
@end verbatim
@c END SOURCE

The bind of a revision is also the branch point.  Its result is consumed by the
immediately following Haskell case.  Both outcomes carry the candidate then in
hand:

@c SOURCE workflow/example/Harden.hs
@verbatim
    case result of
      Settled patch -> W.do
        ok <- confirm (person "owner") [wf|
            Apply this patch?
            {patch}
            {flagSpec}|]

        when ok $ W.do
          act (tool "apply") [wf|
              Apply:
              {patch}
              Write the patched file here, then reply DONE.|]
      Unsettled _ -> stop
@end verbatim
@c END SOURCE

@code{Settled} carries an approved candidate.  @code{Unsettled} carries the last
candidate reviewed when the bound ran out.  Each pattern binds its own typed
handle in its own arm, and Haskell lexical scope prevents one arm from using the
other arm's value.

@code{revisingOn} reads the three tags of the review verdict separately.
Approval produces @code{SettledOn}.  An outstanding objection at the bound
produces @code{UnsettledOn}.  Refusal produces @code{AbandonedOn}.  Every
constructor carries the current candidate.  The continuation is represented in
all three arms, so the tail grows @code{2n+1} times rather than the @code{n+1}
copies that @code{revising} uses.  A long shared tail belongs in a function
that each arm calls.

@node Functions Inputs and Schemas
@chapter Functions, Inputs, and Schemas

This chapter explains the constructions that organize larger programs.  These
are straight-line functions, typed arguments, operator inputs, runner-supplied
facts, and schema-indexed structured answers.

@section Functions

@cindex workflow function
A workflow function is a named open Plan over a typed parameter context.  Its
body is a straight-line @code{W.do} block.  A call substitutes the arguments
into the body and grafts the resulting nodes at the call site.  A call
therefore adds no runtime dispatch node, and it costs exactly what the inlined
body costs.

A parameter list ends at @code{noParams}.  Each @code{takes @@"name" Answer}
adds a printed name and answer kind.  The name is a type-level symbol because
the function signature and every prompt hole in the body must agree on its
spelling.  Duplicate parameter names and reserved generated names are compile-time
errors.

@code{function} takes the function name, the parameter list, and the curried
body.  The terminal determines the result kind.  @code{answer handle} returns a
value of the kind of the handle.  @code{done} returns a receipt to the caller.
A function body permits binds, questions, acts, calls, and one final answer.
Branches, bounded revision, and @code{knownHere} belong to the workflow that
calls the function.

The @code{reviewReport} function from the compiled Isaac examples has six text
parameters and a receipt result:

@c SOURCE workflow/extra/Isaac.hs
@verbatim
reviewReport ::
  Fn '[ 'CodeText, 'CodeText, 'CodeText, 'CodeText, 'CodeText, 'CodeText] 'CodeAck
reviewReport =
  function
    "review-lite.report"
    ( takes @"correctness" Text
        . takes @"haskell" Text
        . takes @"claims" Text
        . takes @"failures" Text
        . takes @"braids" Text
        . takes @"cuts" Text
        $ noParams
    )
    \correctness haskell claims failures braids cuts -> W.do
      act (tool "write-report") [wf|
          {reviewReportBrief}
          {correctness}
          {haskell}
          {claims}
          {failures}
          {braids}
          {cuts}|]
      done
@end verbatim
@c END SOURCE

A Program with functions is built by
@code{defining [SomeFn first, SomeFn second] block}.  Names must be unique, and
every callee must precede its caller in the table.  This declaration order
prevents recursion and makes function ask counts available before a call is
priced.

@section Calls and argument chains

@code{call fn args} is a value source, and it can be bound wherever an
@code{Rhs} can stand.  @code{call_ fn args} is a statement call that is
restricted to a function that returns a receipt.  The underscore again records
that no value is bound.

Arguments form a right-associated chain with @code{:>} and end at
@code{noArgs}.  @code{arg handle} passes a live answer by reference at its exact
kind.  @code{arg textDefine} passes literal text.  A fenced define can also
supply text whose holes read bindings in the caller's scope.  Kind and arity
mismatches are ordinary type errors.

The same @code{:>} spelling builds program input lists.  The overloaded pattern
has only these two construction instances.  A pattern match on a chain in user
code is outside the supported surface.

@section Program inputs

@cindex program input
An input is text that is supplied before the Program is built.  It behaves
like a Haskell define that is placed in prompts, and not like an answer that a
question obtains.  Plan folds do not inspect prompt text.  A program that only
splices an input therefore has the same level, node counts, answer-code
structure, and cost for every input value.

@code{input "subject" :> noInputs} declares one ordinary input.
@code{argsInput} declares command-tail text under the default name @code{args}.
@code{stdinInput} declares literal standard input under the default name
@code{input}.  @code{argsInputAs} and @code{stdinInputAs} select custom names.
Names are unique, and a program can declare at most one command-tail source and
one standard-input source.  @code{taking} curries the body over the ordered
declarations and produces @code{Parameterized}.  The compiled
@code{review-lite} example uses a custom stdin name:

@c SOURCE workflow/extra/Isaac.hs
@verbatim
reviewLite :: Parameterized
reviewLite = taking (stdinInputAs "subject" :> noInputs) \subject ->
  defining [SomeFn reviewReport] W.do
@end verbatim
@c END SOURCE

A registry stores a parameterized value with @code{Needs}.  Descriptor version
2 exposes each operator input as a record with a @code{name} and a
@code{source}.  New clients interpret version-1 string entries as ordinary
prompt inputs.  @code{plan} and @code{cost} can leave inputs empty, because the
folds read no prompt.  @code{run} requires every input.

The explicit forms remain @code{--input FILE}, @code{--input-file NAME=FILE},
and @code{--input-arg NAME=VALUE}.  For @code{run} and for the machine and
lineage commands, an unbound stdin declaration reads file descriptor 0 to end
of file, strictly as UTF-8.  Empty input is valid.  A terminal is refused
rather than read, so the command does not hang.  Explicit input flags take
precedence.  Stdin and command-tail inputs preserve the exact text, while an
ordinary file input has one final line feed removed.

@section Optional runtime facts

@cindex run fact
Four reserved inputs are supplied by the runner rather than the operator:

@table @code
@item run.backends
A one-line roster describing how many answering services the run reaches and
which kinds they are.
@item run.engine
The engine and the session-sharing policy, in the wording that
@code{sessionPolicy} returns.
@item run.routes
The resolved route table, one @samp{label = backend} line per answerer, with
@code{(default)} first.
@item run.sentinel
A line generated for this run and inserted nowhere except prompts that hole the
fact.
@end table

A workflow declares one of these names with the ordinary @code{input} of the
DSL.  The name is a symbol, so the workflow package remains DSL-only.  The CLI
treats @code{run.} as a closed namespace, and it supplies the fact from the run
that it is making.  A name under that prefix outside the closed set is refused.
An operator flag that attempts to supply a valid run fact is also refused,
because a command line cannot state what its own run did.  Hosts share the
constants and the readers through the Text-only @code{Agentic.Runtime.Facts}
API.

@code{sharesOneSession} reads @code{run.engine} and reports whether at least one
answerer shares a conversation across the run.  @code{routedBackend} reads
@code{run.routes} and returns the named backend, then the default backend when
one is present, then empty text.  @code{plan} and @code{cost} leave every run
fact empty.  Scripted execution binds the roster, engine, and sentinel while
leaving @code{run.routes} empty because it has no backend table.

@section Structured answers

@cindex schema-indexed answer
A structured answer carries its schema in the answer code.  Primitive schemas
cover unit, Boolean, integer, exact rational, and text values.  Arrays and named
product fields compose them.  @code{SchemaEl} interprets that structure as
ordinary Haskell values.

@code{HasSchema} relates a host type to its schema and supplies total conversion
in both directions.  Primitive types and lists have built-in instances.
@code{deriveSchema} derives a record's schema, witness, and conversion from one
record declaration.  The compiled structured example uses:

@c SOURCE workflow/example/Structured.hs
@verbatim
data ReleasePlan = ReleasePlan
  { title :: Text,
    priority :: Integer,
    steps :: [Text]
  }
  deriving (Eq, Show)

$(deriveSchema ''ReleasePlan)
@end verbatim
@c END SOURCE

A model question selects this answer through @code{structured @@ReleasePlan}.
Using @code{annotated} prints the structured code in the first-order source:

@c SOURCE workflow/example/Structured.hs
@verbatim
structuredProgram :: Program
structuredProgram = workflow W.do
  _plan <-
    ask (model "structured-producer") [wf|{producerBrief}|]
      `annotated` (structured @ReleasePlan)
  stop
@end verbatim
@c END SOURCE

The runtime appends a JSON Schema instruction to the rendered question.  It
rejects duplicate object keys and schema mismatches, and it decodes a successful
response to the semantic @code{SchemaEl} value.  Where host-side code needs
that boundary, @code{decodeAs @@ReleasePlan} converts the same bytes directly
to the host record.

@code{Agentic.Schema.Json} also provides explicit functions that decode,
encode, render, and produce the schema document.  User-facing JSON rejects a
numeric exponent whose magnitude exceeds 4096.  The encoding of an arbitrary
exact rational succeeds only when the rational has a finite decimal
representation.  The conformance representation uses an exact numerator and
denominator, so distinct semantic values do not collapse.

@node Inspecting Workflows
@chapter Inspecting Workflows

This chapter describes the commands that inspect a registered Program without
starting an adapter or asking an addressee.

@section List and help

@code{agentic-run list} prints the registry in declaration order.  The current
repository has ten rows, named @code{harden}, @code{hello}, @code{structured},
@code{structured-result}, @code{capital}, @code{plan-feature},
@code{review-lite}, @code{ship-feature-lite}, @code{grind-tests}, and
@code{stack-prs}.  Each line
includes the short description of the row.

@code{agentic-run help NAME} and @code{agentic-run NAME --help} print the same
program page.  The page combines the current static facts with the prose that
is stored in the row.  That prose gives the purpose, the meaning of each input,
the suitable transport, a worked command, a scripted rehearsal, and caveats.
The command starts no answering service and exits successfully.  A bare
registered name is refused, because it is ambiguous between inspection and
execution.

@code{--help} or bare @code{help} prints general usage.  A bare executable with
no arguments prints the same usage as an error, since no command was requested.

@section Plan

@cindex plan command
@code{plan NAME} reports five structural observations.  These are the level,
the size, the ask nodes, the answer codes where a single sequence exists, and
the cost summary.  The result comes from the Haskell Plan, and it is available
before any world supplies an answer.

@code{--raw} adds the emitted @code{RawProgram}.  The printed tree is the same
first-order value used by guards, route and fail-over analysis, curated
conformance cases, and the Lean oracle boundary.  Input values can change its
prompt literals without changing the structural folds.

@code{--json} changes @code{plan} to a machine-readable object.  The stable
keys include @code{name}, @code{blurb}, @code{level}, @code{size},
@code{askNodes}, @code{minFold}, @code{maxFold}, @code{paths}, the operator
@code{inputs}, the declared @code{runFacts}, the routing @code{pins},
@code{codes}, and the cost @code{fold}.  @code{--raw --json} also includes
@code{program}.  The order of the object keys is not part of the interface, and
the order of the inputs is.

@section Cost

@cindex cost command
@code{cost NAME} prints the cost summary in prose and then the folded value for
each syntactic path.  For @code{hello}, the report is:

@example
$ agentic-run cost hello
hello, priced:

  costSummary   minFold 3, maxFold 3, over 1 path

  every path consults 3 times, so this program has one price and not a range.

  the fold, path by path (1 in all):
    3
@end example

@code{cost} has no @code{--json} option because @code{plan --json} already
contains the machine-readable summary and histogram.  The command accepts
program inputs so that its input account matches the intended run, even though
the static numbers do not inspect those values.

@section Inputs during inspection

For a Program with operator inputs, @code{plan} and @code{cost} accept
@code{--input}, @code{--input-file}, and @code{--input-arg}.  A missing input
is bound to empty text for inspection and is named as unbound in the output.
Run facts also remain empty because no run exists yet.

When @code{plan --raw} prints a Program that was built with missing inputs, a
note appears above the tree.  The note matters because the printed prompt text
differs from the Program that real values will build, even though the folds
are the same.

@section The pinning guard

@code{plan NAME --require-pinned} checks the emitted first-order Program before
printing anything.  Every question addressed to a model must name its serving
model through @code{servedBy}.  Tool and person questions are outside this
guard.  The same option is available on @code{run} and is checked before an
adapter starts or any request can be spent.

A fallback chain counts as pinned, because it has a primary serving model.  The
guard protects future model questions when it refuses the complete Program.  It
does not infer or add a serving model.

@node Running Workflows
@chapter Running Workflows

This chapter gives the operator-facing execution procedures.  Begin with the
scripted table, use fixture gates for transport tests, and select a live engine
only when the destination and its consequences are understood.

@anchor{Scripted execution}
@section Scripted execution

@cindex scripted execution
@code{run NAME --scripted} executes the registered Plan against the canned
reply bytes that are stored in its row.  The match uses a prefix of the
authored prompt rather than a substring.  An earlier answer that is spliced
into a later prompt therefore cannot select the wrong reply by accident.  An
unmatched prompt receives a deterministic default for its code.

Canned bytes use the normal decoder and recovery settings.  A canned
@samp{maybe} at a flag is unreadable.  The runtime re-asks it according to
policy, and the run can abandon, exactly as with the same bytes from a live
service.  Scripted mode therefore exercises Plan scheduling, branching,
memoization, decoding, and billing, and it leaves transports and external tools
untouched.

A standard rehearsal, in any directory, is:

@example
agentic-run run hello --scripted
@end example

The command uses the installed runner, as @ref{First Workflow} states.  The
maintainer route runs the runner that @code{test/cabal.sh list-bin agentic-run}
names in the same way.

The closing report gives the workflow result, @code{billFresh}, and
@code{billMemo}.  A workflow Program normally returns unit.  Its useful record
is the execution trace, which is printed as questions and answers occur.

@section ACP adapters

@cindex ACP engine
An ACP run starts an adapter process and owns its line-delimited JSON-RPC pipe:

@example
agentic-run run NAME --engine acp [--adapter ADAPTER] [--model ID]
    [--effort LEVEL] [--scratch DIR]
@end example

@code{--adapter} accepts @code{stub}, @code{claude}, @code{codex},
@code{droid}, or an executable path.  The default is @code{stub}, the
deterministic local adapter, so an omitted option never starts a paid live
model.  The stub is the command @command{python3
engine/acp/test/stub_adapter.py}, with @command{python3} from @code{PATH}.  The
script is a data file of the package.  The runner uses the copy in the working
directory when that directory is the root of an agent-cat checkout, and
otherwise the copy in its installed data directory.  A stub run therefore
works from any directory.  The builds of @file{test/cabal.sh} set the data
directory to the checkout, and the Nix package installs the data files below
its own @file{share/agentic} directory.

@command{claude-agent-acp} and @command{codex-acp} must be on @code{PATH}.  The
runner looks for each of them on @code{PATH} first.  Its fallback for each is
one fixed Nix store path of one adapter build, and that path exists only on a
machine whose store holds that exact build.  When the program is not on
@code{PATH} and the fallback path is absent, the run stops with a transport
failure that names the program.  Droid expands to
@command{droid exec --output-format acp} on @code{PATH}.  It uses the native
ACP mode of Droid, and it does not require the Python SDK.

@example
agentic-run run structured --engine acp --adapter droid
@end example

Authenticate Droid locally, or provide @code{FACTORY_API_KEY} in the
environment that starts the runner.  Never put credentials in
@code{--adapter-arg} or in the routing configuration.

Without @code{--model} and @code{--effort}, an adapter uses its own session
defaults.  @code{--model ID} and @code{--effort LEVEL} select a model value and
an effort value that the default answerer offers.  To see the offered values,
run the inspection command:

@example
agentic-run adapter-options --adapter claude
@end example

It starts the adapter, completes @code{initialize} and @code{session/new}, prints
the values of the model option and the effort option, and exits.  It sends no
prompt.  @code{--json} prints one object with the fields @code{adapter},
@code{command}, @code{model}, and @code{effort}.  Each of the last two is
@code{null} or an object with @code{option} and @code{values}.

The run then applies each chosen value through @code{session/set_config_option}
on every session that the default answerer opens, and it does this before the
prompt.  Before the first question, a capability preflight opens one session and
applies the values.  A value that the adapter does not offer stops the run there,
before any prompt, and the message lists the offered values.  The exit status of
that refusal is 2, the transport-failure status, because the refusal comes from
the adapter connection.  An adapter that does not advertise a model or effort
option is refused in the same way.  @code{--verbose} narrates each option that
the runner sets.  A routing profile whose backend is an @code{acp:} adapter
applies the model, thinking, and output settings of its rung through the same
call and the same preflight.

One adapter process can retain subscriptions for sessions that were opened
earlier in the run.  The runner accepts answer chunks only for the current
@code{sessionId}.  A permission request must also match the session and the
dynamic extent of an active prompt.  Stale or delayed requests that arrive out
of turn are cancelled.

@code{--adapter-arg ARG} appends one argument to every ACP adapter argv, and it
can repeat.  @code{--timeout MS} bounds one request, and the default is 900000
milliseconds.  @code{--verbose} narrates protocol activity on standard error.

@code{--scratch DIR} sets the working directory of the adapter and the
directory in which an effect can receive permission.  Without it, the runner
creates and prints a fresh temporary directory.  A live command that is
intended to alter the current tree therefore names @code{--scratch "$PWD"}
explicitly.  This is an authorization decision, and it is not a convenience
flag.

By default, ACP opens a new session for every question.  This policy limits the
history that is carried from one semantic question to the next.  It is an
approximation of the world model rather than a theorem about the adapter.

@section agent-deck sessions

@cindex agent-deck engine
A deck run joins a session that another process already started:

@example
agentic-run run NAME --session ID [--binary PATH] [--poll MS]
@end example

@code{--session} by itself selects the deck engine.  The optional explicit form
@code{--engine deck --session ID} means the same thing.  An explicit session is
the complete command-line route table, even when the redundant @code{--routing}
flag is present.  @code{--binary} names the @command{agent-deck} executable,
whose default is @code{agent-deck} on @code{PATH}.  @code{--poll} defaults to
1000 milliseconds.  The common @code{--timeout} defaults to 600000 milliseconds
for a deck turn.

Before it sends a question, the runner records the timestamp of the output
that is already present.  It sends the rendered question, polls the session
until the session is idle, and accepts a reply only after the timestamp
changes.  This staleness guard prevents the output of the previous turn from
becoming the current answer.

When no @code{--route} option is present, every model, tool, and person question
goes to the selected session, except the tools that the row answers in process.
Text questions send the authored prompt unchanged.
For verdicts, flags, receipts, and structured answers, the runtime appends the
required reply-format instruction unless the authored prompt already ends with
it.  Structured instructions include the JSON Schema used by the decoder.
The addressee, intent, scope axes, and draw remain in request metadata and the
run trace rather than being injected into the session.  Serving-model pins still
control runtime routing, but sending a symbolic label does not reconfigure the
session.  Deck reports no ACP stop reason, so the completion evidence is limited
to an idle session and newer output.

@section Routes

@cindex route option
An explicit live target has one default backend, and @code{--route} can refine
that backend by serving-model pin:

@example
--route NAME=acp:stub
--route NAME=acp:claude
--route NAME=acp:droid
--route NAME=deck:SESSION
@end example

@code{NAME} is a primary or alternate model that @code{servedBy} or
@code{fallingBackTo} names, and it is not a model-party identifier.  A route
name that is absent from the pin set of the Program is refused before
execution.  One name can appear only once.  Pinned questions without a route,
unpinned model questions, people, and the tools that the row does not answer in
process use the explicit default backend.

Routes can mix ACP and deck backends.  Engine-specific flags are accepted when
at least one backend of the corresponding scheme uses them.  @code{--adapter},
@code{--adapter-arg}, and @code{--scratch} configure ACP backends.
@code{--binary} and @code{--poll} configure deck backends.  @code{--timeout}
and @code{--verbose} apply to both.  A foreign flag with no backend to consume
it is refused rather than ignored.

A routing-only version-2 run uses @code{--realize AXIS=MODEL-ALIAS} as its safe
override.  The alias carries its authorized engine, provider, environment, and
catalogue.  Raw @code{--route} belongs only to an explicit command-line target.

Scripted execution accepts no routes, because its table does not dispatch by
serving-model scope.  A raw route also requires an explicit default live
answerer.  Without an explicit target, routing configuration must cover every
engine-bound question.  Every model ask therefore needs a configured pin, while
a person question, or a tool question that the row does not answer in process,
requires @code{--engine} or @code{--session}.

@section Inputs and preflight checks

A run accepts the three operator-input flags described in @ref{Functions Inputs
and Schemas}.  Every declared operator input must be supplied exactly once, and
a Program that declares none refuses all three forms.  Run facts are bound by
the runner after it resolves the complete backend table and before it builds the
Program.

@code{--require-pinned} can accompany any execution target.  Routing-only
execution imposes full pin and configured-route coverage automatically.  Route
syntax, duplicate routes, unknown pins, function-table validity, reserved input
names, and required operator inputs are checked before a live backend starts.
This ordering keeps usage errors at exit status 1, and it prevents a malformed
request from spending a turn.

@section Exit status and failure classes

@cindex exit status
The executable distinguishes five outcomes:

@table @asis
@item 0
The inspection completed or the run reached its represented end.
@item 1
The command line or the Program failed preflight.  An unpinned Program under
@code{--require-pinned} is one such failure.
@item 2
A transport failed.  An executable was missing, a session stopped, a pipe or a
protocol failed, or a turn exceeded its time budget.
@item 3
The run abandoned.  Either an answer was not decodable after recovery, or an
ACP turn lacked the required completion evidence.
@item 130
A machine run was cancelled by a control command or by the close of its
control input.  Human @code{run} never exits with this status.
@end table

Usage and preflight messages go to standard error.  @code{list --json} and
@code{plan --json} still emit prose refusals on standard error, so a machine
reader checks the exit status before parsing standard output.

@section Deterministic transport tests

The repository supplies local fixture doubles for both live transports.  They
exercise process ownership, polling, the rejection of stale output, timeouts,
decode recovery, effects, memoization, and route combinations.  They need no
credentials and no network access:

@example
./engine/acp/ci/acp.sh
./engine/agent-deck/ci/deck.sh
@end example

The ACP gate drives @file{engine/acp/test/stub_adapter.py} and the misbehaving
adapters in the same directory.  The deck gate drives
@file{engine/agent-deck/test/stub-deck.sh}.  @file{engine/acp/ci/route-live.sh}
is different.  It is a manual smoke test against real backends, it is outside
the deterministic suite, and no automatic gate invokes it.  Run it only with an
explicit choice of authenticated adapters and destinations.

@section Operational safety

Inspection and scripted execution are local and reversible.  A live run can
send repository contents to an external service.  It can consume paid
capacity, join a conversation that another process watches, and authorize
tools in a scratch directory.  Select the engine, the route table, the inputs,
and the scratch directory as one operational decision.

Keep the default temporary scratch directory for a rehearsal.  Point
@code{--scratch} at a working tree only when the effect of the workflow is
intended to modify that tree.  Before you join a deck session, close or
coordinate the other writers.  Read the run header to confirm every resolved
backend before the first question is sent.

A successful exit establishes that the represented execution completed under
the evidence rules of the runner.  When an external result matters, follow the
act with an independent observation or verification step, and record its
answer in the workflow.

@node Building and Verification
@chapter Building and Verification

This chapter gives the procedures that build the repository and the gates that
verify it.  It is written for a maintainer.  A reader who only writes workflows
needs the first section and the scripted rehearsal in @ref{Running Workflows}.

@section Environments

@cindex development shell
A user installs the Nix package with @code{nix profile add .#agentic-run}, as
@ref{First Workflow} states, and needs no development shell.  The rest of this
chapter is the maintainer route.

Two Nix development shells are the supported environments of a maintainer,
and nothing is installed globally.  The root shell supplies one GHC with the
external Haskell dependencies and @command{cabal-install}.  The model shell
supplies Lean and @command{texinfo}.  The checkout tracks no @file{.envrc}
file, so a maintainer attaches each shell with direnv once.  In the repository
root, write an @file{.envrc} that holds the line @code{use flake}.  In
@file{model} and in @file{bisim}, write an @file{.envrc} that holds the line
@code{use flake path:../model}.  Then run @code{direnv allow .} once in each of
the three directories.  @code{direnv exec .} then runs one command in the shell
of its directory.  @file{model/lean-toolchain} pins Lean 4.30.0, and
@file{model/lakefile.toml} pins Mathlib at the matching tag.

@section Building the Haskell workspace

@cindex cabal components
The Haskell side is one Cabal package, @code{agentic}, and @file{agentic.cabal}
declares its components:

@example
direnv exec . test/cabal.sh build all
direnv exec . test/cabal.sh test all
@end example

The first command is the developer build.  @file{test/cabal.sh} builds offline
into the directory that @env{CABAL_BUILDDIR} names, or into
@file{dist-newstyle} when that variable is unset.  The command
@code{direnv exec . test/cabal.sh list-bin agentic-run} prints the path of the
runner that it built.

The public library @code{agentic} gathers the source directories @file{dsl},
@file{plan}, @file{cost}, @file{runtime}, @file{engine/api}, @file{engine/acp}
with its three selector directories, @file{engine/agent-deck}, and @file{cli}.
Three internal libraries build beside it.  @code{examples} holds the compiled
workflows and their registry data.  @code{bisim-support} holds the generators,
the guards, and the oracle client.  The private @code{verification} library
assembles complete conformance observations.  The executables are the runner
@command{agentic-run}, the three conformance programs @command{tier0},
@command{tier1}, and @command{bisim}, and the six probes
@command{policy-probe}, @command{routing-config-probe},
@command{routing-v2-probe}, @command{routing-discovery-probe},
@command{routing-fixed-point-probe}, and @command{schema-probe}, which the gate
scripts drive.  The test suite @code{engine-api-test} exercises the neutral
engine interface against a deterministic fake engine.

@section Building the runner package

@cindex Nix package
@cindex source distribution
The root flake defines the package @code{agentic-run}, which is also its
default package and the user build.  @command{nix build} builds it from the
same pinned package set as the root shell.  The user command
@code{nix build .#agentic-run} can fetch inputs from the binary caches that Nix
is configured to use.  When the store already holds the closure of the root
development shell, for example after direnv has loaded that shell once, a
maintainer can build with substitution disabled:

@example
nix build .#agentic-run --no-update-lock-file --option substitute false
result/bin/agentic-run run hello --scripted
@end example

The derivation runs @command{cabal-install} offline with no active package
repository.  It uses the GHC of the root shell, which carries the dependency
overrides of @file{nix/haskell-overrides.nix}.  These overrides include the
subject-alternative-name patch of @code{crypton-x509-validation} and, on Linux,
the descriptor-closing patch of the @code{process} boot library.  The
derivation builds the component @code{exe:agentic-run} with optimization level
1 and parallel compilation, and it installs @file{bin/agentic-run}.  It also
installs the three data files of @file{agentic.cabal}, the stub adapter and
the manager reference pair, below @file{share/agentic}, and the runner reads
them from there.  With the closure of the root shell in the store, the
build has every input of the derivation, and the option
@code{--option substitute false} prevents any contact with a binary cache.

The source of the derivation is a filtered copy of the repository.  It holds
@file{agentic.cabal}, @file{cabal.project}, the three files under @file{nix},
the three data files, and the Haskell and C sources of the library, the
@code{examples} library, and the runner.  Other documentation, tests,
fixtures, and the Lean model are outside it, so a change to them leaves the
derivation path unchanged.  From a Git working
copy, Nix reads only the files that Git tracks, and a new source file must be
added to the index before the build can read it.  A copy of the repository that
is not a Git working copy builds with the reference @code{path:$PWD#agentic-run}.

The source distribution comes from the shared Cabal entry point:

@example
direnv exec . test/cabal.sh sdist
@end example

The command writes @file{sdist/agentic-0.1.0.0.tar.gz} beneath
@code{CABAL_BUILDDIR}.  Its listing includes
@file{nix/haskell-overrides.nix}, @file{nix/crypton-x509-validation-san.patch},
and @file{nix/process-close-fds-linux.patch}, so the dependency patches travel
with the source.  @file{doc/workflow-manager-release-evidence.md} records the
identities of the package, the executable, and the source distribution.

The @code{package} mode of @file{manager/test/service_http.py} accepts the
built executable.  It reads the path of @file{bin/agentic-run} from the
environment variable @env{PACKAGE_RUNNER} and runs that file as the manager and
as the runner of a scripted profile.  It completes one request of the
@code{hello} workflow through exact approval, downloads the verified result and
compares its digest, reads @code{status} through the live administration
channel, and stops the manager through @code{shutdown} with exit status 0.  The
release evidence also records the identities of that run.

The mode then checks schema upgrade and refusal.  The command
@code{manager-store-check schema-fixtures @var{dir}} writes one manager root for
each older schema version from 1 to 11, and one root whose database has
@code{user_version} 13.  The mode reads @var{dir} from the environment variable
@env{SCHEMA_FIXTURES} and works on a copy of each root.  For each older root,
offline @code{status} migrates the database to version 12, @code{check-store}
reports @code{valid}, and @code{RUNNER --manager serve} answers 200 on
@code{GET /v1/capabilities} and stops through @code{shutdown}.  For the version
13 root, offline @code{status} refuses with @code{storage-unavailable},
@code{RUNNER --manager serve} exits with status 2, and the version stays 13.

@section Building the Lean model and the oracle

@cindex one-build rule
The model and the conformance package are separate Lake packages.  The
conformance package requires the model by a local path, so the model is built
first:

@example
(cd model && direnv exec . lake build)
(cd bisim && direnv exec . bash -c 'lake build && lake exe corpus-gen')
@end example

The default targets of the model are @code{Agentic} and @code{Pollution}.  The
second target checks that an import of the model installs no unwanted global
instance.  The default targets of the conformance package are the
@code{Conformance} library and the two executables
@command{conformance-oracle} and @command{corpus-gen}.  Never run two full
model builds at once.  @file{model/Agentic/Core/DslFlagship.lean} proves its
theorems when it runs the checker, the cost algebra, and the interpreter inside
the kernel, and this takes minutes of wall clock and several gigabytes of
memory.  The conformance package deliberately excludes that module and builds
in seconds.  A conformance build that takes minutes therefore indicates that
the dependency was re-coupled.

After @command{corpus-gen}, @code{git status --short bisim/corpus} is expected
to print nothing.  A diff is a change to the specification.

@section Conformance gates

@cindex tier0
@cindex tier1
The conformance gates live beside the code they check:

@example
./bisim/ci/tier0.sh
N=500 SEED=1 ./bisim/ci/tier1.sh
@end example

@file{bisim/ci/tier0.sh} runs two executables with no Lean in the loop.
@command{tier0} replays every frozen vector through the Haskell codec, the
guards, the string layer, and the observation.  @command{tier1} rebuilds the
curated checked entries in the production authoring surface, and it compares
the whole reply with the frozen one.  The comparison covers the printed
program, the folds, the ask counts, and one trace and two bills per world.
@file{bisim/ci/tier1.sh} runs the live differential test through the
@command{bisim} executable.  That executable draws fresh programs and worlds,
sends them to the prebuilt @command{conformance-oracle}, and compares the
versioned observation surface.  The oracle path comes from @code{ORACLE}, and
it defaults to the build output of the conformance package.  The iteration
count comes from @code{N}, and the seed comes from @code{SEED}.  When the
oracle is missing, the script refuses to build it and refuses to fall back
silently, which preserves the one-build rule.

@section Runtime, command-line, and engine gates

@cindex policy gate
The deterministic gates for the runner and the engines use local process
doubles and contact no paid service:

@example
direnv exec . ./cli/ci/policies.sh
direnv exec . ./cli/ci/examples.sh
direnv exec . ./cli/ci/routing-config.sh
direnv exec . ./cli/ci/citations.sh
direnv exec . ./engine/acp/ci/acp.sh
direnv exec . ./engine/agent-deck/ci/deck.sh
@end example

@file{cli/ci/policies.sh} checks the import policy between the source
directories, and it drives the policy, control, and lineage probes.  Those
probes pin memoization, scheduling, effect multiplicity, decode recovery,
fail-over, persistence, restart, resume, fork, steering, and redirect behavior.
@file{cli/ci/examples.sh} reads the registry out of the built runner.  For
every registered program, it holds the level, the size, the ask-node count,
the cost summary, and the scripted bills against a table that is pinned in the
script.  A program therefore cannot be registered without a price.
@file{cli/ci/routing-config.sh} exercises the model-definition loader, and it
covers schema validation, precedence, fail-over, preflight, and provenance.
@file{cli/ci/citations.sh} checks that every Lean citation in a Haskell
docstring still names an existing file and line.  @file{engine/acp/ci/acp.sh}
drives the ACP transport against @file{engine/acp/test/stub_adapter.py} and
the misbehaving adapters beside it.  @file{engine/agent-deck/ci/deck.sh}
drives the deck transport against @file{engine/agent-deck/test/stub-deck.sh}.
@file{engine/acp/ci/route-live.sh} is the one script that contacts real
backends.  It is manual and paid, and it is outside every automatic gate.

@section Documentation gates

@cindex manual checks
The manual is checked against the source it describes:

@example
direnv exec . make -C doc check
direnv exec . make -C doc check-haskell
direnv exec . python3 doc/check-prose.py
@end example

@code{make -C doc check} renders GNU Info and single-page HTML in a temporary
directory, and it treats any diagnostic as a failure.  It then runs the
checkers in @file{doc/}.  @file{check-manual-sources.py} confirms that every
marked Haskell excerpt in the manual is a verbatim excerpt of live source, and
it confirms that the two complete example modules are included.
@file{check-manual-coverage.py} derives the public authoring surface and the
command-line options from the source.  It then confirms that the reference
chapters account for, classify, and index every item.  @file{check-prose.py}
enforces the prose rules of the manual.  @file{check-workflow-smoke.py}
confirms two things.  The retained smoke record of the manual-authoring
workflow in @file{doc/workflows/} still describes the checked-in script, and
this chapter names the verification commands.

@code{make -C doc check-haskell} builds the package and compiles
@file{doc/examples/Hello.hs} and @file{doc/examples/Registry.hs} against it.
It runs the resulting registry, and it compares the deterministic command-line
transcripts that this manual quotes with fresh output.  It also verifies the
compiler-derived member and instance ledger in
@file{doc/haskell-member-coverage.texi}.  When an export changes, regenerate
that ledger with @code{make -C doc update-haskell-inventory}.
@code{make -C doc all} renders both formats under the ignored
@file{doc/build/} directory.

@section The Pi extension

@cindex ext-pi tests
The TypeScript extension under @file{ext-pi/} has its own checks:

@example
cd ext-pi
npm run check
npm test
AGENT_CAT_E2E_RUNNER="$(cd .. && direnv exec . bash test/cabal.sh list-bin agentic-run)" npm run test:integration
@end example

The packages under @file{ext-pi/node_modules/@@earendil-works} are symbolic links
into the built Pi fork, and the section ``Supported host'' of
@file{ext-pi/README.md} names the supported versions.  A registry install
replaces those links, so the checks do not start with @command{npm ci}.
@file{test/host-versions.test.ts} fails when a linked package reports a version
outside that set or is not listed.

The unit tests run against fixtures.  The integration tests require a freshly
built @command{agentic-run} and the accompanying local Pi packages.  They
exercise the scripted, native ACP, native deck, current-session, owned-child,
and remote targets, and they make no paid call.

@cindex ext-pi service mode
The extension has an explicit service mode.  It is active only when
@env{AGENT_CAT_MANAGER_PROFILE} or @env{AGENT_CAT_MANAGER_PROFILES} names
manager client profiles.  At session start the extension restores only the
local runs of its local state directory, after the check that refuses a
manager state root.  It then opens one manager session for the first profile,
reads the overview and follows the event stream.  The runs, requests and
decision heads of the overview are service observations, keyed by the
endpoint identity of the binding.  They have no local reducer, no file-system
monitor and no local process, and they grant no control authority.
@code{/wfm-status} shows the endpoint, its delivery state (@code{live},
@code{polling} or @code{unreachable}) and the observations.
@code{/wfm-endpoints} chooses among the configured profiles.  A switch
advances the refresh generation, discards a late read of the earlier
endpoint, and refuses a stored reference of the earlier endpoint.  An
unsupported profile, an unsupported capability version, a refused credential
and an unreachable manager show a refusal state, and none of them sends a
command.  Session shutdown and an extension reload close the transport and
send no command, so the manager keeps every run under its own supervision.
The local commands work in both modes.  @file{test/service-mode.test.ts}
checks this lifecycle with a fake transport, and the @code{pi-client} mode of
@file{manager/test/service_http.py} confirms over HTTP that a run continues
after the extension closes during its live stream.

@node Haskell Authoring Reference
@chapter Haskell Authoring Reference

This chapter is the exhaustive reference for the modules that author a
workflow.  An @dfn{author} export is written directly in normal workflow
source.  A @dfn{support} export names a public type, witness, conversion, or
printed form that author-facing signatures expose.  A @dfn{machinery} export
exists for qualified do-notation, type inference, or lowering.  It is
documented so that a compiler message has a place to lead, and ordinary source
does not call it directly.

@include haskell-api-index.texi
@include haskell-member-coverage.texi

@section Programs and inputs

@table @code
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Program support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.ProgramOf support
@item Program, ProgramOf c
@code{ProgramOf c} is a complete result-indexed builder value carrying
@code{progResultCode}, @code{progRawOut :: RawProgram}, and
@code{progPlan :: Plan '[] (El c)}.  @code{Program} is its receipt-valued
specialization.  Build one with @code{workflow} or @code{defining}, and inspect
and execute it through a registry.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.Registry support
@item Registry
A command-line registry with fields @code{regBinary}, @code{regNoun},
@code{regBanner}, and ordered @code{regRows}.  Construct it with the exported
record constructor, as shown in @ref{Registration}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.Row support
@item Row
One registered entry with fields @code{rowExample}, @code{rowDoc},
@code{rowHelp}, @code{rowScript}, and @code{rowTools}.  The record constructor
requires a help page, a deterministic reply table, and a tool table for every
row.  The tool table of most rows is empty.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.Tool support
@item Tool
A function that answers a named tool in process.  The constructor
@code{Tool :: SCode c -> (FilePath -> Text -> IO (El c)) -> Tool} pairs the
code at which the tool answers with a function of the working directory of the
run and the rendered prompt.  The section on in-process tools in
@ref{Operational Model} describes how a run answers with it.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.textTool support
@item textTool function
Build a @code{Tool} that answers text.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.flagTool support
@item flagTool function
Build a @code{Tool} that answers a flag.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.receiptTool support
@item receiptTool function
Build a @code{Tool} that stands in statement position and answers a receipt.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.cliMain author
@item cliMain registry
Run the shared parser and dispatcher for a registry.  Its binary and row noun
come from the Registry, so downstream tools receive the same verbs, input rules,
routing, transports, terminal mode, and exit statuses as @command{agentic-run}.
It selects the default in-process data broker.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.cliMainWithBroker support
@item cliMainWithBroker broker registry
Run the same parser and dispatcher with an injected @code{DataBroker} from
@code{Agentic.Runtime}.  The object governs actual request, reply, control,
event, log and persistence delivery for the execution.  Runtime retains
workflow interpretation and policy, while the original receivers retain
their resource ownership.  The operation and extension contracts are stated
in @file{runtime/BROKER.md}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.Command support
@item Command
Parsed command with constructors @code{Tui}, @code{TuiService}, @code{Usage},
@code{Version}, @code{Help}, @code{List},
@code{Plan}, @code{Cost}, @code{Run}, @code{RoutingInspection},
@code{AdapterOptions},
@code{MigrateRouting}, @code{Machine}, @code{LineageCheck},
@code{MachineLineage}, and @code{ReadFlow}.  This type is exported for policy tests and front ends
that need to inspect a parse.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.parseCommand support
@item parseCommand registry arguments
Purely parse argument texts into @code{Either Text Command}.  The parse
applies verb precedence and checks the coherence of the engine options.
Execution and the resolution of file inputs occur later.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.routesFact support
@item routesFact maybeRoutes
Render the canonical @code{run.routes} text from a resolved route table or the
absence of one.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.workflow author
@item workflow block
Build a Program with an empty function table.  The block begins and ends at an
empty open scope and must finish with a workflow terminal.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.defining author
@item defining functions block
Build a Program with the listed @code{SomeFn} declarations.  Names must be
unique, and each function can call only earlier entries.  A violation is raised
when the Program is forced.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.SomeFn support
@item SomeFn
Existential wrapper for a typed @code{Fn}.  It lets functions with different
parameter and result types occupy one declaration list.  Its constructor is
@code{SomeFn}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Example support
@item Example
Registry payload with constructors @code{Fixed Program} and
@code{Needs Parameterized}.  The first is complete, and the second awaits
operator and runner inputs.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Parameterized support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.ParameterizedOf support
@item Parameterized, ParameterizedOf c
A result-indexed Program that awaits input texts.  Its exported fields are
@code{inputSpecs :: [InputSpec]} and
@code{supply :: [Text] -> Either Text (ProgramOf c)}.  @code{inputNames}
projects the ordered names, and @code{Parameterized} is the receipt-valued
specialization.  Registry and CLI code use these fields, and normal source uses
@code{taking}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.InputSource support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.InputSpec support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.inputNames support
@item InputSource, InputSpec, inputNames
@code{InputSpec} pairs @code{inputName} with @code{inputSource}.  Sources are
@code{PromptInput}, @code{CommandTailInput}, and @code{StandardInput}.  They
govern acquisition only and do not enter @code{RawProgram} or denotation.
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Ins support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.In support
@item Ins, In
@code{Ins hs} is the typed input chain, with constructors @code{INil} and
@code{ICons}.  @code{In} is one checked input name on its way into that chain.
Use @code{input}, @code{:>}, and @code{noInputs} rather than the constructors.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.noInputs author
@item noInputs
Terminate an input chain.  The result type is @code{Ins ()}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.input author
@item input name
Create an ordinary named @code{In}.  A name under @code{run.} must be one of
the four reserved run facts, and an unknown reserved name raises a value-level
error.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.argsInput support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.argsInputAs support
@item argsInput, argsInputAs name
Declare the one unsplit command-tail input.  The default name is @code{args}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.stdinInput support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.stdinInputAs support
@item stdinInput, stdinInputAs name
Declare the one literal standard-input source.  The default name is @code{input}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.taking author
@item taking inputs body
Curry a Haskell function over the declared input texts and return
@code{Parameterized}.  The arity of the body follows from @code{Ins}.  A
different number of inputs through its low-level field returns an explanatory
@code{Left}.
@end table

See @ref{Functions Inputs and Schemas} for complete constructions and the CLI
rules for operator inputs.

@section Prompts, handles, and names

@table @code
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.wf author
@c COVER API Agentic.WF.wf author
@item [wf|text and @{holes@}|]
The scoped prompt quasiquoter.  It removes blank edge lines and common
indentation, emits no trailing newline, and resolves holes as Haskell
expressions.  The result is @code{Words s}.  An invalid fence layout or an
invalid hole syntax is a compile-time quasiquoter error.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.wft author
@c COVER API Agentic.WF.wft author
@item [wft|text and @{defines@}|]
The define-text quasiquoter with the same layout.  A hole can name a define but
not a live @code{V}.  The result is @code{Text}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Says support
@c COVER API Agentic.WF.Says support
@item Says
Class of values that a prompt hole accepts.  Its method is
@code{says :: a -> Words s}.  Instances cover a spliceable live handle,
@code{Text}, and existing prompt pieces at the same scope.

@c COVER API Agentic.WF.saysText support
@item saysText value
Read a scopeless define through the same path used by @code{wft} holes and return
@code{Text}.  A live binding fails the @code{Scopeless} constraint.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.V support
@c COVER API Agentic.WF.V support
@item V h c
Typed live handle.  Constructor @code{V} stores @code{vName}, the printed name,
and @code{vIx}, the de Bruijn variable at the own scope of the binding.
Binders create it, and prompt holes, decisions, branches, calls, and terminals
consume it.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.named author
@item named name statement
Select the printed name of a binding while preserving its meaning and typed
variable.  Names of the forms @code{bN} and @code{rN} are reserved and raise a
value-level error.  A nonbinding statement ignores the selection.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Piece support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Words support
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.lit support
@item Piece, Words, lit
@code{Piece s} is one literal or interpolation prompt fragment, and
@code{Words s} is a list of them.  @code{lit :: Text -> Piece s} constructs a
literal.  The quasiquoters are the normal source-level interface.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.KnownIx support
@item KnownIx h s
Constraint proving that handle @code{h} is live in scope @code{s}.  It resolves
the necessary weakening automatically and appears in signatures for operations
that consume handles.
@end table

@section Parties and question sources

@table @code
@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.PartyK support
@item PartyK
Promoted party kind with constructors @code{IsModel}, @code{IsTool}, and
@code{IsPerson}.  It restricts modifiers at compile time.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Party support
@item Party p
Authored addressee together with serving chain and draw.  Construct it through
@code{model}, @code{tool}, or @code{person}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.model author
@item model identifier
Create a model party with no serving pin and draw zero.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.tool author
@item tool identifier
Create a tool party with no executable argv and draw zero.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.person author
@item person identifier
Create a person party at draw zero.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.servedBy author
@item party `servedBy` modelName
Set the primary serving model of a model party.  Tools and people do not satisfy
the required party kind.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.fallingBackTo author
@item party `fallingBackTo` modelName
Append an alternate serving model.  On an unpinned model party, the first
alternate becomes the primary.  Alternates affect execution chains and printed
Raw while leaving the Plan and cost unchanged.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.running author
@item toolParty `running` (command, arguments)
Attach a literal argv to a tool.  The prompt is sent on standard input, and no
shell or argv interpolation is used.  Value position lowers to observation, and
statement position lowers to effect.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.drawing author
@item drawing party n
Set the question's draw.  A different draw forms a different semantic question
and memo key.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Ask support
@item Ask s
A question before its position imposes an answer kind.  Its fields retain the
addressee, the serving chain, the draw, and the prompt words.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.ask author
@item ask party words
Build @code{Ask s}.  In ordinary binding or function-body position it is a text
question.  Panel, review, act, and explicit-answer forms impose other kinds.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.confirm author
@item confirm party words
Build a flag-valued @code{Rhs}, normally bound and consumed by Haskell
@code{if}, @code{when}, or @code{unless}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.panel author
@item panel members
Build a verdict-valued @code{Rhs} as the product of the member verdicts in
order.  The list must be nonempty, and @code{panel []} raises the empty-panel
error of the language when forced.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.panelText author
@item panelText labeledMembers
Build text from labeled member blocks in order.  The list must be nonempty,
and the labels must be valid and distinct.  An invalid value raises the
corresponding builder error.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.Decider author
@item Decider
Closed classification vocabulary with constructors @code{LastNonEmptyLineIs},
@code{ContainsLine}, @code{AnyLineStartsWith}, and @code{AnyPathMatches}.

@c COVER API Agentic.Workflow.decide author
@item decide decider textHandle needles
Purely classify bound text and return a flag @code{Rhs}.  Needles are literal
and nonempty, and at least one must be present.  Empty input raises a
value-level error.  The operation asks nothing and adds no request node.
@end table

