@node Runner Reference
@chapter Runner Reference

This chapter is the complete command-line reference for @command{agentic-run}.
Local commands operate on the registry that is supplied to
@code{Agentic.Cli.cliMain}.  The noun below is @samp{example} in this
repository.  Terminal service mode uses the selected manager's catalogue.

@include runner-index.texi

@section Synopsis

@example
agentic-run --help
agentic-run --version
agentic-run --tui [--local]
agentic-run --tui --service CLIENT_PROFILE [CLIENT_PROFILE ...]
agentic-run --manager init --root DIR [--port PORT]
agentic-run --manager add-client --config FILE --profile-file FILE [--profile ID ...]
agentic-run --manager serve --config FILE [--legacy-history ROOT=PROFILE ...]
agentic-run --manager admin --config FILE
agentic-run --manager --help
agentic-run frontend --capabilities
agentic-run frontend
agentic-run frontend-io < request.json
agentic-run frontend-export --state STATE < request.json
agentic-run list [--json [--descriptor-version 3]]
agentic-run --routing [--json] [--persona NAME] [--offline | --refresh-models]
agentic-run --migrate-routing SOURCE --output DESTINATION
agentic-run adapter-options --adapter ADAPTER [--adapter-arg ARG ...] [--json]
agentic-run help NAME
agentic-run NAME --help
agentic-run plan NAME [--raw] [--require-pinned] [--json] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run cost NAME [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME --scripted [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME [--routing] [ROUTING-OPTION ...] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME --session ID [DECK-OPTION ...] [--routing] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME --engine acp [ACP-OPTION ...] [--routing] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run machine RUN_ID NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run lineage-check restart|resume|fork PARENT_STORE NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run machine-restart RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run machine-resume RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run machine-fork RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME [FORK-EDIT ...] RUN-OPTION ...
@end example

A verb in head position wins over a registered row of the same name.  Help has
two per-row spellings and one general spelling.  A bare registered name is
refused rather than interpreted as help or execution.  An incomplete
@option{--manager} or @option{--tui} form, such as @samp{--manager serve}
without @option{--config} or @samp{--tui --service} without a client profile,
is refused at exit 1 with the usage lines of that form.  The form
@samp{--manager --help} prints the usage lines of the four @option{--manager}
forms at exit 0.

@section Terminal frontend

@c COVER CLI --tui author
@c COVER CLI --local author
@c COVER CLI --service author
@table @code
@item --tui
Open the terminal frontend for the current executable's registry.  The command
requires terminal input and output and refuses pipes before initializing Vty.
A downstream executable that calls @code{cliMain}, including @command{wf},
therefore browses and launches its own registry rather than this repository's
example table.
@item --local
Select the local backend with @option{--tui}.  The form
@samp{--tui --local} behaves exactly as @option{--tui} alone.
@item --service CLIENT_PROFILE [CLIENT_PROFILE @dots{}]
Select the manager backend with @option{--tui}.  The command takes 1 to 8
arguments, and the frontend connects the first one at startup.  Each argument
is an absolute path to a private version-1 JSON client profile identifying an
HTTPS API
endpoint, a @code{credentialFile}, and a @code{caFile}.  These files belong to
the client.  They are not manager registry or store paths.  The client trusts
only the certificates in the @code{caFile}, accepts only TLS 1.3, and keeps
certificate and hostname verification enabled.  It also refuses a certificate
chain whose Name Constraints use a form that the pinned validator evaluates
incorrectly, such as an @code{iPAddress} subtree or an excluded
@code{dNSName} subtree that differs from the server name only in letter case
or a trailing dot, or that uses the leading-dot form.  The API reference in
@file{doc/api/README.md} lists these refusals.

Service mode starts no local machine, helper process, or local runner state.
When the connection fails at startup, the frontend prints one fixed line for
the declared failure and exits with status 1 before it initializes Vty.  The
lines include @samp{--tui --service: invalid client profile},
@samp{--tui --service: credential refused},
@samp{--tui --service: manager API version unsupported}, and
@samp{--tui --service: manager unreachable}.

A failed TLS handshake has its own line.  When the @code{caFile} does not
validate the certificate of the manager for the endpoint host, the line is
@samp{--tui --service: manager certificate not accepted by caFile; the
certificate must chain to caFile and its subjectAltName must match the
endpoint host}.  Every other handshake failure gives @samp{--tui --service:
TLS handshake with the manager failed; the endpoint must serve TLS 1.3}.

A client file that the client refuses gives a line that names the role of
the file and the first rule that it fails.  The roles are @samp{client
profile}, @samp{credentialFile}, and @samp{caFile}.  Each file must exist, must
not be a symbolic link, must be a regular file within the size limit of its
role, and must not be writable by group or others.  The client profile and the
credential file must also be owned by the user, give no access to group or
others, and have one hard link.  For example, a client profile with mode 0644
gives @samp{--tui --service: client profile is not private; give it mode
0600}.

When the terminal has at least 72 columns and 16 rows, the header of every
service screen has an identity row.  The row names the endpoint host and port
of the client profile, the first 18 characters of the authority epoch, the
credential scopes, and the stream identifier of the manager capabilities.  The
stream identifier comes last, so a narrow terminal shows only its leading
characters.

@kbd{E} opens the Endpoints view from every service screen without text
entry, which includes the profile and workflow browsers and the request
screen.  @key{TAB} and the other browser keys keep their behavior.  The view
lists each client profile in command-line order with its number, its
connection state, its path, and the identity row of its latest session.  The
state is @samp{active}, @samp{not connected}, @samp{connecting}, or
@samp{failed:} followed by the fixed reason.  @key{UP} and @key{DOWN} select a
profile, @key{RET} connects it, and @key{ESC} returns to the screen below the
view.  The active session continues to work during the connection.  When the
connection fails, the active session stays active and the view shows the
fixed reason, which is the startup line without the @samp{--tui --service:}
prefix.  When the connection succeeds, the frontend stops the reads and
commands of the earlier session, closes it, clears every observation,
selection, retained result, and settled command, and loads the manager
profiles through the new session.  No result of the earlier session changes
the screen after the switch.  A command whose outcome is unresolved at the
switch, or whose send was in progress, stays listed under its own profile as
unresolved.  No later session sends it.  Selecting an earlier profile again
opens a new session.

After the profiles load, the frontend reads the authorized manager overview
through the single-flight read lane, and it shows the profiles when that read
completes.  The same happens after a switch to another endpoint and after
@kbd{r} on the profile browser.  @kbd{O} opens the Manager overview view from
the workflow browser.  @key{TAB}, @kbd{h}, @key{RET}, the arrow keys, and the
other browser keys keep their behavior, and @key{ESC} returns from the view to
the workflow browser.  The view lists the requests with their workflow, phase,
admission state, and blocking reasons, then the preparations, then the runs
with their runtime status, supervision, and verification as distinct fields,
and then the pending decisions with their run and kind.  A queued request
also shows its position among the queued requests of its profile, in overview
order, as @samp{request queued 1 of 2} in the list and as
@samp{Profile queue position: 1 of 2} in the details.  The @samp{Position}
line shows the position that the manager reports, which counts the queued
requests of every profile.  The view lists every active run of the
authorized profiles, so several runs that run at once each show their own
runtime status and decisions.  A wide terminal shows
the list beside the details of the selected row.  A narrow terminal shows one
pane at a time, and @key{LEFT} and @key{RIGHT} select the pane.  @key{UP} and
@key{DOWN} select a row.  The frontend keeps the selected row by its kind and
identity, so the same row stays selected when a refresh adds or removes other
rows.  When the selected row leaves the overview, the row at its last position
is selected.  @kbd{g} reads the overview again.  A declared
refusal keeps the last complete overview and marks it stale with the refusal
code, for example @samp{Overview: stale (TransportUnavailable)} after the
manager stops.  The view is not read on the one-second timer.

@key{RET} on an overview row opens it.  A request row selects the request and
shows it by its phase: the request screen for a draft, and the review or the
live monitor of its run after the next read.  When the loaded workflow
catalogue does not list the workflow revision of the request, the frontend
first loads the catalogue of its profile.  A preparation row opens its request
when the overview lists that request.  A run row selects the run by its
identifier, and a decision row selects the run of the decision.  The frontend
reads a selected run by its identifier, so a run that no request of this
session created opens.  A row opens only while no manager command is in
progress.  @key{ESC} on the live monitor, on a question or recovery head, and on
the request screen while no command is in progress returns to the overview, or
to the Manager decisions view when the operator last used that view, and
sends nothing.  The footer of the live monitor names that view as
@samp{Esc OVERVIEW} or @samp{Esc DECISIONS}.  The selection stays, so the run
continues at the manager, and @key{RET} on its row shows it again.
@key{RET} on the row of another run selects that run, and the earlier run
continues at the manager.  The workflow browser creates a new request while
earlier runs continue.  A frontend that starts again reads the overview, which
lists the active runs that an earlier session or another client started, and
@key{RET} on the row of such a run shows its live monitor.

@kbd{D} opens the Manager decisions view from the workflow browser.
@key{TAB} and the other browser keys keep their behavior, and @key{ESC}
returns from the view to the workflow browser.  The view reads
@code{/v1/decisions} without @code{runId}, which lists the pending decision
head of each run of the authorized profiles in manager observation order, and
it keeps that order.  A collection that names one decision or one run twice
is refused.  Each row shows the kind and the run of the head, and the details
show the decision, the run, the kind, the state, the occurrence, and, for a
question, the answer type, the addressee, and the prompt.  An ask that the
reviewed policy field @code{personAnswers} routes to the person is a question
decision whose addressee names the person, for example
@samp{person model:fixed-point}, so it appears in the view like every other
pending head.  The decision rows of the overview show the same details.
@key{UP} and @key{DOWN} select a row, and the frontend keeps the selected row
by its identity.  @key{RET} opens the run of the selected head by its
identifier, and the live monitor shows the question head of that run.  The
answer editor converts the typed text with the simple answer codes:
@code{text} takes the text, @code{flag} takes yes, no, true, or false,
@code{receipt} takes no text, and @code{verdict} takes JSON.  A question with
a structured code takes JSON text in the structured answer editor.  The
details of its head show the answer schema, and the header of its editor shows
the editor schema of the decision, for example @samp{structured JSON
@{"notes": [string], "ok": boolean@}}.  Before any send, the frontend checks
the JSON value against that schema: an object must have exactly the fields of
the schema, and each value and each array item must have the type of its
schema.  A value that does not agree is refused with a numbered key outcome
that names the first field, for example @samp{answer did not start: answer
field ok must be a boolean}.  A structured question whose decision gives no
editor schema takes no answer.  @kbd{C-d} sends the answer to that decision.
@kbd{C-d} while an answer to that decision is in flight shows the numbered key
outcome @samp{answer did not start: an answer to this decision is in flight.}
and sends nothing.  @key{ESC} on the live
monitor returns to the Manager decisions view.  The frontend reads the
decision heads when the view opens and keeps them current through the manager
event stream while the view is shown.  @kbd{g} reads them again.  A declared
refusal keeps the last complete heads and marks them stale with the refusal
code, for example @samp{Decisions: stale (TransportUnavailable)}.  The status
line above the list states the number of pending heads, for example
@samp{Decisions: current; pending heads: 2}.

@kbd{H} opens the History view from the workflow browser.  @key{TAB} and the
other browser keys keep their behavior, and @key{ESC} returns from the view to
the workflow browser.  The view reads every page of every window of
@code{/v1/runs}, which lists the managed runs of the authorized profiles and
the legacy entries of their bound retention roots, and it keeps the
identifier order of the collection.  A collection that names one run twice is
refused.  A legacy entry is an entry with an unreadable manifest, or a known
run with @code{observer} supervision and no request.  Each row shows the
runtime status and the run, and the details show the workflow, the profile,
the request, the runtime status, the supervision, the verification, the
integrity, and the limitations.  The status line above the list counts the
runs, for example @samp{History: current; runs: 302 (managed 2, legacy
300)}.  @key{UP} and @key{DOWN} select a row, @key{HOME} and @key{END} select
the first and the last run, and the frontend keeps the selected row by its
identity.  The frontend reads the run list when the view opens and keeps it
current through the manager event stream while the view is shown.  @kbd{g}
reads it again.  A declared refusal keeps the last complete run list and
marks it stale with the refusal code.

@key{RET} on a row opens the read-only run detail of that run.  It selects
nothing and sends nothing.  The detail of a managed run comes from its
snapshot and shows the run, the workflow, the target, the runtime status, the
supervision, the integrity, the verification, the result reference, and the
number of occurrences.  The detail of a legacy entry comes from
@code{/v1/runs/@var{id}}, because the manager refuses the other run resources
of a legacy entry.  The event stream keeps the detail current while it is
shown, and @kbd{g} reads it again.  @key{UP} and @key{DOWN} scroll a detail
that is taller than the main area.  The saved result, export, and lineage
lines come last, and a new outcome of one of them scrolls the detail to its
end, so the outcome of the key is visible at 80 by 24 cells.  @kbd{r}
retrieves the verified result of a succeeded managed run whose result is
verified or referenced.  The download
must have the size and the SHA-256 digest that the run outputs state.  For
any other run, @kbd{r} downloads nothing, and the status line states the
reason, for example @samp{result not retrieved: a legacy entry publishes no
size and digest for its result}.  A refused retrieval retains no bytes, and
the next @kbd{r} retries it at once.  @kbd{s} then opens the save dialog, which
publishes the retained bytes at a new absolute path as on the live monitor.
@key{ESC} returns to the History view.  The frontend keeps the verified
results by run identifier, for the live monitor and the run detail alike, so
the result of one run stays retained while another run is retrieved.  At most
eight runs are kept, and a retrieval of a new run beyond that bound removes
the run whose retrieval completed first.

@kbd{e} on the live monitor or on the run detail of a managed run opens the
export name editor when the snapshot of the run publishes a verified result
of a succeeded run.  For any other run, and for a legacy entry, @kbd{e}
starts nothing and shows a numbered key outcome, for example
@samp{export did not start: the run did not succeed.}  The editor takes one
export name of 1 to 128 ASCII letters, digits, dots, underscores, and hyphens
that starts with a letter or a digit.  @key{ESC} closes it and sends nothing.
@kbd{C-d} sends the export once through the command lane.  The frontend first
reads the first page of @code{/v1/runs/@var{id}/exports} and sends its
strong entity tag as @code{If-Match} with the body
@code{@{"name": @var{name}@}}.  A 412 @code{stale-revision} refusal, which the
manager gives when the export collection changed after that read, leaves
nothing to resend, and the export line of the run states
@samp{Export @var{name}: refused (412 stale-revision); the export collection
changed and nothing was sent again; e exports again}.  After the effect
@code{exported} of the command, the frontend reads the export receipt.  It
must have the state @code{published}, and the frontend downloads the export
and checks its size and SHA-256 digest against that receipt.  The export
lines of the run then show the receipt, the verified size and digest, and a
bounded preview.  The exported bytes are the code and value of the verified
result as compact JSON followed by one LF, so they differ from the bytes of
the source-result artifact.

@kbd{l} on the live monitor or on the run detail of a managed run reads the
first page of @code{/v1/runs/@var{id}/lineage-requests} and opens the lineage
menu.  The menu names the parent run and states for each of restart, resume,
and fork whether the @code{eligible} list of the page names it.  When that
list is empty, the menu shows the @code{refusal} code of the page.  For a
legacy entry, @kbd{l} opens nothing.  @kbd{r} sends a restart and @kbd{s}
sends a resume.  @kbd{f} opens the fork edits, which list the completed
occurrences of the run.  There, @key{UP} and @key{DOWN} select an occurrence,
@kbd{d} drops its answer, @kbd{k} keeps it, and @key{RET} opens the
replacement answer editor.  In that editor, @kbd{C-d} converts the text by the
observation code of the occurrence, as the answer editor converts an answer:
text as given, a flag from @samp{yes}, @samp{no}, @samp{true}, or
@samp{false}, an acknowledgement from empty text, and a verdict or a
structured answer from JSON text.  @kbd{C-d} on the fork edits sends the fork
with one edit for each dropped or replaced occurrence.  @key{ESC} closes the
editor, leaves the fork edits, or closes the menu, and sends nothing.  An
operation that the @code{eligible} list does not name is refused before any
send, and the menu shows the reason.  An eligible operation is sent once
through the command lane with the strong entity tag of the first collection
page that the menu showed as @code{If-Match}.  A 412 @code{stale-revision}
refusal, which the manager gives when the revision of the run changed after
that read, leaves nothing to resend, and the lineage line of the run states
the refusal.  After the effect @code{lineage-created} of the command, the
frontend reads the child request, which must name the parent run and the
operation, and opens it by its phase.  The child request declares no input,
because its inputs come from the parent run, and its request screen names the
parent run and the operation.  @key{RET} prepares its review.  The summary
review names the parent run, the operation, and each fork edit, where a
replacement shows the SHA-256 of its answer.  @kbd{y} approves the child
review as it approves any exact review.

The frontend keeps the text drafts by identity: the input text of each request
and input, and the answer text of each decision.  A draft survives a refresh, a
resize, a change of the selection, and leaving and reopening a run.  A
completed input or answer command removes its draft.  When the decision at the
head of a run changes, the draft of the earlier decision is removed, and the
answer editor shows the draft of the new head or no text.  One exception keeps
a draft: when the head of the displayed run changes before the frontend sent
an answer to the earlier head, the new head takes the draft of the earlier
head unless it has its own draft, a run without a head keeps the draft, and
the line @samp{Control:} states @samp{decision changed; draft kept}.  The
frontend never sends a kept draft by itself.  At most 64 drafts are kept.

A 412 @code{stale-revision} refusal of a command is definite, because the
manager recognizes a matching retry of a durable command before it evaluates
the precondition.  The command lane becomes idle, nothing is retained for a
resend, and the frontend reads the selection again.  A refused answer keeps
its draft, and the line @samp{Control:} states @samp{answer refused: 412
stale-revision; decision changed; draft kept}.  @kbd{C-d} then sends the kept
draft to the new head only when the operator presses it.

After the first overview of a session is installed, the frontend follows the
manager event stream from the cursor of that overview.  Each invalidation
names a resource.  An invalidation of an overview member, which is a request,
preparation, run, or decision, or of a resource below a member, causes the
overview to be read again while its view is shown.  The manager reports a
change of the runtime status of a run as the snapshot resource of that run,
so the overview shows the new runtime status.  When the view is hidden, that read waits and runs when the view
opens.  An invalidation of a resource that the composite read of the selected
request reads, such as the request, its run snapshot, or the receipt of a
retained command, causes that read to be read again.  An invalidation of a
run or a decision, or of a resource below one, causes the decision heads to be
read again while the Manager decisions view is shown, and that read waits
while the view is hidden.  An invalidation of a run, or of a resource below
one, causes the run list or the shown run detail to be read again while the
History view or its run detail is shown, and that read waits while both are
hidden.  Each of the four reads has at most one fetch in progress, and
invalidations that arrive during it
cause exactly one later fetch.  These reads use the single-flight read lane
under the rules of automatic refresh, so no read starts during a preparation
or a send, after an internal fault, or while a deferred key pauses refresh.
The header row above the identity row shows the delivery state:
@samp{delivery connecting}, @samp{delivery live}, @samp{delivery polling},
@samp{delivery disconnected since @var{time} (@var{code})},
@samp{delivery resnapshot}, @samp{delivery stopped (@var{reason})}, or
@samp{delivery not started}.  The time of a disconnection is the UTC time of
the first failure since delivery last succeeded.  When the manager ends the
stream or the connection fails, the frontend reconnects with the identifier
of the last complete event after a delay of one second that doubles up to
thirty seconds.  After two consecutive failures of the stream, or when the
manager refuses the stream, for example with status 429 when the two event
stream readers of the credential are in use, the frontend polls the events
from the same identifier every second and shows @samp{delivery polling}.  It
tries the stream again after the delay, and a stream that delivers returns
the state to @samp{delivery live}.  While the frontend polls or is
disconnected, it reads the selected request every second.  When the manager
refuses the cursor with status 410, the frontend discards the results of the
reads in progress that its refresh coordinator started, reads the overview,
the selected request, and the decision heads again, and follows the stream from the cursor of the new
overview.  This holds for every overview read in progress, also one that
@kbd{g} or the startup bootstrap started before the 410: it installs no
rows, and the overview read that follows the 410 then installs its rows
and starts the stream.  When the manager refuses that overview read, or
an overview read that follows an invalidation, the frontend reads the overview
again after a delay of one second that doubles up to thirty seconds, until an
overview is installed.  A switch to another
endpoint stops the stream of the earlier session.

When a read fails because the transport fails, or because the manager refuses
it with status 503 and the code @code{storage-unavailable}, the header row
shows @samp{manager unreachable since @var{time}} in place of the delivery
state.  @var{time} is the UTC time of the first such failure.  The frontend
keeps every last observation and shows it as stale, also an observation whose
view it has not read since that failure.  The pending decisions stay on the
screen.  The frontend sends no command by itself, and it shows no run as
cancelled or completed because of the failure.  The first read that reaches
the manager again ends the state, and every view is then read again when it is
shown.  The event stream reconnects from the identifier of its last complete
event, or reads a new overview after a 410 refusal, as described above.  After
a manager restart, the live monitor shows a run of the earlier lifetime as the
manager reports it, with its lost supervision after the runtime status of its
snapshot, for example @samp{Runtime: Running; supervision lost}.  The runtime
line names every supervision other than @code{owned} in the same way.  A
restart publishes new profile revisions, so the frontend reads the current
profile before each workflow catalogue, and a request that the earlier
lifetime queued opens from the overview.

When the manager refuses the credential with status 401, when the client
cannot read the credential file, or when the credential changes during the
session, the frontend enters the refused-credential state.  A revocation of
the credential through local administration has this effect.  The header row
shows @samp{credential refused} in place of the delivery state, and the
status line states that no mutation or automatic refresh starts.  Every key
that asks for a mutation then shows the numbered key outcome
@samp{@var{operation} did not start: the credential was refused.}, a summary
@kbd{y} on the exact review shows @samp{Approval did not start: the
credential was refused.}, and no exact resend is offered.  The event stream
stops, and the frontend reads nothing by itself.  The last observations stay
on the screen, marked stale with the refusal code, and the manager runs
continue.  Only an explicit read, such as @kbd{g} on the overview, reads the
manager again.  When such a read succeeds, the state ends, and the next
overview starts the event stream again.  The frontend never starts a local
run in place of the refused session.

A key whose operation needs a credential scope that the capabilities do not
list starts nothing.  It shows the numbered key outcome
@samp{@var{operation} did not start: this credential lacks @var{scope}.} before
any other admission, and it sends no request.  The scopes are those that the
manager requires: @code{submit} to create a request, capture, set or remove an
input, enqueue, or withdraw, @code{submit} and @code{control} to approve or
discard, @code{control} to answer, retry, or use any run control, and
@code{observe} and @code{export} to export.  On the exact review, a summary
@kbd{y} without such a scope shows the approval notice
@samp{Approval did not start: this credential lacks @var{scope}.}, and the
approval hint is absent.

Service mode follows one selected request or run at a time through a running
manager.  It browses the manager's profiles, workflows, and help, creates a
request, opens an existing request or run from the Manager overview view,
submits literal or captured inputs, removes a supplied input, withdraws a
request, discards a review, and displays the exact manager review before
explicit approval.  After approval it follows the run in the live monitor, answers the
questions that the manager routes to the person, invokes an offered recovery
retry, recognizes the terminal state, retrieves the verified result, and saves
its bytes.  Receipt status is separate from request association and runtime
outcome.

In the input editor of a request, @kbd{C-d} sends the editor text as a
literal input.  @kbd{C-t} captures the exact editor text as raw UTF-8 bytes
instead.  @kbd{C-o} opens a path editor, where @kbd{C-d} reads the named local
file and captures its exact bytes, and @key{ESC} closes the editor.  The file
must be a regular UTF-8 file of at most the @code{captureBytes} limit of the
capabilities.  The frontend reads the file itself, so the manager receives
the bytes and never the path.  A path that the frontend cannot read keeps the
path editor open with its reason, and nothing is sent.  A capture is one
@code{application/octet-stream} POST of @code{/v1/captures} for the request,
without @code{If-Match}.  Its receipt must name the request, the profile, and
the size and SHA-256 digest of the sent bytes.  The frontend then sends a new
@code{set-input} command with source @code{capture} and the capture
identifier, and that command completes on its own @code{input-changed}
effect.  The request screen lists the captured inputs.  A captured input
agrees with the exact review only when the review repeats the size and digest
of a capture receipt that this session received, so the review of a capture
made by another session cannot be approved here.

In the input editor, @kbd{C-r} removes the supplied value of the displayed
input with a @code{remove-input} command.  When the request supplies no value
for that input, a key outcome states this and nothing is sent.  The request
screen lists the missing inputs of the request.  On the request screen of a
request in the @code{draft} or @code{queued} phase, @kbd{W} opens the
confirmation of a withdrawal.  On the exact review, @kbd{X} opens the
confirmation of a discard of the review.  A key that cannot start its command
shows its key outcome and opens no confirmation.  In a confirmation, @kbd{y}
sends the @code{withdraw} or @code{discard} command once, for the request or
preparation that the key named, and only while that resource is still
displayed.  @kbd{n} or @key{ESC} closes the confirmation with a key outcome
that states that nothing was sent.  Each of these commands completes only on
its own observed effect: @code{input-changed} for a removal,
@code{withdrawn} for a withdrawal, and @code{discarded} for a discard.  A
withdrawn request shows the phase @code{withdrawn}.  After a discard the
request is a draft without a preparation, and @key{RET} prepares a new
review.

Every manager command is sent once.  The client retains the original pending
command and its receipt location, and it never rebuilds or retargets them.
When the effect of a send is uncertain, the frontend states that it made no
automatic resend.  If the manager offers an exact resend, @kbd{x} opens a
confirmation, @kbd{y} sends the retained command again unchanged, and @kbd{n}
or @key{ESC} closes the confirmation without a send.  The frontend never repeats
an uncertain send without that confirmation.  When a read of the receipt
location reports the command @code{refused} or @code{unresolved}, for example
an approval or an answer whose dispatch a manager restart left unresolved, the
frontend keeps the retained command and its receipt location.  The notice
then states the receipt state and the reconciliation rule of the client, for
example @samp{Outcome unresolved: receipt unresolved; reconciliation
uncertain}, and only @kbd{x} and @kbd{y} can send the command again.

All reads of the manager pass through one single-flight observation lane.  No
read starts while another read is in flight.  The frontend reads the selected
request, its review, the receipt of a retained command, and the run snapshot,
run controls, and pending decision in one composite read, and it installs a
complete read in one step.  Without a live event stream, it repeats that read
on a one-second timer.  While the stream is live, the timer read occurs at most
every five seconds as a safety read.  @kbd{g} requests the read at once.  When the manager refuses a read with a declared
failure, the frontend keeps the last complete observation, marks it stale with
the refusal code, and continues to display it.  If no complete observation
exists, the status line states that none is installed.  Observations are
display and precondition data only, and they never confer ownership.

Every key that asks for a mutation has exactly one visible outcome, which is a
start, a refusal, or a deferral.  A refusal or a deferral appears as a numbered
key outcome that states the reason, and it remains until the next key press or
until the view changes.  A key never cancels a read of a page set, because an
abandoned page set holds a manager slot until it expires.  The key is deferred
instead, and a deferred key is never replayed.  A key that arrives during a
read of single resources cancels that read, and its mutation starts.

Exiting the frontend with @kbd{C-c}, or with @kbd{q} while no answer editor has
the keys, closes its transport without cancelling manager-owned work.  The run
continues in the manager.  Service mode starts no child process, so no process
of the frontend remains after the exit.

On the exact review, only @kbd{y} in the summary view approves, and while the
discard confirmation is open, @kbd{y} confirms the discard instead.  @key{RET}
never approves, @kbd{y} never approves in the detail view that @kbd{d} opens,
and no key approves while the key help is open.  A summary @kbd{y} starts an
approval only when no manager command is in progress or unresolved, no internal
fault has occurred, and the displayed review is the installed one, is still
live, binds the request, its literals, and its captured inputs, and fits the terminal in full.  An
observation read in flight does not prevent the approval, because the approval
ends that read before it prepares the command.  Every press of @key{RET} or
@kbd{y} on the review shows one fixed notice that carries the sequence number of
the press among the approval-key presses of the session.  The notice appears in
the summary, in the detail view and in the key help.  It remains until a key
changes the screen or the view, or until the review leaves the screen, and
observation refreshes that keep the review do not remove it.  When the
preflight check refuses an approval before any send, the notice of that press
changes to state that the approval was not sent.  The approval hint appears
exactly when @kbd{y} would approve, and it then states that @kbd{y} approves
the exact review and starts the run and that @key{RET} does not approve.  The
summary view shows the request, the preparation, the profile with the expiry,
the @code{If-Match} value, the parent run, the operation, and the edits of a
lineage review, and the five approval selectors, and it reserves the rows of
the longest notice.  The summary has no frame, so its rows use the whole main
area.  With the identifiers that the manager issues, a review without
lineage, a restart or resume review, a fork review with one replacement, and
a review whose profile identifier has the maximum length of 128 characters
each fit a terminal of 80 columns by 24 rows, so the operator can approve at
that size.  A review that does not fit is refused.  At 40 columns by 12 rows
the selectors do not fit, and @kbd{y} shows @samp{Approval did not start: the
complete review does not fit.  Resize the terminal.}  A resize keeps the
review, the input drafts, and the answer drafts.

A declared client failure keeps its declared meaning.  Any other failure of a
manager call inside the frontend is an internal frontend fault, and the
frontend reports it with fixed text that contains no exception detail.  After
such a fault, the session starts no further mutation and offers no exact
resend.  Only read-only actions remain available.  These include an explicit
observation refresh with @kbd{g} when a request exists, and detachment with
@kbd{q}.  The session also stops automatic refresh, and the fault notice names
any command whose intent the manager accepted but whose effect it has not yet
observed.  A send that the fault interrupted keeps its original pending command
and any earlier receipt location, and its outcome stays uncertain.

After association, the frontend shows the run in the live monitor from the
manager's run snapshot, which the observation lane supplies.  The monitor shows
the published runtime status, or states that the runtime is not yet observed,
and it shows the approval receipt status and any stale observation on separate
lines.  It shows the occurrences and their published output as the snapshot
reports them.  Elapsed time remains unknown.  The monitor offers no key of the
local mode for a cancel, a steer, a redirect, or a result.  It sends only the
run controls that the manager offers, as the following paragraphs describe.

The run is terminal only when the snapshot runtime status is succeeded,
failed, cancelled, or orphaned.  A null, starting, running, or cancelling
runtime is never terminal.  For a succeeded run whose verification is verified
and whose result reference names the verified artifact, the frontend reads the
source-result metadata from the run outputs.  That metadata must name the run,
the source-result kind, and the size, digest, and code of the result reference.
The frontend then downloads the artifact through
@code{Agentic.Manager.Client.downloadVerified}, which checks the size and the
SHA-256 digest against that metadata.  The frontend
keeps the verified bytes unchanged in memory, up to 64 MiB, and shows the
terminal status, the verified size, the digest, and a bounded preview.  The
manager records the verification of a result reference when the run outputs
are read.  When the snapshot publishes a referenced result, the frontend reads
the run outputs, reads the snapshot again, and downloads only when that
snapshot publishes the verified state for the same result reference.  It
keeps the verified bytes of a run and does not retrieve them again.  A refused
retrieval, or a retrieval that finds no verified result, keeps no bytes, and
the status line and the result lines show its code as a failure that the next
refresh retries.  An automatic refresh retries after the next installed
composite read, so each automatic refresh starts at most one retrieval, and
@kbd{g} retries at once.  Any other terminal status or verification state downloads
nothing.  Service mode never reads a local result artifact.  After the
retrieval, and while no decision head and no run details are shown, @kbd{s}
opens the save dialog.  @kbd{C-d} publishes the retained verified bytes
unchanged at the typed absolute path through the exclusive save function, and
the new file has mode 0600.  A path that is not one absolute single-line file
path, an existing entry at the destination, including a symbolic link, and any
other input or output failure are refused.  The dialog then stays open with a
fixed message and the path, and the destination is left as it was.  A
successful save closes the dialog and adds a result line with the saved size
and the path.  The save publishes a private file with a hard link, so the
destination file system must support hard links.  When the private file cannot
be removed after the link, the save succeeds, and the result line names the
private file that remains.

The frontend also shows the decision head that the manager presents in the
same observation.  A head is shown only when the run controls are owned and
name the decision as the pending head at position zero.  A question head shows
the manager prompt in an answer editor below the service lines.  Text keys
edit the answer, @kbd{C-d} sends it, and @kbd{C-c} detaches.  The editor
supports the simple question codes @code{text}, @code{verdict}, @code{flag},
and @code{receipt}, and it converts the input with the same code-directed
encoding as the local person view.  A @code{flag} answer accepts @samp{yes},
@samp{no}, @samp{true}, @samp{false}, @samp{y}, or @samp{n}, so the input
@samp{false} is sent as the JSON value @code{false}.  A @code{text} answer is
sent as a JSON string, a @code{verdict} answer is parsed as JSON, and a
@code{receipt} answer must be empty and is sent as @code{null}.  An input that
the code cannot convert is refused before any send.  The answer is sent once to the
decision with the entity tag of the displayed decision observation as its
precondition.  It completes only when the command receipt reports an observed
@code{answer-accepted} effect for the answered occurrence of the run.  A
refused or unresolved receipt leaves the answer uncertain, and only an
explicitly confirmed exact resend may repeat it.  A retained answer stays bound
to its original decision when the head changes.  A recovery head shows the
published message and choices, and @kbd{d} opens the run details over it.  When
the run controls offer a retry for the head, @kbd{r} sends that retry once to
the run controls, with the entity tag of the displayed control observation as
its precondition.  A @code{retry} offer sends the @code{retry} operation, and a
@code{choose-recovery} offer that carries the retry choice sends that choice.
The retry completes only when the command receipt reports the observed effect
of the offer kind, @code{retried} or @code{recovery-chosen}, for the recovering
occurrence and attempt of the run.  When a @code{choose-recovery} offer of the
run controls carries the fail-over or the abandon choice for the head, @kbd{f}
or @kbd{a} sends that choice once to the decision, with the entity tag of the
displayed decision observation as its precondition.  The choice completes only
when the command receipt reports the observed @code{recovery-chosen} effect for
the recovering occurrence and attempt of the run.  The recovery dialog shows
the key of each offered choice and lists the published choices that the run
controls do not offer.

The live monitor also sends the run controls that the displayed run controls
offer.  When the controls allow a cancel, @kbd{c} opens a cancel confirmation,
and its @kbd{y} sends the cancel once to the run controls with the entity tag of
the displayed control observation as its precondition.  The cancel completes
when the runtime acknowledgement in its command receipt accepts, queues, or
delivers it.  The runtime cancellation names no control, so the receipt
records no cancel effect.  The line @samp{Control: cancel accepted; waiting for
the runtime status Cancelled} then remains until the snapshot publishes the
runtime status cancelled, and the line becomes @samp{Control: cancel accepted;
the runtime status is Cancelled}.  An accepted cancel is never shown as a
finished or succeeded run.  When a steer offer has the timing
@code{interrupt-now} or @code{next-boundary}, @kbd{i} or @kbd{b} opens the steer
editor below the monitor for that timing.  The offer of the selected occurrence
comes first.  @kbd{C-d} sends the text once to the run controls with the entity
tag of the displayed control observation, and @kbd{ESC} closes the editor.  The
steer completes only when the command receipt reports the observed
@code{steered} effect for the steered attempt.  The line @samp{Control:} shows
@samp{steered}, @samp{failed over}, or @samp{abandoned} from the receipt of the
latest completed control of the run.  The footer shows @samp{c CANCEL},
@samp{i/b STEER}, and @samp{1-9 REDIRECT} only while the controls offer them.

The run controls offer a @code{redirect} for an occurrence inside its dispatch
window, and after the window for the one attempt in flight of an occurrence
that is not an effect.  The live monitor shows the redirect offer of the
selected occurrence, or else the first redirect offer, on the line
@samp{Redirect occurrence}, which states the open dispatch window or the
attempt in flight and lists each offered target with its digit.  A digit key
from @kbd{1} to @kbd{9} sends the redirect of that occurrence to the target of
that digit once to the run controls, with the entity tag of the displayed
control observation as its precondition.  The body names the occurrence and
the target, and not the attempt in flight.  A digit without an offered target
sends nothing and shows a numbered key outcome with fixed text, such as
@samp{redirect did not start: the manager offers no target 9 for occurrence
0.}, or @samp{redirect did not start: the manager offers no redirect for this
run.} while no redirect is offered, for example while an effect is in flight.
The redirect completes only when the command receipt reports the observed
@code{redirected} effect for the occurrence.  The line @samp{Control:} then
shows @samp{redirected occurrence 0 to @var{target}}, or @samp{redirected
occurrence 0 from attempt 1 to @var{target}} for a live redirect.

A runtime acknowledgement that rejects a control, with the state
@code{rejected-stale}, @code{unsupported}, or @code{failed}, is the outcome of
that control.  The line @samp{Control:} shows the operation and the state, for
example @samp{redirect to @var{target}: runtime acknowledgement
rejected-stale}, the command lane becomes idle, and nothing is sent again.

Every @kbd{C-d}, @kbd{r}, @kbd{f}, @kbd{a}, @kbd{c}, @kbd{i}, @kbd{b}, or digit
key that sends nothing, for example for an operation that the run controls do not
offer, shows a numbered key outcome that states the reason.  When an observation read in flight defers a mutation key,
automatic refresh pauses until that read completes or for at most three
seconds from the key outcome, whichever comes first.  While the pause holds,
the observation line states that automatic refresh is paused instead of
@samp{Observation: current}.

A declared client failure keeps its declared meaning, and the internal-fault
rules above apply to every step of the journey.

The manager refuses a revoked credential at every read, so after a
revocation the refused-credential state lasts for the rest of the session.  A
new session with a new credential reads the manager runs again.

A numbered key outcome that is wider than the terminal takes the first of the
two footer rows as a second status row, so its reason stays readable at 80 by
24 cells.  The main area keeps its rows, and with them the fit of the exact
review.
@end table

In local mode, the application enters its Brick screen before runner discovery
begins.  Its trusted configuration keeps the runner alias, executable, and ordered prefix
arguments as invocation data.  Before workflow or routing discovery, a bounded
subprocess obtains the capability server record from that exact configured
invocation.  Child manifests take @code{runnerId}, @code{runnerExecutable}, and
@code{runnerVersion} from the discovered server record rather than from the alias
or wrapper tuple.  A fixed shell keeps the current section, context, status, and
applicable controls visible while bounded workers load the catalogue, help,
routing, launch preview, or machine process.  The browser cycles through Workflows,
Runs, and Routing with @kbd{TAB}.
@kbd{UP} and @kbd{DOWN} move the current selection.  On a wide terminal, each
workflow occupies one name-only row beside an overview containing its description.
On a narrow terminal, @kbd{LEFT} and @kbd{RIGHT} select the list or details pane.
A bullet marks the focused pane.  @kbd{?} opens context-specific key help.

In Workflows, @kbd{/} opens an inline fuzzy filter whose matching rows update as
text is entered.  @kbd{RET} or @kbd{C-d} applies the filter, while @kbd{ESC} keeps
the previous filter.  Run details show lineage,
persona, concrete realizations, bills, final-result availability, and ownership.
Routing details show engine credential readiness, symbolic profile chains, output
limits, optional execution fingerprints, and concrete inventory provenance.
@kbd{RET} selects a workflow or reconstructs a stored run.  @kbd{h} opens the
selected workflow's bounded runner help.  @kbd{q} exits only when this process owns
no live child.

Workflow values are collected in descriptor order.  @kbd{RET} inserts a newline and
@kbd{C-d} accepts the exact current value.  Bracketed paste is enabled when Vty
reports terminal support.  Printable characters remain editor data while an editor
has focus.  Within workflow configuration, @kbd{ESC} returns to the preceding input
and preserves the current draft.  From the first input it cancels configuration and
returns to the browser.  @kbd{p} cycles the available routing personas.  Scripted and
routing-only targets use @kbd{s} and @kbd{l}.  An engine whose routing inspection
reports @samp{NOT READY} cannot enter launch review.  @samp{READY (offline)} records
local configuration readiness, not acceptance of the selected model by a live provider.

Launch review first strictly decodes the exact post-input plan and verifies its
workflow identity against the selected catalogue row.  The compact view shows the
workflow, target, persona, request bounds, path count, effect capabilities, working
directory, routing warning count, live billing warning, and profile chains whose names
are exact members of the post-input pins.  @kbd{d} opens a scrollable exact-details
view with the complete warnings first, followed by all plan fields, including the
level, size, ask count, and fold histogram,
runner executable, direct argv, routing provenance, launch fingerprint, and execution
fingerprints when supplied.  Input bodies and the raw program do not appear in either
view.  @kbd{RET} or @kbd{y}
confirms, and @kbd{n} returns to target selection with the input state intact.
Confirmation is enabled only when every required review row and the complete launch,
back, and exact-detail controls fit the current terminal cells.  Resizing does not
alter selection, editor contents, pane focus, detail mode, or pending consent.  Launch
requires full pin coverage, repeats offline routing-only resolution, and refuses a
changed CLI-owned fingerprint.  Restarts, resumes, and forks retain the billing
classification of the restored target.

A confirmed run uses protocol version 2, store format 2, local person answering,
and a control pipe on file descriptor 3.  Its context line shows workflow, persona,
concrete realization, human-readable status, elapsed time, follow state, and known
bills.  Terminal elapsed time ends at the last recorded event.  @kbd{d} opens the
complete run diagnostic and run identifier.  A run-level failure appears above the
occurrence panes, even when no request started; stored-run details begin with the same
failure.  Machine callbacks remain gated until the frontend adopts process ownership,
and the monitor is entered only after a valid @code{run.started} event.  Startup and
protocol failures retain ownership until process-group termination and reap complete.
The request rail occupies at most one quarter of a wide terminal, capped at 32
columns.  Its rows show request number, lifecycle, and addressee.  The reading pane
shows the answer or the latest attempt output with paragraph spacing.  Full prompts,
earlier attempts, public progress, routing history, and control acknowledgements
remain available in @kbd{d} Details.
@kbd{TAB} changes pane focus.  @kbd{UP} and @kbd{DOWN} move the occurrence selection
when the list is focused and scroll output when the output pane is focused.
@kbd{j} and @kbd{k} also move the occurrence selection.  Manual output scrolling
sets the visible state to @samp{Paused}, while @kbd{G} or @kbd{END} resumes tail
following.

@kbd{ESC} detaches without cancelling and reattaches from the run browser.  On a
live run outside an editor, @kbd{c} opens a cancellation confirmation.  Active attempts accept
immediate and next-boundary steering with @kbd{i} and @kbd{b}.  The bounded steering
editor appears beneath the run view, keeping output visible during composition.
Recovery uses
@kbd{r}, @kbd{f}, and @kbd{a}, and numbered choices select pre-dispatch redirects.
Person questions and recoveries share one FIFO ordered by machine protocol sequence.
Input, filter, person, steering, and save editors retain separate drafts when a
mandatory decision preempts another editor.  In a person-answer editor, printable
characters including @kbd{c}, @kbd{q}, and @kbd{?} are answer text, @kbd{C-d}
submits, and @kbd{ESC} opens cancellation confirmation.  After result
verification, @kbd{r} shows the bounded result pane on demand.  @kbd{s} opens an
explicit absolute-path prompt which copies the JSON value and a final line feed
to a new mode-0600 file.  The frontend writes the bytes to a private file in
the destination directory and publishes it with a hard link, so an existing
entry, including a symbolic link or a dangling symbolic link, is never replaced
and a failed save leaves no new file.  When the private file cannot be removed
after the link, the save succeeds and the status line names that file.  @kbd{PGUP} and @kbd{PGDN} scroll save errors.

The monitor renders answer output separately from bounded public message, tool,
todo, context-usage, and explicit reasoning-summary updates.  ACP thought chunks
are not reasoning summaries and are not retained.  Plain text, lightweight Markdown
line cues, status classes, and unified-diff classes cover the implemented event data
without a native grammar library.  The color map keeps the terminal's default
background.  A nonempty @env{NO_COLOR} removes semantic foreground colors while
retaining textual focus, reverse-video selection, headings, warnings, and diff
prefixes.  The presentation boundary replaces terminal control characters in every
rendered string before Brick constructs Vty cells.

State is private beneath @env{AGENT_CAT_STATE_DIR} when that variable is set to an
absolute path.  An existing root must be a no-follow directory owned by the effective
user and must expose no group or other mode bits.  A symlink, foreign owner, or public
mode is refused rather than repaired.  The frontend retains the validated root
descriptor and performs descendant mutation through no-follow descriptor-relative
operations.  Plan and machine children receive @env{AGENT_CAT_STATE_ANCHOR}, a
captured path, device, and inode identity that they validate before accessing inputs
or creating a runtime store.  Runtime stores retain their own descriptors, including
during atomic artifact publication.
Without it, terminal runs use
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/agent-cat/tui/RUNNER-ID}, with @file{~/.local/state} as
fallback.  Runner defaults and ext-pi's default are distinct; sharing is therefore
explicit.  Live foreign owners are read-only; stale owners are orphaned.  Restart,
resume, and fork create new stores and preserve the runner's compatibility checks.
The supported terminal platforms are macOS and Linux.  The repository records the executable requirement
matrix and platform evidence in @file{doc/tui-release-evidence.md}.

@section Verbs

@table @code
@c COVER CLI verb:list author
@item list [--json]
Print every registered name and its one-line description in registry order.
JSON form emits an array of static fact objects.  No Program is executed.

@c COVER CLI verb:help author
@item help [NAME]
With no name, print general usage.  With one name, build the row at empty inputs
and print its computed facts and help page.  @code{NAME --help} is byte-identical.

@c COVER CLI verb:plan author
@item plan NAME [options]
Build the Program and print its static level, size, ask nodes, code sequence
when one exists, and cost summary.  @code{--raw} adds the emitted first-order
Program, and @code{--json} selects the machine object.

@c COVER CLI verb:cost author
@item cost NAME [inputs]
Print the cost summary and every folded syntactic path.  Machine-readable cost
data is available through @code{plan --json}, and @code{cost} accepts no JSON
option.

@c COVER CLI verb:run author
@item run NAME [target] [options]
Build, preflight, and execute the Program against one scripted target, one
explicit live route table with a default, or fully covered configured routes
without a default.  The trace is printed as the run progresses, followed by
result and bills.

@c COVER CLI verb:machine author
@item machine RUN_ID NAME target [options]
Execute the same Program through the durable runner while emitting strict
versioned NDJSON events on standard output.  Omission selects protocol version
1; an explicit version 2 writes store format 2 and a verified result artifact.
Workflow text uses standard input; correlated controls use the inherited
descriptor named by @code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_FD} (fd 3 in Pi).  The private
store and owner metadata use the remaining documented @code{AGENT_CAT_*}
environment variables.  Human prompt narration is omitted from machine
diagnostics.  @code{manifest.json} references a separate mode-0600
@code{program.json}, keeping input-expanded text out of the manifest while
preserving exact lineage validation.

@c COVER CLI verb:lineage-check author
@item lineage-check OPERATION PARENT_STORE NAME target [options]
Validate restart, resume, or fork compatibility without a child run.  Resume
requires a compatible checkpoint, and restart and fork do not.

@c COVER CLI verb:machine-restart author
@c COVER CLI verb:machine-resume author
@c COVER CLI verb:machine-fork author
@item machine-restart|machine-resume|machine-fork RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME target [options]
Create a durable child run with an immutable parent link.  Restart inherits no
answers.  Resume uses an exact compatible checkpoint.  Fork validates any
explicit edits and then reuses the matching complete answers.

@c COVER CLI verb:flow author
@c COVER CLI --follow author
@c COVER CLI --from author
@item flow PATH... [--follow] [--route PREDICATE] [--from CURSOR]
Read run logs and manager logs and verify them.  Each @code{PATH} names a run
store directory or its @file{flow.ndjson}, a manager log file, or the
@file{flow} directory of a manager private root, which stands for every
@file{.ndjson} file in it.  With one run log the command reads that log alone,
as this entry first describes.  The command opens the store as the
account that owns it and refuses a store that fails the private-root ownership
and mode checks.  It writes one JSON object for each record on standard
output, in log order.  Each object holds the @code{position}, @code{schema},
@code{from}, @code{to}, @code{about}, @code{replyTo} and @code{at} fields of the
record and a @code{problems} list.  An inline body appears as @code{body}.  A
claim check appears as @code{claim} with its SHA-256 and size, and @code{body}
then holds the value of the claim-check file after its size, digest and exact
encoding verify.  An event record carries @code{event} with its sequence
number and the joined line of @file{events.ndjson}.

The command reads each complete line with the frame bound of the runtime
protocol and decodes it with the strict line codec.  It reports a final line
without its newline by its size and does not decode it.  It decodes each body
with the codec of its schema.  It verifies that each reply names an earlier
ask of a schema that the reply may answer, and that no other reply names the
same ask.  It verifies that the first record is @code{start} and that each
event record has its line in @file{events.ndjson}.

After the records, the command writes one summary object with the number of
records, the verification result and its failures, the torn final line, the
position of the stop and the states of the log.  The stop is the first event
record whose event ends the run.  The states are the asks without a reply, in
flight in a live log, uncertain in an ended log without its stop and
unanswered at the stop in an ended log with its stop, the controls without a
later acknowledgement event, the recovery waits without a later choice, the
person-answer waits without a later answer, the effect starts in
@file{effects.ndjson} without a completion, lost supervision when an ended log
has no stop, and each ask after the stop.  A killed run therefore leaves its
question uncertain even when its engine start has a reply.  A run that races a
cancel can leave an ask unanswered at the stop.  The command exits 1 when a
verification fails.  When every verification passes, it exits 2 when the
ended log has no stop, and 0 otherwise.  The other states do not change the
exit status.  A store that the command cannot open exits 3.

@code{--route} prints only the records that match a conjunction of
@code{field=value} terms separated by commas.  The fields are @code{schema},
@code{from}, @code{to}, @code{request}, @code{managerRun}, @code{nativeRun},
@code{occurrence}, @code{epoch}, @code{attempt} and @code{command}.  A sender
or an address is written as @code{manager}, @code{public},
@code{local:UID}, @code{credential:CLIENT:CREDENTIAL}, @code{model:TARGET},
@code{tool:NAME}, @code{adapter:NAME}, @code{workflow:RUN} or
@code{approvers:PROFILE}.  @code{--from} names the first position to print.
The route and the start position select only the printed records, and the
summary always covers the whole log.  Without @code{--follow} the command
reads the log once as an ended log.  With @code{--follow} it reads the log as a
live log every 250 milliseconds and prints each record once its line is
complete, and each event record once its line of @file{events.ndjson} exists.
It stops at the stop, reads the log once more as an ended log and writes the
summary.  An interrupt ends the command without a summary.

With a manager log, or with more than one log, the command reads every log
once as an ended log and joins them.  The command refuses @code{--follow}
with more than one @code{PATH} as a usage error, and it exits 3 when
@code{--follow} names a manager log.  It opens
each manager log through its flow directory as the account that owns it,
reads its sealed segments in @file{sealed/<stream>/} in start order and then
the file that the operand names, verifies each line as it verifies a run log,
and decodes each body with the manager codec of its schema.  Each record of a
manager log has its position in the whole log, sealed segments included.
The pruner of the manager removes old sealed segments, and the retained
floor is the position of the first record that remains.  A reply whose ask
lies below the floor carries @code{"pruned": true}, and the command does not
check it against its ask.  It writes the records of the manager logs and
then those of the run logs, and each record object also names its
@code{log}.  The route and the start position apply to each log.

The summary object names each log with its kind and its size, each manager
log with its @code{floor} and the positions of its pruned replies as
@code{prunedReplies}, and each run log with its own summary.  It joins a review to the approve and discard
commands and the review endings of its preparation, a start or control relay
to the @code{start} or @code{control} of the run log with its native run and
command identifiers, a control to its acknowledgement event, a command to its
reply and its later command notices, and a person answer of a run log to the
answer command with its command identifier.  Its states are the commands
without a reply, which are undecided, the start and control relays without
their record in a run log that was read, which are unresolved deliveries, the
reviews without a command or an ending, which are pending, and each lifetime
without its shutdown notice.  The summary states that the Store ledger is the
authority on commands, reviews and requests.

For each start relay the summary verifies the consent.  The manager log must
hold, in this order, the review, the approve command from a credential that
names the selectors of the review, its accepted receipt or a gap notice that
names that receipt, and the relay.  The SHA-256 of the review bytes must equal
the @code{reviewSha256} of the binding, the SHA-256 of the binding bytes must
equal the digest that the approve command names, the binding must name the
native run of the relay and carry no credential in its arguments, and a run
log that was read must begin with a @code{start} for that native run.  When
the floor of the manager log is above 0 and the review or the approve command
is not in the retained records, that record lies below the floor.  The
consent is then pruned: the checks of the retained records still apply, and
a pruned consent with no failed check has @code{"verified": false} and
@code{"pruned": true} and is not a failure.  A join whose counterpart lies
below the floor has none and is not a failure either.  The
command exits 1 when a verification or a consent fails.  When every
verification passes, it exits 2 when a run log has no stop or a lifetime has no
shutdown notice, and 0 otherwise.  To verify the consent of a start relay, the
command therefore needs the run store of that run among its operands.

@end table

@section Top-level routing modes

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --routing author
@item --routing [--json] [--persona NAME] [--offline | --refresh-models]
Load the conventional routing files and print the resolved policy without an
engine or a run store.  Human output names the persona, axes, aliases, exact model
identifiers, engines, and inventory source.  JSON also carries the available
persona and model choices, credential-readiness booleans, profile rungs, cache
ages and timestamps, warnings, endpoint fingerprints, and an opaque routing-only
launch with its policy fingerprint.  It omits secret names
and values, environment and header data, raw endpoint URLs, and unselected
inventories.  Inside live run options, the same spelling explicitly names the
routing target, but omission means the same thing when no engine or session was
given.  An explicit engine or session takes precedence over this flag.

Each catalogue warning appears once for each engine, with the engine name.  The
warning @code{tls-not-supported} adds its remedy: remove the https catalogue or
use a loopback http catalogue.  An engine whose secret environment variable is
unset is reported with @code{credentialReady} false, and the human output names
the unset secret.  The inspection still exits 0, and a launch that needs that
engine is refused.  When no routing file exists, the command exits 0 and names
the expected user file and the example file
@file{cli/model-definitions.example.yaml}.  The same holds with
@code{--offline}.  With @code{--json}, it prints the empty schema-version-1
inspection with the additional fields @code{userFile} and @code{exampleFile}.

@c COVER CLI verb:adapter-options author
@item adapter-options --adapter ADAPTER [--adapter-arg ARG ...] [--json]
Start one ACP adapter, open one session, print the model and effort values that
it offers, and exit 0.  No prompt is sent.  A failure to start the adapter or to
complete the handshake exits 2.  The values are those that @code{--model} and
@code{--effort} accept.

@c COVER CLI --migrate-routing author
@c COVER CLI --output author
@item --migrate-routing SOURCE --output DESTINATION
Decode one complete schema-version-1 file and create an offline version-2 user
file exclusively.  The output has one engine per router, one exact model alias
per router and model pair, one @code{default} persona, and equivalent profile
chains.  The source and every existing destination are refused rather than
overwritten.
@end table

@section Workflow manager service

@cindex workflow manager service
@cindex local administration
@cindex two-configuration rule
Four forms of @command{agentic-run} create, serve and administer the workflow
manager.  The runner reads these forms before every other form, and each form
takes exactly the words that the synopsis shows:

@example
agentic-run --manager init --root DIR [--port PORT]
agentic-run --manager add-client --config FILE --profile-file FILE [--profile ID ...]
agentic-run --manager serve --config FILE [--legacy-history ROOT=PROFILE ...]
agentic-run --manager admin --config FILE
@end example

@var{FILE} is the absolute path of a private version-1 operator configuration
file, which @code{loadManagerConfiguration} reads.  @file{manager/CONFIGURATION.md}
documents its format, @file{doc/api/README.md} specifies the @code{/v1} routes
that the manager serves, and @file{manager/README.md} describes the owners of
the manager.  Each refusal line below starts with the name of the executable
and a colon.

@table @code
@item --manager init --root DIR [--port PORT]
@cindex manager root creation
Create a manager root for the executable that runs the command.  @var{DIR} is
an absolute directory that does not exist or is empty, and @var{PORT} is the
HTTPS port on @code{127.0.0.1}, 8443 by default.  The command creates the
directories @file{manager}, @file{admin}, @file{workspace}, @file{tls},
@file{bin} and @file{client} with mode 0700, links @file{bin/agentic-run} to
the executable, and writes @file{serve.json} and @file{offline.json} from the
reference pair @file{doc/examples/manager-serve.json} and
@file{doc/examples/manager-offline.json}, which are data files of the package.
The canonical path of @var{DIR} replaces the placeholder root, and @var{PORT}
replaces the port in @code{port} and in @code{allowedHosts}.  The command then
makes a self-signed certificate for @code{127.0.0.1} and its key with the
@command{openssl} of @code{PATH}, validates @file{offline.json} offline,
issues one credential for every configured profile offline, and writes the
client profile @file{client/profile.json}.  Every written file has mode 0600.
The command prints the commands that start the manager, ask for its status,
stop it, and connect the TUI, Pi and Emacs.  An existing root that is not
empty, a relative root, a port outside 1 to 65535, a root whose
@file{admin/admin.sock} path exceeds the 103-byte Unix socket address limit
of macOS, or a failed step exits with status 1 and a line that starts with
@samp{manager init:}.

@item --manager add-client --config FILE --profile-file FILE [--profile ID @dots{}]
Issue one credential through the local authority that the configuration file
selects, and write its client profile.  The credential file is the profile
file with the extension @file{.credential} in place of @file{.json}.  Each
@option{--profile} names a configured profile, and without the option the
credential covers every configured profile.  The scopes are @code{observe},
@code{submit}, @code{control} and @code{export}.  An existing profile file or
an unknown profile identifier exits with status 1 and a line that starts with
@samp{manager add-client:}.

@item --manager serve --config FILE [--legacy-history ROOT=PROFILE @dots{}]
Start the manager in the foreground.  The command loads the configuration,
opens the coordination Store under @code{managerRoot}, opens the HTTPS
listener of the @code{https} section, and serves until it stops.  When the
listener is bound, it writes the line @samp{manager listening on
https://HOST:PORT/v1} to standard output.  When the
configuration names @code{administrationRoot}, the manager also serves the
local administration channel through a Unix socket in that private directory.

Each @option{--legacy-history} option binds the configured local retention
root @var{ROOT} to the configured profile @var{PROFILE}.  @var{ROOT} is an
absolute path that appears once, and @var{PROFILE} follows the last equals
sign.  @code{/v1/runs} then also lists the legacy run entries of the bound
roots with @code{observer} supervision.  Without the option, the run
collection lists managed runs only.

A termination signal, or a @code{shutdown} request through the local channel,
stops the manager.  Each owned run is then cancelled with its cleanup, the
open event and route streams end, the manager log receives the shutdown
notice, and the process exits with status 0.  A keyboard interrupt performs
the same cleanup and ends the process as an interrupted command.

An option other than @option{--legacy-history}, a relative or repeated
@var{ROOT}, or an empty @var{PROFILE} exits with status 1 and the line
@samp{manager serve takes only --legacy-history ROOT=PROFILE options with
distinct absolute roots}.  A relative @var{FILE} exits with status 1 and the
line @samp{manager --config requires an absolute file}.  An invalid
configuration, a binding that the configuration refuses, or a listener that
cannot open exits with status 1 and the line @samp{manager configuration or
HTTPS listener is unavailable}.  For a configuration file that the loader
refuses, the line continues with the cause, and a path with a symbolic-link
component names that component.  A certificate or key file that the
private-file rule refuses exits with status 1 and a line that names
@code{https.certificateFile} or @code{https.keyFile}, the file, and the rule.  Any other failure of the service exits with
status 2 and the line @samp{manager service is unavailable}.  A Store whose
schema version is newer than 12 is such a failure.

@item --manager admin --config FILE
Read one JSON request of at most 2 MiB from standard input until end of file,
perform one local administration operation, and write one JSON line to
standard output.  An answer with @code{"ok": true} exits with status 0.  A
refusal has the answer @code{"ok": false} with a fixed error code, and it
exits with status 1.  A malformed request, an unknown field, or an unknown
operation is refused before dispatch, and its answer has
@code{"operation": null}.  The @code{message} of a refusal is empty.  A
refusal whose cause the operator can act on also writes one line that starts
with @samp{manager admin:} to standard error.  The line names the cause, for
example that no manager serves on the @code{administrationRoot}, a
symbolic-link component of the configuration path, or a profile identifier of
@code{issue-credential} that the configuration does not define.  Local
administration has no HTTP equivalent.
@end table

The configuration file selects the channel of an administration request.  The
operator therefore keeps two configuration files for one @code{managerRoot}.
The serve configuration names @code{administrationRoot}.  The manager serves
with it, and @samp{--manager admin} with it sends the request to the serving
manager through the local channel.  The offline configuration is the same
file without @code{administrationRoot}.  @samp{--manager admin} with it
acquires the configuration lease and opens the Store itself, so it serves
only while no manager serves.  A configured @code{administrationRoot} is
authoritative even when no manager serves.  A request through the serve
configuration then refuses with @code{storage-unavailable} and never falls
back to the offline path.  An offline request while a manager serves refuses
with @code{storage-unavailable}, because the serving manager holds the
configuration lease.  When one file changes, the operator makes the same
change in the other file.

The local administration protocol has thirteen operations.  Each one is
implemented, and the channel decides its effect.  The table names the effect
through the serve configuration while a manager serves, and through the
offline configuration while no manager serves.

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .39 .39
@headitem Operation @tab Serving manager @tab No manager serves
@item @code{status}
@tab The current facts of the lifetime, with @code{state} @code{serving} or
@code{draining}.
@tab Opens the Store as a restart does and answers with @code{state}
@code{stopped}.
@item @code{check-store}
@tab The integrity of the Store and at most 256 quarantine identities.
@tab The same facts from the stopped Store.
@item @code{check-quarantine}
@tab The cleanup state of one quarantined reservation, with cleanup evidence
when it is clean.
@tab The same answer from the stopped Store.
@item @code{release-quarantine}
@tab Frees the slot and resources of a quarantined reservation whose cleanup
evidence is verified again.
@tab The same release in the stopped Store.
@item @code{list-credentials}
@tab At most 256 credential records with their metadata.
@tab The same records from the stopped Store.
@item @code{issue-credential}
@tab Creates a client and its credential and writes the bearer to a new
private output file.
@tab The same issuance in the stopped Store.
@item @code{rotate-credential}
@tab Creates the successor credential, with an overlap of at most 60 seconds.
@tab The same rotation in the stopped Store.
@item @code{revoke-credential}
@tab Revokes a credential, which then receives 401 at its next check.
@tab The same revocation in the stopped Store.
@item @code{reload-profiles}
@tab Loads the configuration again and installs its profiles with new
revisions.
@tab Validates the given file and opens no Store.
@item @code{drain}
@tab Stops the admission of new work and lets the started runs continue.
@tab Refuses with @code{state-conflict}, because no lifetime serves.
@item @code{shutdown}
@tab Answers @code{stopped} and then stops the manager as a termination signal
does.
@tab Answers @code{stopped} and changes nothing.
@item @code{backup}
@tab Refuses with @code{state-conflict}.
@tab Copies the stopped Store into a new private directory.
@item @code{restore}
@tab Refuses with @code{state-conflict}.
@tab Replaces the stopped Store with a backup after a check of the fencing
evidence, rotates the authority epoch and the stream identity, and revokes
every credential.
@end multitable

The operator runbook @file{manager/OPERATIONS.md} gives the procedure for each
operation with its preconditions, the exact request body, the expected answer,
the diagnostics, and the failure answers.  It also covers manager-log
pruning, quarantine release, disk pressure, lost replies, a corrupt Store, the
restoration of an older backup, and rollback to explicit local clients.  The
section @samp{Command-line boundary} of @file{doc/api/README.md} states the
fields and the answers of each operation.

A terminal client of the manager is @samp{agentic-run --tui --service
@var{CLIENT_PROFILE} @dots{}}, and @samp{agentic-run --tui --local} or
@samp{agentic-run --tui} selects the local backend.  The section
@samp{Terminal frontend} of this chapter describes both selections.


@section Common inspection options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --help author
@item --help
Alone, print usage at exit 0.  After a row name, print that row's page.  After
@option{--manager}, print the usage lines of the four @option{--manager} forms
at exit 0.  It is not accepted as an option to other verb forms.

@c COVER CLI --version author
@item --version
Print the name of the runner and the version of its package, for example
@samp{agentic-run 0.1.0.0}, at exit 0.

@c COVER CLI --json author
@item --json
Machine rendering for @code{list}, @code{plan}, and @code{--routing}.  With
@code{plan --raw}, add the @code{program} key.  Help, cost, and run have no JSON
form; refusals remain prose on standard error.

@c COVER CLI --descriptor-version author
@item --descriptor-version 3
With @code{list --json}, request descriptor version 3.  It advertises protocol
and store versions 1 and 2, local person answering, and sanitized routing
inspection while the unqualified command retains descriptor version 2.

@c COVER CLI --raw author
@item --raw
With @code{plan}, print or include the emitted @code{RawProgram}.  It can be
combined with @code{--json} and with input flags in any order.

@c COVER CLI --require-pinned author
@item --require-pinned
With @code{plan} or @code{run}, refuse unless every model question names a
primary serving model.  Checked before output, process startup, or spending.
@end table

@section Input options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --input author
@item --input FILE
Supply the sole operator input of a Program that declares exactly one.  The path
is not parsed as @samp{NAME=FILE}, so an equals sign remains part of the path.

@c COVER CLI --input-file author
@item --input-file NAME=FILE
Supply a named operator input from UTF-8 file contents.  It can repeat.
Invalid UTF-8 is refused.  An ordinary prompt input strips one trailing
newline.  Declared command-tail and stdin inputs preserve the exact decoded
text.

@c COVER CLI --input-arg author
@item --input-arg NAME=VALUE
Supply a named operator input inline.  It can repeat.  The value can contain
equals signs, because the parse splits at the first one.
@end table

Plan, cost, and run accept the input flags.  A Program with no operator inputs
refuses them.  A valid @code{run.} fact name is also refused, because the runner
supplies it.  An unknown reserved name is an authoring error.  During
@code{run}, machine, and machine-lineage commands, an unbound stdin declaration
reads descriptor 0 to end of file.  Plan and cost never read stdin
automatically.

Machine supervisors set @code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_FD} to an inherited descriptor
of at least 3; Pi uses fd 3.  Control NDJSON and workflow input therefore coexist
without framing.  The compatibility form @code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_STDIN=1} puts
controls on standard input instead; it works only when the stdin-designated
value was supplied explicitly, and both control modes together are refused.

@section Frontend capability discovery

@c COVER CLI --capabilities author

The @code{frontend --capabilities} command writes one UTF-8 JSON object followed
by an LF and exits at zero.  It does not read standard input, inspect a workflow,
load routing or provider configuration, open state, or perform a managed write.
The response has @code{version: 1}, @code{operation: "capabilities"}, and the
following records.

@table @code
@item server
This record contains @code{runnerId}, the actual registry binary name,
@code{executable}, the value returned by @code{getExecutablePath}, and
@code{runnerVersion}, the package version derived from
@code{Paths_agentic.version}.  Workflow descriptors use the same version source.

@item session
This record advertises session version 1, the @code{prepare},
@code{prepare-lineage}, @code{start}, and @code{discard} operations, the
@code{literal}, @code{file}, and @code{transport} input sources, invocation
version 1, and a @code{maxRequestBytes} value of 2097152.

@item io
This record advertises version 1 and the @code{open-root},
@code{read-question}, @code{read-result}, @code{list-runs}, and @code{read-run}
operations.

@item export
This record advertises version 1, the @code{export-result} operation,
@code{format: "result-json"}, and @code{destination: "state-exports"}.
@end table

The top-level @code{frontendManifestVersions} value is @code{[2, 3]}.
The separate @code{legacyFrontendManifests: true} field reports support for
unversioned manifests.  It does not authorize an explicit manifest version 1.
A client checks the document version and every operation, version, and input
source that it needs.  Failed, malformed, or incomplete discovery does not
authorize a fallback, and a descriptor version or executable name is not a
substitute for this response.  The command is represented by
@code{Agentic.Cli.Command.FrontendCapabilities}.

@section Native frontend file access

@c COVER CLI verb:frontend-io author

The @code{frontend-io} command reads one versioned JSON request from standard
input through end of file and writes one JSON response followed by a newline.
It opens existing private roots and verifies referenced artifacts without
creating a run, reading routing configuration, or acquiring live controls.
Successful requests exit at zero.  Invalid requests and failed verification
exit at three with diagnostics on standard error and no JSON response.

Every request contains a supported @code{version} and an @code{operation}
string.  Unknown operations, versions, and top-level fields are refused.  The
request limit is 2 MiB, including the encoded root identity and artifact
reference.  Responses are bounded by 64 MiB plus 4096 bytes, including the
terminating newline.  The following operations require @code{version: 1}.

@table @code
@item open-root
The request supplies an absolute @code{path} naming an existing directory.
The directory must satisfy the runtime's private-root ownership and permission
checks.  The response contains @code{rootIdentity}, an opaque string that the
client retains unchanged.  It identifies the directory rather than granting
authority.  A missing root is refused rather than created.

@item read-question
The request supplies @code{rootIdentity}, @code{runId}, @code{occurrenceId},
@code{codeName}, and @code{reference}.  The occurrence identifier is a decimal
Word64 string of at most 20 digits.  The reference has the existing
@code{QuestionRef} fields.
The response contains the run and occurrence identifiers, @code{intent}, and
the full @code{question} value.

@item read-result
The request supplies @code{rootIdentity}, @code{runId}, and @code{reference}
with the existing @code{ResultRef} fields.  The response contains the run
identifier, the verified @code{code}, and the result @code{value}.

@item list-runs
The request supplies @code{rootIdentity}.  The response contains @code{runs},
a bounded catalogue of summary records and visible corrupt entries.  A summary
contains the run, runner, workflow, directory, creation time, lineage, persona,
ownership classification, and compact snapshot status and billing fields.
@code{resultReferenceAvailable} reports a recorded reference, not verified
result content.  Every healthy summary also contains @code{invocation}.  Its
value is null for an unversioned or version-2 manifest and is the retained
invocation record for a version-3 manifest.

@item read-run
The request supplies @code{rootIdentity} and @code{runId}.  The response contains
@code{run}, with the frontend manifest, sanitized runtime policy, ownership
classification, and public runtime presentation snapshot.  This presentation
is not a restoration checkpoint.  No caller-supplied owner identifier is
accepted, so these queries cannot report control ownership for their caller.
@end table

Every successful response contains its operation's version and the requested
operation.  Artifact requests reopen and verify the captured root identity,
then traverse @file{runs/RUN_ID/runtime} relative to retained directory
descriptors.  Ancestor and leaf symlinks, nonregular files, mismatched identity,
size, digest, code, and noncanonical artifact bytes are refused.

The client obtains each expected reference and code name from its validated
event stream or restored snapshot.  It supplies that reference unchanged rather
than computing a new digest from the file it wants to read.  Artifact queries
verify those expectations independently of journal replay and do not establish
store health, decision eligibility, or control ownership.  Catalogue and
@code{read-run} queries separately validate and replay the runtime journal.
A torn or changing journal can refuse inspection while previously authenticated
artifact references remain independently readable.  Catalogue corruption does
not hide healthy siblings.  Input bytes are authenticated separately when
preparing lineage.

@subheading Restorable observations and answer editors

Frontend capabilities advertise IO versions 1 and 2.  Version 2 supports only
@code{read-run-checkpoint} and @code{read-question-schema}, while version 1
retains the operations and replies above.  Neither version substitutes a
compact catalogue for a complete restoration representation.

@table @code
@item read-run-checkpoint
The request supplies @code{rootIdentity} and @code{runId}.  The response contains
@code{run}, with @code{runId}, @code{directory}, @code{manifest}, @code{ownership},
@code{policy}, and a nullable @code{checkpoint}.  Missing runtime evidence and
an empty journal yield null, while corrupt or torn stores refuse inspection.
The record and retained envelopes come from one verified store read.

@item read-question-schema
The request supplies @code{rootIdentity}, @code{runId}, @code{occurrenceId}, and
the original @code{QuestionRef} in @code{reference}.  It requires no client code
name.  The response contains the run and occurrence identifiers, @code{intent},
the full @code{question}, @code{codeName}, and @code{answerSchema}.  Private
artifact verification binds the actual stored code bytes, and the planning
layer derives the editor schema from the decoded code witness.
@end table

A checkpoint is an object with exactly @code{checkpointVersion: 1},
@code{representation: "runtime-envelope-prefix"}, @code{runId},
@code{protocolVersion}, @code{lastSequence}, and @code{envelopes}.  It retains
every original envelope value from sequence zero through the declared boundary.
For an empty prefix, the protocol and sequence are null.  A nonempty prefix has
one supported protocol and a canonical decimal sequence string.  Decoding
validates each envelope with the existing protocol codecs, replays
@code{stepRunSnapshot}, and verifies the declared boundary.  The whole last
envelope survives, including the timestamp and payload used to distinguish
exact and conflicting duplicate delivery.

The complete encoded checkpoint is limited to 64 MiB, including metadata and
array delimiters, and each encoded envelope is limited to 1 MiB.  Capture and
suffix replay refuse overflow without returning a partial checkpoint.  The
helper also limits the journal read to 64 MiB before allocating its contents,
while existing store readers retain their 512 MiB bound.  The final reply has
its separate 64-MiB-plus-4096-byte limit.  These are byte bounds, not total heap
bounds.  Arbitrary edited snapshot records and histories beyond the advertised
bounds are outside this representation.

The editor schema describes person and control answers accepted by
@code{answerFromJson}.  Structured numbers are objects with exactly an integer
@code{numerator} and a positive integer @code{denominator}, recursively through
arrays and records.  Model-response JSON numbers remain unchanged.  Text is a
string, flag is Boolean, and receipt is null.  Verdict objects retain the
decoder's acceptance of extra members and ignored objections for approval and
decline.  Observation and public Ack names use @code{receipt}, while authoring
syntax uses @code{ack}.  The observation adapter refuses the authoring spelling.
Runtime byte limits and decoding remain authoritative.

Catalogue enumeration refuses child 1001, counting corrupt entries, symlinks,
and non-run children.  It does not return a successful truncated catalogue.
Artifact reads remain independent of journal health.  None of these
representations establishes control authority, publication durability, or an
atomic observation across ownership files, manifests, and the journal.

The Haskell facade exposes @code{runFrontendQuery} and
@code{maxFrontendQueryBytes}.  @code{withPrivateRootIdentity} supplies the same
captured-root validation used by the existing process-boundary state anchor.
The command is represented by @code{Agentic.Cli.Command.FrontendIo}.

@section Managed verified result export

@c COVER CLI verb:frontend-export author
@c COVER CLI --state author

The @code{frontend-export --state STATE} command is a separate mutating
interface.  @code{STATE} is mandatory, must be an absolute bounded path, and
comes from the client's current trusted configuration.  The directory must
already exist and satisfy the private-root ownership and mode checks.  The
command reads one JSON request through end of file.

@example
@{
  "version": 1,
  "operation": "export-result",
  "rootIdentity": "CAPTURED_ROOT_IDENTITY",
  "runId": "RUN_ID",
  "reference": @{@dots{} complete ResultRef fields @dots{}@},
  "name": "result.json"
@}
@end example

The request is strict and bounded at 2 MiB.  The root identity must exactly
name the path, device, and inode opened from the trusted @code{--state}
argument.  The result reference is the complete existing @code{ResultRef}
obtained from a validated event or snapshot.  The exporter verifies its fixed
path, size, digest, run identifier, canonical artifact encoding, and result
code through retained directory descriptors.  It captures the verified value
once and does not require fresh journal replay, establish whole-store health,
or acquire control ownership.

The name is one nonempty filename component of at most 255 UTF-8 bytes.  It
cannot be @file{.} or @file{..} and cannot contain a slash, backslash, NUL,
ASCII control character, or delete character.  The only destination is
@file{STATE/exports/NAME}.  There is no request field for another destination,
file body, overwrite mode, or client-side fallback write.

The published bytes are compact JSON containing exactly @code{code} and
@code{value}, followed by one LF.  Both fields retain their original JSON types,
and the complete sequence cannot exceed 67108864 bytes.  Publication creates or
validates an owned mode-0700 @file{exports} directory, writes through a unique
mode-0600 exclusive temporary file, and commits atomically without replacing an
existing file, directory, or symbolic link.  Concurrent requests for one name
therefore have at most one success.  A temporary file left by a dead process
does not reserve the final name.

A successful response reports version 1, @code{operation: "export-result"}, the
run and filename, the fixed absolute path, a decimal-string byte count, a bare
lowercase SHA-256 digest of the published bytes, and the verified result code.
Validation and input or output failures exit at three with bounded diagnostics
and no success object.  Failure before publication leaves no final partial file.
A lost response after publication can leave a complete file, and retrying the
same name refuses rather than overwriting or deleting it.  A client reports
that outcome as uncertain.  The Haskell facade exposes
@code{runFrontendExport}, and the command is represented by
@code{Agentic.Cli.Command.FrontendExport}, while @code{frontend-io} remains
read-only.

@section Durable private capture publication

@findex publishPrivateCaptureAt
@findex privateCapturePath
@findex privateCaptureBytes
@findex privateCaptureSha256
The Haskell facade exposes @code{publishPrivateCaptureAt} as a separate storage
operation.  It takes a private root, relative path components, a nonnegative
byte limit, and an IO source of strict byte chunks.  An empty chunk ends the
stream.  Each chunk is bounded before writing, and the count and SHA-256 are
computed incrementally.  Source allocation and UTF-8 or typed validation remain
the caller's responsibility.

The root and its own ancestor namespace must already be durably provisioned.
The operation creates no directories.  It retains the directory chain below
that boundary, writes an exclusive mode-0600 temporary, flushes and synchronizes
its descriptor, closes it, and revalidates the retained parent identities.
It publishes through a no-clobber link and removes the temporary entry, then
synchronizes every directory from the containing parent back through the root
and checks their current bindings.  Each barrier calls @code{fsync}, followed
on macOS by @code{F_FULLFSYNC}.  A failed or unsupported barrier is not silently
downgraded.  Persistence remains conditional on the filesystem and hardware
honoring these calls, and ancestors above the root are outside this barrier.

@code{CapturePublished} carries the relative path, byte count, and SHA-256 as
a @code{PrivateCapture}.  @code{CaptureNotPublished} means this call did not
install its final name, rather than that no destination exists.
@code{CaptureUnconfirmed} means the name was published but a later barrier,
identity check, or cleanup failed.  It carries the capture metadata and IO
error, and the final name remains intact.  Cancellation, process death, or a
lost receipt requires reconciliation and never authorizes overwrite on retry.
Temporary files are not reclaimed by age alone, and no protection against
a process with the same user's filesystem privileges is implied.

This operation does not change the existing Store or frontend-export receipt
contracts.  It supplies neither a metadata transaction nor a manager service,
and its tests do not establish SQLite confinement or physical power-loss
behavior.

@section State-root ownership

@findex readStateRootRoleAt
@findex assertLocalStateRoot
@findex withLocalStateRoot
@findex establishManagerRootRole
A manager storage root has a private, owned, regular
@file{.agentic-root-role.json} file.  Its canonical version-1 bytes are
@code{@{"version":1,"role":"manager"@}} followed by one LF.
@code{readStateRootRoleAt} reads that exact marker through a retained directory
descriptor.  Absence yields @code{UnmarkedStateRoot}, while the canonical marker
yields @code{ManagerStateRoot}.  Malformed, noncanonical, inaccessible, linked,
or non-private markers refuse rather than becoming unmarked local state.

@code{assertLocalStateRoot} refuses manager markers in the observed directory
ancestry.  The walk requires readable directories and is bounded at 256 levels.
@code{withLocalStateRoot} checks each directory role while opening or creating
the local path through retained descriptors, then validates the resulting root
against the configured pathname.  The TUI uses this contract before startup,
discovery, catalogue refresh, preview, and launch.  Missing manifests do not
bypass the root-level refusal.  Generic Runtime access remains available to
manager code, and no existing frontend-IO request version is changed.

@code{establishManagerRootRole} requires a durably provisioned root.  It publishes
the marker only into an empty unmarked namespace with unmarked ancestry.
A matching existing marker is synchronized again without replacement, including
when its earlier publication was unconfirmed.  Failed barriers refuse success
and never remove the installed marker.  The role does not provide a service
lock, an ownership-transfer operation, or protection against arbitrary code with
the same OS authority.  Other local clients must adopt the role contract, and
older clients require disjoint configured directories.

@section Trusted profile library

@findex newRegistry
@findex reloadProfiles
@findex publicProfiles
@findex selectProfile
@findex probeProfile
@findex validateRootSeparation
@code{Agentic.Manager} provides a private profile registry and bounded trusted
runner discovery, with registry-based operator-file composition in the CLI.
The registry is not itself a manager service, and the section
@samp{Workflow manager service} describes the service that uses it.  Trusted
composition supplies
@code{OperatorProfile} values with an exact configured invocation, working
directory, target arguments, explicit environment, public labels, ownership
classification, quarantine state, person-answering mode, resource keys, and
a configuration-limits snapshot.
Historical manifests and process-reported identity do not grant that authority.

A successful reload validates the whole candidate set and atomically replaces
the registry.  Every profile receives a new opaque revision, and invalid
definitions leave the old snapshot intact.  Revisions use a random registry
namespace and a generation rather than hashes of secret values.  Selection and
probing check the current ID and revision, and unavailable profiles do not
supply a mutation selection.  Returned contexts are immutable values, not
approvals.  The owning coordinator must serialize approval with reload.

Discovery uses direct argument vectors and an explicit environment for
@code{frontend --capabilities} and @code{list --json --descriptor-version 3}.
Each query has a positive budget of at most 4 MiB per stream and 30 seconds.
Both streams are drained before the shared Runtime codecs validate the reply.
The registry lock covers both queries and their readiness update.  Runtime
cleanup preserves sole-reaper authority and can exceed the query deadline.
Public profiles contain only the frozen safe projection and fixed refusal
categories, never private execution fields or raw diagnostics.

@code{validateRootSeparation} refuses equal, nested, or observed aliased manager
and local-retention paths using canonical ancestry and directory identities.
It includes not-yet-created suffixes and revalidates the manager root.  It does
not freeze mount configuration or confer authority over later pathname IO.
Quota enforcement, service ownership, approval integration, and the remaining
clients are not supplied by this library alone.

@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.validateManagerTarget support
@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.validateManagerPreparedTarget support
@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.loadManagerConfiguration support
@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.openManagerConfiguration support
@c COVER API Agentic.Cli.reloadManagerConfiguration support
@findex readPrivateConfigurationFile
@findex validateManagerTarget
@findex validateManagerPreparedTarget
@findex loadManagerConfiguration
@findex openManagerConfiguration
@findex reloadManagerConfiguration
@code{loadManagerConfiguration} reads a private version-1 operator JSON file
through @code{readPrivateConfigurationFile}.  It rejects symlink components,
non-regular files, foreign ownership, public permissions, more than 2097152
bytes, duplicate decoded keys, more than 64 nested containers, and unknown or
invalid fields.  The required top-level fields are @code{version},
@code{managerRoot}, @code{localRetentionRoots}, @code{runners}, @code{limits},
and @code{profiles}.  The complete format is documented in
@file{manager/CONFIGURATION.md}.

The CLI supplies @code{validateManagerTarget}, which reuses the native target
grammar without a workflow lookup, and its existing credential-argument
predicate.  Every runner prefix is checked, including unused definitions.
Wrapper prefixes are not parsed as native target options.  Failures use fixed
diagnostic categories rather than source text or private argument values.

The captured @code{validateManagerPreparedTarget} function checks the original
worker's prepared target through the same CLI grammar without later routing-file
IO.  Arguments remain exact except for the native ACP resolver's final scratch
suffix, which must match that worker's private policy and cannot override an
explicit scratch choice.  The bound native response supplies program-dependent
resolution facts, not a free-standing policy value or stored profile identifier.

@code{openManagerConfiguration} acquires an exclusive native directory lease on
an existing, durably provisioned root before publishing its role.  Active
operations recheck that identity, existing role, and separation.
@code{reloadManagerConfiguration} requires the same root binding and never
recreates a lost marker.  Its masked transaction updates limits and profile
revisions together.  Closing an installed configuration retires the handle
before releasing its descriptors, not its role marker.  An active coordination
store retains a duplicate lease until its own scope ends.  Captured limits are
configuration facts, not per-profile allocations or quota enforcement.

@subsection Private coordination storage
@findex withCoordinationStore
@findex storeIdentity
@findex checkpointStore
@code{withCoordinationStore} brackets one SQLite store beneath an installed
configuration.  Only one store may claim that installation, and its lease
excludes distinct installations in the same process and other processes.
Lease descriptors close atomically on exec.  The public facade exposes scoped
composition, @code{storeIdentity}, and @code{checkpointStore}, not SQL, cursors,
connection handles, or worker authority.

Schema migration, initial durable identities, and schema-version publication
commit together.  Newer schemas refuse before journaling or schema changes.
Authority epoch and stream identity survive ordinary reopen, while process
generation is fresh for each lifetime.  Relational constraints retain historical
preparations while released reservations relinquish slots and resource claims.
The owning coordinator, not a stored identity, decides when release is safe.

One connection admits one bounded operation.  Ordinary callers refuse a busy
admission cell immediately.  Original terminal-owner persistence can explicitly
wait within the same five-second allowance used for that Store action.  Waiting
uses no SQL interrupt, and closure, poisoning, root identity and remaining time
are rechecked before the action begins.  The admitted action executes once.
Rollback keeps its separate five-second bound.  Internal transaction programs
return a strict result and bounded invalidations from the
same database snapshot, so a matching duplicate can emit no event.  Resource
changes and sequence allocation commit together.  Failure rolls back, and an
uncertain commit or rollback poisons the connection.  The connection verifies
WAL, synchronous FULL, foreign keys, busy limits, disk-backed temporary storage,
and fixed cache settings.  Passive checkpoints report observed progress rather
than guaranteed completion.

Cancellation repeatedly interrupts the original SQLite action until its thread
joins.  The interruption task also joins before rollback, connection reuse, or
close, so a late interrupt cannot affect a subsequent operation.  Caller
exceptions remain distinct from storage refusals and operation expiry.

SQLite operates under the operator-approved stable private namespace assumption
documented in @file{manager/STORAGE.md}.  Native temporary files can reside
outside the manager root.  Cache settings are not total heap or temporary-disk
quotas, and cooperative cancellation is not an absolute OS IO deadline.
The storage layer alone does not implement live approval, recovery, wider
resource quotas, the manager service, or client transports.

@subsection Command receipt transactions

The internal manager command layer uses the existing storage transaction
rather than a separate ledger engine.  It verifies bounded bearer possession
against the stored token verifier and rechecks current client, credential, and
profile authority inside the transaction.  Opaque possession proofs and live
dispatch tickets cannot be constructed from stored identifiers.  Configuration
checks hold the owning installation boundary, with configuration-before-store
lock ordering.  Each Store action waits for the configuration guard and the
store gate within its five-second allowance, and no durable mutation queue
exists.

An idempotency key binds the registered client, method, canonical resource URI,
authority epoch, exact body digest and length, media type, and precondition.
Current authorization and epoch checks precede ledger lookup.  An authorized
matching retry returns the immutable original receipt before a fresh revision
or lifecycle transition is attempted, without another event, charge, or ticket.
Conflicting bindings refuse.  Aged inactive receipts retain charged non-content
tombstones rather than permitting replay.

The version-two command migration preserves existing version-one facts.
The version-three migration adds draft representations, internal schema version
four adds admission order, resource domains, and native observations, and version
five adds immutable start intents.  Version six retains immutable ingestion
prefixes and protects observed run identities.  These migrations preserve earlier
records transactionally.
Legacy raw bodies remain exact and are compared within SQLite rather than copied
through the result buffer.  The frozen public manager-store compatibility domain
remains version one.  Logical ledger reservations include bounded future receipt
and observation space.  Ordinary admission cannot consume the finite cancellation
reserve within the configured total.  Fresh ordinary commands use per-credential
minute counters, while cancellation uses its independent global rate.  Exact
retries consume neither counter, and backward clock movement grants no refill.

Only a fresh committed intent may yield a one-shot dispatch ticket.  Reservation
and attempted delivery are distinct durable facts, and the attempted marker
commits before the owning adapter is invoked.  A callback return is not native
acknowledgement or effect evidence.  Explicit local coordination effects can
commit without a fabricated dispatch.  Receipt, acknowledgement, and effect
codecs follow the frozen wire schema and its bounded representations.

Credential administration, transport authentication, actual worker correlation,
safe lifecycle transitions, and wider retention and supervision remain with
their owning layers.  Logical reservations are not physical database, temporary
disk, or total-heap limits.  The complete contract and evidence limits are in
@file{manager/COMMANDS.md}.

@subsection Drafts and immutable inputs

The internal draft layer creates requests from the current installed catalogue,
using exact workflow, descriptor, and profile revisions.  Failed discovery and
reload clear the cached catalogue.  Authorized completed retries retain the
original creation view without adopting new discovery facts or revealing later
edits.  Readiness records missing inputs and validation errors without executing
a workflow, and empty text remains supplied input.

Literal values retain their UTF-8 bytes in bounded ordered chunks with length,
count, and digest metadata.  Migration preserves previous bytes and declarations.
Unknown native transport lengths remain unknown rather than becoming zero or
ready.  Missing or changed chunks refuse instead of becoming empty input.

Uploads reserve bounded holding capacity before streaming to Runtime's durable
private publisher.  The database transaction does not span upload IO, and the
exact command-body binding is computed from the actual stream through EOF.
Confirmed publication and command acceptance remain distinct.  An uncertain
publication retains its installed bytes and charged orphan protection without a
successful capture binding.  Completed retries do not publish a second file.

Materialization verifies capture bytes and preserves declaration order through
the shared frontend encoder.  Captures use inline transport only when the actual
encoded frame fits, otherwise they use verified server-owned immutable files.
Oversized literal frames refuse without changing their input representation.
Per-client and global draft limits, per-request holding and assembled-input
limits, and global capture reservations are independent accounting bounds.

The file-operation slot is fail-fast and is joined before store closure.
The standalone draft operation requires no active reservation or live preparation
before editing.  The admission owner invalidates its live association and retains
all claims until actual worker cleanup before completing an edit or withdrawal.
The representation and verification contract remains in @file{manager/DRAFTS.md}.

@subsection Manager-owned native workers

The internal worker adapter launches the configured native frontend through the
existing CLI proxy and pre-RTS fd 3 bootstrap.  Current catalogue, invocation,
working directory, target arguments, and person mode establish its launch
association.  File inputs are verified against retained manager capture records,
and a fresh capability query does not replace the selected catalogue revision.
The shared @code{frontendOwnedEnvironment} list names the native proxy bindings
that manager profile validation rejects before any discovery or launch.

Each worker has a separate Store-owned registration, private root, and retained
lease.  The absolute ceiling of sixteen includes construction through joined
cleanup and does not replace configured admission policy.  Store closure fences
new registrations and joins existing workers outside the configuration and
database locks.  Only the original Runtime process-group completion can prove
cleanup, and unproven completion retains the registration and ownership lease.
The internal retry operation cannot reconstruct a process from a stored PID.

The private writer serializes native decisions and controls without automatic
retry after uncertain writes.  Preparation retains the native identity without
a synthetic started event.  A successful write does not establish native
acknowledgement, effect, or workflow success, and a low-level start operation
does not replace the approval transaction.

One internal ingestion consumer reads a bounded queue of unchanged native
envelopes.  A successful callback removes only its in-memory queue head.
Exceptions preserve that head without invoking the callback again.  Status
observers neither consume the queue nor close worker pipes.  The queue is bounded
at thirty-two frames and eight MiB of wire bytes, while stderr retains at most
65536 bytes with explicit truncation and continued concurrent draining.
Buffered evidence remains readable through the original opaque handle after
physical cleanup.  An empty queue waits for original producer completion before
returning its recorded failure or end of input, without restoring execution or
control authority.

@code{withStartingFrontendWorker} loans the opaque handle during construction,
while @code{withFrontendWorker} waits for the actual prepared response before its
callback.  Startup retains one thirty-second deadline even when the early caller
does not await preparation, and writes have a five-second budget.
The outer termination grace is five seconds, with the CLI proxy's existing
two-second inner grace unchanged.  Final cleanup remains joined rather than
being represented as an absolute OS IO deadline.  Exact approval, durable
ingestion, and broader service supervision remain separate owners.
The current contract and verification boundary are in @file{manager/WORKERS.md}.

@subsection Admission and resource reservations

The internal admission controller selects the globally oldest eligible queued
request and reserves its global slot and complete exclusive resource footprint
in one transaction.  Current profile and structural readiness remain separate
from successful native preparation.  Blocked older work does not prevent an
independent eligible request from progressing, and no partial key set is held
while waiting for another resource.

Enqueue is explicit, with a one-hundred-request global queue and a persistent
UInt64 ordering clock distinct from event sequences.  The current execution limit
is global, defaults to one, and cannot exceed sixteen.  Retained reservations
still consume capacity after a lower limit is installed.  Operator resource keys
declare complete exclusive footprints.  Empty declarations share a separate
unclassified domain that cannot collide with an operator key string.

Commands retains an opaque invocation context before acceptance, so a lost return
can be reconciled only for that known operation.  An accepted enqueue permit
allows exact internal materialization after submitting-credential expiry without
granting new client access or approval.  Original receipts remain immutable, and
replay cannot reconstruct a missing continuation, timer, or native worker.

The controller retains construction, native preparation, and cleanup ownership
in bounded memory associated with the actual Store.  Review waiting consumes the
reservation.  The ten-minute pending-review lifetime starts at the genuine native
prepared observation and uses monotonic time, not a restored wall-clock field.
A scoped @code{CommitDeadline} is checked after bounded transaction work and
invalidations, immediately before COMMIT.  An expired fresh acceptance rolls back,
while exact authorized receipt replay does not reapply fresh eligibility.

Live edits and withdrawal first invalidate the old association and mark cleanup
pending without releasing claims.  Only confirmed cleanup of the original worker
or proven absence of construction permits the final request transition, effect,
and release.  Storage failure can leave durable claims unresolved even after
physical cleanup, and physical uncertainty retains its original ownership fence.

The controller supplies actual native review observations and a guarded opaque
handoff to the exact approval owner.  Reservations remain held through accepted
start intent, native running, and original cleanup.  Admission tests establish
the pre-start lifecycle, while the approval tests exercise accepted-start
integration.  Neither establishes a completed manager service or an SQL
refinement theorem.  The current contract and evidence limits are in
@file{manager/ADMISSION.md}.

@subsection Exact review and original start intent

The internal approval layer consumes the original live preparation rather than
reconstructing a worker from stored records.  It validates the native identity,
descriptor, plan, result code, input summaries, and captured invocation and
target association.  The public plan contains the same validated native plan
encoded as JSON text.  Capture summaries derive from the verified materialized
bytes rather than a later pathname lookup.

Public policy uses a fixed allowlist that omits private invocation, environment,
scratch, and routing fields.  Content-bearing strings are screened against
known private paths, available private values, every nonempty selected operator
environment value, and recognized credential syntax.  Unsafe or oversized
required facts refuse rather than being redacted or truncated into different
consent.  Public labels and schema-owned identifiers retain their public meaning.
These checks do not establish universal secret detection or sandbox isolation.

The protected binding records request and profile revisions, original worker
and process generation, reservation, native identity, and expiry.  Private
canonical-value hashes and a fresh cryptographic nonce bind the public review
digest without publishing the nonce or private hashes.  Repeated publication
and authorized reads preserve the original digest and expiry.  Stored bindings
and hashes cannot recreate live authority after the owning process ends.

Fresh approval checks current authorization, preparation ETag, review digest,
catalogue, and the complete original resource footprint.  The final transaction
guard checks the original registration, prepared lifecycle, generation, scope,
and monotonic deadline, with a nonblocking liveness observation through Runtime's
original process-group token.  Detected loss refuses before COMMIT, but later
death does not revoke an accepted intent.  Physical commit-time liveness and
completion before a wall-clock deadline are not promised.

Acceptance commits the approving client, immutable receipt, consumed preparation,
start intent, run identity, reservation association, and invalidations together.
The original invocation and one-shot dispatch ticket survive a lost return.
Delivery writes the existing start frame to the same worker outside the database
and configuration locks.  Authorized exact retries return the original receipt
without another worker or start attempt, and ambiguous delivery remains unresolved.
A review timer that observes the accepted intent retires rather than expiring it.

Accepted workers retain their reservations until original cleanup is confirmed.
Supervision changes to cleanup-pending and lost advance the run revision and
publish matching invalidations in their existing transactions.  Failed publication
can retain durable claims after physical cleanup, and only the original retry
path can publish release.  These observations preserve the original consent and
do not fabricate Runtime acknowledgement, terminal status, or a successful result.

@file{manager/APPROVAL.md} states the complete internal contract and native
verification boundary.  Durable Runtime ingestion, public control endpoints,
the manager service, and client transports remain outside this approval layer.

@subsection Durable Runtime ingestion

The internal @code{Agentic.Manager.State} layer consumes validated envelopes
through the original accepted-worker association.  Each record is bound to its
trusted profile, root, native run, and unsigned Runtime sequence.  Its complete
original bytes and digest remain immutable, including the final LF when present.
The shared decoder applies its one-MiB limit to the payload before that delimiter.

Ingestion appends the envelope through Runtime's checkpoint operation rather
than a second interpreter.  The durable projection is a versioned manifest of
the immutable sequence-zero prefix, its boundary, and its shared snapshot digest.
Envelope identity, projection, decision observations, reference-only artifacts,
and bounded invalidations commit together.  First committed evidence also moves
a bound managed request from start-pending to associated, with matching request
and reservation revisions.  Observer-only runs have no request transition.

Matching original-byte duplicates have no effect.  Conflicting duplicates, gaps,
wrong associations, and invalid terminal histories preserve the prior valid
prefix and refuse.  Failed callbacks retain the original Worker queue head for
explicit retry, including a committed record whose callback return was lost.
Association can commit while cleanup publication remains pending, and only the
original finalization owner can reconcile that post-start revision and release
its reservation.  Pre-start edit and withdrawal guards remain unchanged.

Restoration captures one fixed boundary, then reads each immutable record in at
most three 512-KiB slices and restores it through Runtime.  Concurrent appends
cannot change that captured prefix.  Publication rechecks the prior boundary
and refuses a stale fold rather than replacing a newer projection.  Canonical
checkpoints and complete original-wire evidence have independent 64-MiB bounds.
Storage statement, row, input, result, and time limits remain unchanged.

The initial implementation replays the full prefix per append, with quadratic
total replay cost and no mutable projection cache.  Decisions remain observations
and referenced artifacts remain unverified content.  Neither process exit nor
prose can establish Runtime success.  Public control endpoints, artifact
verification, retention, restart orchestration, and the manager service remain
with their owning layers.  The storage contract and executable ingestion gate
are documented in @file{manager/STORAGE.md}.

@section Native frontend preparation
@c COVER CLI verb:frontend author

The @code{frontend} command prepares one execution and retains it until the
client approves or discards it.  The client invokes the runner directly with
ordinary standard-input and standard-output pipes, without arranging fd 3.
The runner owns a separate process group and reserves fd 3 inside its worker
before the Haskell RTS initializes its event managers.  Each preparation and
decision is one newline-terminated JSON object, bounded at 2 MiB.

A preparation specifies the workflow, an absolute private state directory,
the ordinary target arguments, and every declared input.  It may also carry the
client's exact configured invocation.  Literal input values remain in the
private pipe rather than appearing in process arguments.

@example
@{
  "version": 1,
  "operation": "prepare",
  "workflow": "review",
  "stateDirectory": "/home/user/.local/state/workflows",
  "targetArguments": ["--scripted"],
  "personAnswering": "local-control",
  "invocation": @{
    "version": 1,
    "runnerAlias": "configured-alias",
    "executable": "/trusted/wrapper",
    "prefixArgs": ["--profile", "work"]
  @},
  "inputs": [
    @{"name": "subject", "source": "literal", "value": "Review this change"@},
    @{"name": "notes", "source": "file", "path": "/home/user/notes.txt"@}
  ]
@}
@end example

Input names occur exactly once and are checked against the descriptor.
File sources are bounded regular files opened without following the leaf
symbolic link.  The captured bytes remain in memory, so subsequent source-file
changes do not change the prepared execution.  Captured input transports are
bounded at 64 MiB in total.  Literal values use UTF-8, with one additional LF
in prompt-file transport so that the existing single-LF decoding preserves
the complete literal value.  Command-tail and standard-input transports retain
their UTF-8 bytes without that addition.  The shared Runtime operation
@code{frontendLiteralBytes} supplies this encoding for both CLI capture and
manager accounting.

The @code{transport} source carries UTF-8 text captured by the client before
preparation.  Its @code{value} encodes the original transport bytes rather
than the logical input value:

@example
@{"name": "notes", "source": "transport", "value": "Captured text\n"@}
@end example

The runner encodes that text without adding an LF, removing a BOM, or
normalizing Unicode.  The existing input decoder then applies the descriptor
source rule once.  Prompt inputs lose one final LF when present, while
command-tail and standard-input values retain their text.  Byte counts,
digests, persisted inputs, and restoration retain the original transport
bytes.  No source path is accepted or reread for this variant, and capture
requires valid UTF-8 without replacement decoding or BOM removal.

Transport input remains within the preparation frame's 2 MiB limit, including
JSON escaping.  The 64 MiB aggregate input limit does not enlarge that frame
limit.  Oversized captures are refused rather than converted to a file or
literal source.  Request version 1 and the existing source variants retain
their meanings.

Preparation resolves the target through the ordinary CLI grammar and routing
fixed point.  The program, its run facts, the resolved policy, and the worker
environment are retained in the same process.  Inventory discovery follows
the supplied target options, while workflow execution does not begin before
approval.  The state root may be created during preparation, but no run directory,
input files, lease, or workflow store is created before @code{start}.

The response has @code{version: 1} and @code{operation: "prepared"}.  It
contains @code{approvalId}, @code{runId}, @code{rootIdentity}, @code{cwd},
the version-3 @code{descriptor}, the exact @code{plan}, @code{programHash},
@code{targetKind}, @code{targetArguments}, sanitized @code{policy},
@code{personAnswering}, and input names with decimal-string byte counts and
bare hexadecimal SHA-256 digests.  The required @code{server} record has the
same shape and values as capability discovery, while required
@code{invocation} is the validated request record or JSON null.  Its size is
bounded at 64 MiB plus 4096 bytes.
The exact plan includes the prepared run facts.  The separate program hash
retains the existing frontend-manifest meaning: the post-input static program
with run facts unbound.  Returned target arguments include resolved defaults,
including an automatically selected ACP scratch directory, so the retained
arguments describe the execution accepted by the existing lineage commands.
The byte and value reference vectors are in
@file{test/fixtures/runtime/input-sources/v1.json}.

The optional request field @code{targetKind} preserves a native host's
@code{current}, @code{child}, or @code{remote} classification for an ACP target.
Other classifications must agree with the backend selected by the CLI.
Person answering defaults to @code{local-control} and also accepts @code{engine}.
The optional invocation record has exactly @code{version}, @code{runnerAlias},
@code{executable}, and @code{prefixArgs}.  Version must be 1.  Alias and
executable are non-whitespace, NUL-free strings bounded at 256 and 4096 UTF-8
bytes.  The executable remains exact and can be a bare command.  Prefix
arguments remain exact and ordered, with at most 4096 entries and 65536 UTF-8
bytes in aggregate.  An empty vector is explicit.  Existing
credential-argument checks reject recognizable secret-bearing prefix arguments,
but callers remain responsible for excluding credentials and private
environment selectors.  Unknown fields, incompatible classifications, missing
inputs, credential-bearing adapter or invocation arguments, and malformed
requests are refused before managed run writes.

Approval is a decision on that same process and its captured root identity:

@example
@{"version":1,"operation":"start","approvalId":"RETURNED_APPROVAL_ID"@}
@end example

Using @code{discard} instead of @code{start} exits without creating a run.
The approval ID is local to one preparation, and a mismatched ID is refused.
The client does not reconstruct a routing fingerprint or spawn another machine
command after approval.  Changing the setup requires another preparation and
review.

After @code{start}, standard output contains the existing protocol-version-2
runtime envelopes, and the same input pipe accepts existing version-2 controls.
The runner writes the original captured input transports through
@code{Runtime.PrivateRoot}, then owns the lease and runtime execution.  Closing
the control pipe cancels execution.  The public @code{Runtime.ProcessGroup}
implementation retains the group leader until its sole reap and terminates
descendants when that leader exits.

A preparation without invocation metadata writes the existing frontend
manifest version 2 with no @code{invocation} key.  A preparation with invocation
metadata writes explicit version 3, which contains every version-2 field and a
required non-null @code{invocation}.  Decoding uses separate strict branches for
versions 2 and 3, while pre-versioned manifests remain accepted.  An explicit
version 1 is refused.  @code{read-run} exposes the corresponding original
manifest shape.

Native manifests keep server identity separate from configured invocation.
@code{runnerId} remains the registry's binary name, @code{runnerExecutable}
remains the executing binary's absolute path, and @code{runnerVersion} remains
the package-derived server version.  The invocation record is client-declared
non-secret metadata rather than server attestation or execution authority.  A
restoring client resolves @code{runnerAlias} only in its current trusted
configuration, requires exact executable and ordered prefix-argument equality,
and launches that trusted configuration.  It never reconstructs a command from
stored metadata or infers invocation identity from a version-2, unversioned, or
parent record.

The @code{Agentic.Cli.Command.FrontendSession} entry point also accepts a
@code{prepare-lineage} request.  It names an existing state directory, a
@code{parentRunId}, and @code{lineage} equal to @code{restart}, @code{resume}, or
@code{fork}.  It may carry the same invocation record.  The optional
person-answering mode defaults to local control.  Inputs, target arguments,
workflow identity, and working directory come from the parent manifest and
authenticated input files rather than client-supplied reconstructions.

@example
@{
  "version": 1,
  "operation": "prepare-lineage",
  "stateDirectory": "/home/user/.local/state/workflows",
  "parentRunId": "PARENT_RUN_ID",
  "lineage": "fork",
  "invocation": @{
    "version": 1,
    "runnerAlias": "configured-alias",
    "executable": "/trusted/wrapper",
    "prefixArgs": ["--profile", "work"]
  @},
  "edits": [@{"operation":"replace","occurrenceId":"0","answer":false@}]
@}
@end example

Fork edits either @code{drop} an occurrence or @code{replace} its answer with
a JSON value.  The existing lineage validator checks the stored question's
type and schema, replayability, checkpoint, effects, and person-answer provenance.
Restart and resume retain their exact program and policy requirements, while
fork retains the existing immutable-parent and answer-reuse rules.

Preparation retains the parent directory identity and refuses another current
owner.  A version-3 parent requires an exactly equal invocation in the lineage
request.  A missing or mismatched record is refused before semantic preparation
or managed writes, and the child retains that invocation.  For a version-2 or
unversioned parent, omission produces a version-2 child, while an explicitly
supplied invocation produces a version-3 child and describes only that child.
Its response adds @code{parentRunId}, @code{lineage}, and edit metadata.
Replacement metadata contains a digest rather than the answer content.  After
approval, the parent identity, ownership, and inherited answers are revalidated
before execution, and the child receives separate input files, manifest, lease,
and runtime store.  Parent files are never rewritten by these operations.

An inherited native proxy still requires its host to provide the appropriate
connection environment.  A stored manifest or read-only query does not grant
attachment or control authority.  The @code{frontend-io} interface remains
read-only throughout history inspection and lineage preparation.

The runtime facade also exposes @code{readNdjsonFrame} and
@code{withBufferedControlInputFor}.  These preserve bytes already read after
a preparation decision when the same transport begins carrying controls.
@code{withControlInputFor} retains its existing empty-buffer behavior.

@section Machine protocol options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --protocol-version author
@item --protocol-version 1|2
Select the machine event and store contract.  Omission remains version 1.
Version 2 requires @code{AGENT_CAT_RUN_STORE}, writes store format 2, and
installs the result artifact before successful completion is emitted.

@c COVER CLI --person-answering author
@item --person-answering engine|local-control
Select who realizes every person-addressed question in this machine run.
@code{engine} preserves ordinary routing.  @code{local-control} requires
protocol version 2, @code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_FD=3}, and the private store; it
publishes a verified full-question reference and accepts a schema-checked
@code{answerPerson} control without creating a physical engine attempt.
@end table
value was supplied explicitly, and both control modes together are refused.
@section Target and transport options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --scripted author
@item --scripted
Select the registered canned table.  It is mutually exclusive with
@code{--engine}, @code{--session}, every live transport option, @code{--route},
and every version-2 persona or discovery override.

@c COVER CLI --engine author
@item --engine acp
Select an ACP backend.  @code{--engine deck} is accepted only with
@code{--session ID}, and a bare session already selects deck.  Any other word
is refused.  This backend and explicit @code{--route} entries are the complete
route table, even when @code{--routing} is also present.

@c COVER CLI --session author
@item --session ID
Select an agent-deck session.  It is mutually exclusive with scripted and ACP
targets.  This session and explicit @code{--route} entries are the complete
route table, even when @code{--routing} is also present.

@c COVER CLI --adapter author
@item --adapter stub|claude|codex|droid|PATH
Select the ACP adapter.  It defaults to @code{stub} and is meaningful only when
at least one ACP backend exists.

@c COVER CLI --model author
@item --model ID
Set the model option of the default ACP answerer to @code{ID} before every
question.  The value must be one that the adapter offers.  An unoffered value is
refused before any prompt with the offered values and exit status 2.  It is
accepted only with @code{--engine acp}, and it does not apply to an adapter that
only a @code{--route} entry names.

@c COVER CLI --effort author
@item --effort LEVEL
Set the effort option of the default ACP answerer in the same way.

@c COVER CLI --adapter-arg author
@item --adapter-arg ARG
Append an argument to every ACP adapter argv.  Repeatable and meaningful only
for a run that reaches ACP.

@c COVER CLI --scratch author
@item --scratch DIR
Set the working and authorized effect directory for ACP backends.  It defaults
to a fresh temporary directory printed in the run header and is meaningful only
when ACP is used.

@c COVER CLI --binary author
@item --binary PATH
Set the @command{agent-deck} executable for deck backends.  It defaults to
@code{agent-deck} on @code{PATH}.

@c COVER CLI --poll author
@item --poll MS
Set the interval between deck status checks.  The default interval is 1000
milliseconds, and the value must be a nonnegative integer.

@c COVER CLI --timeout author
@item --timeout MS
Set the per-request or per-turn budget for every live backend.  Without the
flag, ACP uses 900000 milliseconds and deck uses 600000.  The CLI accepts a
nonnegative integer.  Zero leaves ACP unbounded but gives deck a deadline that
has already expired, so a mixed run must use a positive value.

@c COVER CLI --verbose author
@item --verbose
Narrate live transport commands, polls, and protocol activity on standard error.
It is refused under scripted execution.

@c COVER CLI --person-answer author
@item --person-answer model:NAME|tool:NAME
Name a model or tool addressee whose questions a person answers instead of the
routed backend.  The option is repeatable, and each address may appear once.  It
is accepted only with a live target in a machine run or a frontend preparation
whose person-answering mode is @code{local-control}.  The @code{run} verb, the
@code{engine} mode and scripted execution refuse it before a run store exists.
The route table must still cover a named model.

The target policy carries the sorted addresses as the field
@code{personAnswers}, so the review and exact approval cover them.  A target
without the option has no such field, and its policy keeps the same bytes and
digest.  Before an occurrence of a named question starts, the runtime
addresses the question to the person @code{model:NAME} or @code{tool:NAME} and
removes its model pin.  The occurrence therefore starts with the addressee
@code{person model:NAME} and follows the local person path of
@code{--person-answering local-control}: the private question reference, the
pending event and the typed @code{answerPerson} control.  No engine start, turn
or tool call occurs for it, and the run log records the answer from the intake
with the control that supplied it.
@end table

@section Fork edit options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --drop-answer author
@item --drop-answer OCCURRENCE_ID
For @code{lineage-check fork} or @code{machine-fork}, omit one persisted answer
from inheritance so that occurrence runs again.  Repeatable.

@c COVER CLI --set-answer author
@item --set-answer OCCURRENCE_ID=JSON_FILE
Replace one inherited answer with the single JSON value in @code{JSON_FILE}.
The runner validates it against the persisted answer code or schema before child
creation.  Repeatable and valid only for fork.
@end table

@section Routing

@table @code
@item --routing
Explicitly name the routing target for a live run.  The spelling is optional
when neither @code{--engine} nor @code{--session} is present, because their
absence always selects routing.  Every engine-bound question must have a
configured model pin, and there is no default backend.  When an engine or
session is present, that more explicit target wins and routing files are not
read.

@c COVER CLI --route author
@item --route NAME=BACKEND
Route one serving-model pin to @code{acp:ADAPTER} or @code{deck:SESSION}.
Repeatable at most once per name.  The Program must contain the named primary or
alternate pin, and an explicit default live backend is required.  Routing-only
execution uses configured profiles and @code{--realize}, not raw routes.

@c COVER CLI --persona author
@item --persona NAME
Select a version-2 persona for routing-only execution.  Precedence is this flag,
@code{AGENT_CAT_PERSONA}, the project file's @code{persona}, then the user file's
@code{default-persona}.  No repository, branch, hostname, account, or email is
inferred.  An explicit engine or session makes this option inapplicable.

@c COVER CLI --realize author
@item --realize AXIS=MODEL-ALIAS
Replace one managed runtime axis with a concrete alias allowed by the selected
persona.  Repeatable once per axis.  The alias's owning engine must also be in the
persona engine allowlist.

@c COVER CLI --offline author
@item --offline
Perform no catalogue request.  A cache no older than @code{stale-if-error} may
satisfy inventory-dependent selectors; an exact selector may remain
@code{static-unverified}.

@c COVER CLI --refresh-models author
@item --refresh-models
Force every required catalogue to refresh.  A failure is a setup refusal and
never silently falls back to stale cache.  Mutually exclusive with
@code{--offline}.

@c COVER CLI --expect-routing-fingerprint author
@item --expect-routing-fingerprint SHA256
Require version-2 routing-only launch resolution to equal the opaque fingerprint
returned by routing inspection.  Frontends supply this internal preflight token.
A mismatch refuses the run before creating its store or starting an engine.
@end table

A route table can contain both schemes.  ACP-specific and deck-specific flags
are accepted when their corresponding scheme occurs anywhere in that table and
are refused otherwise.  Routes do not alter Plan folds or bills, because backend
identity is absent from the semantic key.

The version-2 user file is
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agent-cat/routing.yaml}, with @file{~/.config} as its
fallback.  The project layer is the nearest @file{.agent-cat/routing.yaml} up to
the Git boundary.  Discovery assigns these roles before decoding; document shape
cannot promote a project file to user authority, and untagged version-2 loading
is refused.  Mixed schema versions fail before a secret lookup.  The CLI
resolves environment references only for engines that remain after the routing
and run-fact fixed point.  ACP inherits the ambient environment after every
declared source and destination name is removed, and then receives the selected
bindings.  This is a routing context, not credential isolation from files,
profiles, or the operating system.

The bounded Haskell HTTP client fetches OpenAI and Anthropic catalogue responses,
and it never invokes @command{curl} or any other process.  It supports no TLS at
present, so it refreshes only plain-HTTP catalogues.  Configuration accepts plain
HTTP only without authentication and only for literal loopback addresses.  The
client refuses an HTTPS catalogue with the failure @code{tls-not-supported}
before it builds a header or opens a connection, and it never retries that
catalogue over plain HTTP.  An explicit refresh then fails with an error that
names the persona, the engine, and the endpoint fingerprint.  A normal refresh
uses a usable stale-if-error cache or returns no inventory, and it reports the
failure as a warning.  The client limits a URL to 8192 UTF-8 bytes and its query
to 4096 bytes and 64 items, and it rechecks those bounds after it adds pagination
parameters.  The complete explicit header set, including the generated request
identifier, shares the count, value, and aggregate-byte bounds.  Response bytes,
pages, entries,
model identifiers, and time are also bounded.  Redirects are
disabled.  Atomic files with
mode 0600 are stored below
@file{$XDG_CACHE_HOME/agent-cat/models/PERSONA/FINGERPRINT.json}.  The path
fingerprint covers the complete non-secret engine definition, including cache
policy, while a separate endpoint fingerprint explains model selection.  A cache
record is bounded before allocation, and managed cache directories cannot be
symbolic links.  The exact selection, its inventory provenance, and the
execution fingerprint enter the manifest policy with a canonical SHA-256
digest before the first question.  The complete snapshot remains immutable
provenance.  Restart and resume compare its stable execution projection, so an
observation-time inventory source, fetch time, cache age, warning, or the digest
derived from those fields cannot reject otherwise identical routing.  Persona
selection, engine and endpoint fingerprints, exact model, settings, options, and
routes remain significant.

@section Outputs and exit status

The human output of @code{plan}, @code{cost}, and @code{help} is descriptive,
and it is not a stable machine interface.  @code{list --json},
@code{plan --json}, and @code{--routing --json} use stable key names.  JSON
object key order is not significant, and the input array follows declaration
order.  @code{list --json} retains descriptor version 2, while
@code{--descriptor-version 3} requests routing and protocol-negotiation
capabilities.  Machine protocol and store format retain version 1 unless
protocol version 2 is selected explicitly.

Run output is the trace record as it happens.  There is no summary JSON,
because a second representation either omits or duplicates the event stream.
Standard error carries refusals, transport narration, failed attempts, and
recovery diagnostics.

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .88
@headitem Status @tab Meaning
@item 0 @tab Inspection completed, usage or version printed on request, or the
run reached its represented end.
@item 1 @tab Usage or preflight refusal, including an incomplete command form.
No live execution has started.
@item 2 @tab Transport failure, missing process, protocol error, stopped session,
or timeout.
@item 3 @tab Run abandonment after answer recovery or failed required completion
evidence.
@item 130 @tab Machine run cancelled by a control command or by control-input
end of file.
@end multitable

@node Diagnostics and Troubleshooting
@chapter Diagnostics and Troubleshooting

This chapter begins with common questions.  It then relates compiler and
runner symptoms to the layer that can explain them.  Handle a diagnostic from
Haskell, from Program preflight, from typed answer recovery, or from transport
execution at that same layer.

@section Where to look

@table @asis
@item What is agent-cat for?
Read @ref{Introduction}.  The architecture and evidence limits follow in
@ref{Architecture and Guarantees}.
@item Why can a repeated question be asked once yet appear twice in a trace?
Read @ref{Memoization and event identity}.  Semantic occurrences and operational
answer reuse are distinct.
@item Why can the displayed minimum cost be unattainable?
Read @ref{Cost trees and bills}.  The tree combines syntactic arms, while
worlds can correlate separate choices.
@item How is a workflow run without credentials?
Use scripted execution in @ref{Scripted execution}.
@item Which Haskell word or type is public?
Look it up in @ref{Haskell Authoring Reference} or the Haskell API index.
@item Which command-line flag belongs to an engine?
Use @ref{Runner Reference} or the CLI index.
@item What does a mathematical term mean here?
Use @ref{Glossary}.  Each cross-cutting term has one canonical definition
there.
@end table

@section Haskell authoring diagnostics

@table @samp
@item Variable not in scope
A prompt hole or later expression names no Haskell variable in lexical scope.
Correct the spelling or move the use into the block or case arm where its handle
is bound.  The workflow layer performs no second string-based lookup.

@item this binding is not live here
A typed handle has crossed its scope boundary, commonly from one branch arm into
another or outside a function body.  Pass a supported value through a function
argument or place the use within the handle's lexical scope.

@item a hole in a [wft|...|] prompt names a define
A @code{wft} fence has no live workflow scope.  Use @code{wf} in the block where
the handle is live, or convert only author-supplied define text through
@code{wft}.

@item Couldn't match 'CodeVerdict with 'CodeText
A source, parameter, argument, branch, or function result has the wrong answer
kind.  Follow the two types that GHC names.  Select a question kind with
@code{confirm}, @code{answering}, @code{annotated}, or @code{structured}, and
keep function parameters and call arguments exact.

@item nothing follows a terminal
A statement appears after @code{stop}, @code{ask_}, @code{if}, a revision case,
@code{when}, or @code{unless}.  Move the statement into every applicable arm or
place it before the terminal.

@item Couldn't match Open with Body
A function-body terminal was placed in a workflow, or a workflow terminal was
placed in a function.  Use @code{answer} or @code{done} to return from a function
and @code{stop} or another workflow terminal to finish the main block.

@item a function body is a straight line
A branch, bounded revision, or @code{knownHere} was placed inside a function
body.  Move finite control to the caller and call the straight-line function
from each arm.

@item a bounded revision reviews first ... and then amends
A revision clause contains an extra statement, lacks its verdict review, or does
not finish with @code{amend}.  Retain exactly the two represented clause sources.

@item panel: a panel needs at least one member
The authoring list is empty.  Supply at least one question.  For @code{panelText},
also give every member a valid distinct label.

@item decide: a decider needs at least one needle
The classifier has no meaningful literal test.  Supply one or more nonempty
needles.  A test that is chosen from model-authored text changes the intended
trust boundary, and it is unsupported.

@item named: b0 is a name this surface generates for itself
A selected binding or parameter uses the reserved @code{bN} or @code{rN} shape.
Choose a descriptive name outside those families.

@item function declared twice or call names a function not declared earlier
The @code{defining} table has a duplicate, forward call, or omitted callee.  Put
each function once and order dependencies before callers.  Recursive tables are
outside the finite authoring language.
@end table

@section Program and command-line refusals

@table @samp
@item no verb 'NAME'
The first word is neither a CLI verb nor an accepted help form.  Use
@code{list}, @code{help}, @code{plan}, @code{cost}, or @code{run}.  A registered
name by itself is deliberately ambiguous and is refused.

@item no example named 'NAME'
The registry has no row of that name.  Run @code{agentic-run list} and use its
exact spelling.

@item has no input named 'NAME'
An input flag names no operator input declared by the Program.  Read the
@code{inputs} line in help, plan, or list JSON.  Run facts appear separately and
cannot be supplied by a flag.

@item input 'run.engine' is a run fact
The runner owns that value.  Remove the input flag, because a live or scripted
run binds the fact automatically.

@item --input needs a program with exactly one input
Use @code{--input-file NAME=FILE} or @code{--input-arg NAME=VALUE} when several
operator inputs exist.  A Program with none accepts no input flag.

@item program asks an unpinned model question
@code{--require-pinned} found a model party without @code{servedBy}.  Add a
primary serving model in source or run without the opt-in guard when unpinned
dispatch is intended.

@item --route names a model this program never pins
The left side must be a primary or alternate serving-model pin from the Program,
rather than the party identifier.  Inspect @code{pins} through help or JSON.

@item --route refines a command-line default answerer, and there is none
Add @code{--engine acp} or @code{--session ID}.  Raw routes refine only an
explicit command-line target.

@item FLAG is not the ENGINE engine's to take
The option is inert, because the selected backend table contains no matching
scheme.  Remove it, or add an actual ACP or deck backend that consumes it.

@item routing without --engine or --session requires full pin coverage
Add @code{servedBy} to every model ask and define each profile in routing
configuration.  A tool or person question cannot carry a model pin and therefore
requires an explicit engine or session.  The refusal of @code{run NAME} ends
with @samp{Or rehearse with agentic-run run NAME --scripted, which asks
nobody.}

@item routing configuration has no route for pinned model NAME
Define the named profile in the selected routing persona, or use an explicit
engine or session.  The refusal of @code{run NAME} also names the scripted
rehearsal.
@end table

@section Execution failures

@table @asis
@item Exit 2 with @samp{could not run}
The adapter, the deck binary, or another required process was not found or did
not start.  Confirm the path and the execute permission before you examine
prompts.

@item Exit 2 with protocol or EOF wording
The live transport ended, or it spoke a message outside the expected protocol.
Use @code{--verbose} and the named failing method.  The represented Program
has not supplied a typed answer for this failure.

@item Exit 2 with @samp{did not answer within ...ms}
The transport exceeded its turn budget.  Check whether the session is stopped,
stale, or still working before increasing @code{--timeout}.  A larger budget
does not repair a session that cannot produce newer output.

@item Exit 3 after an unreadable answer
The normal decoder and the configured re-asks did not obtain a value of the
required kind.  Read the quoted bytes and the answer specification.  Correct
the prompt or the answering service, and do not add a default answer that was
never given.

@item Exit 3 after a cancelled ACP effect
ACP reported a turn that lacked required completion evidence.  The runner
refuses to record a receipt for that occurrence.  Inspect permission and stop
reason before retrying.

@item billMemo is smaller than billFresh
Two reached consult or observe occurrences shared a bare-question memo key.
This is expected reuse.  Effects remain per occurrence.  Compare the annotated
trace when the difference is surprising.

@item billFresh is outside the plan range
Either the run did not execute the Plan that was priced, or a defect exists in
reporting or conformance.  Record @code{plan --raw}, the inputs, the run
facts, and the complete trace.  Every accepted branch-level Program has a sound
interval theorem.

@item generated binder names differ from the source
The authoring library prints depth names, because it cannot inspect the
spelling of a Haskell binder.  Use @code{named} when human-readable first-order
output matters.  Scope-aware alpha comparison still checks binding identity in
the curated cases.
@end table

@section Conformance and build failures

@table @asis
@item @command{tier0} reports a corpus mismatch
The Haskell codec, the guards, the string layer, or the observation diverged
from a committed Lean reply.  Treat a corpus request as specification data.
Regenerate only when you change that specification deliberately.

@item @command{tier1} cannot find the conformance oracle
Build the Lean @command{conformance-oracle} once outside the script, then rerun.
The gate refuses to build or silently skip it and must not overlap another Lean
build.

@item corpus regeneration changes files
The current Lean oracle does not produce the committed observations.  Review
the diff as a specification change.  A successful regeneration changes nothing
and leaves @code{git status --short bisim/corpus} empty.

@item Texinfo check reports missing coverage
Run @code{python3 doc/check-manual-coverage.py --list} to see the
source-derived inventory.  Add or classify the new export or CLI item in the
reference, and update its exact index entry.  Keep the checker policy aligned
with the intended public status.
@end table

@node Glossary
@chapter Glossary

This glossary gives one canonical meaning for the terms used across the manual.
Definitions point outward to detailed treatment rather than repeating it.

@table @dfn
@cindex addressee
@item addressee
The authored model, tool, executable tool, or person to whom a question is put.
It is part of semantic question identity.

@cindex answer code
@item answer code
A member of @code{Code} describing the type of an answer: text, verdict, flag,
receipt, or a structured schema.

@cindex answer source
@item answer source
Execution annotation stating that an answer was reused or was asked from a
particular dispatched question.

@cindex annotated execution trace
@item annotated execution trace
The Plan-ordered list of execution events retaining authored intent and answer
source.  Erasure yields the semantic trace.

@cindex ask node
@item ask node
A request occurrence that is written in the Plan.  The static @code{askNodes}
count covers all branch arms, and a run reaches only one selected path.

@cindex bare question
@item bare question
The typed @code{Q c} without execution intent.  It is the key for worlds,
semantic events, and reusable memo answers.

@cindex bill, fresh
@item billFresh
A price that is folded over the semantic transcript.  It counts every reached
request occurrence.

@cindex bill, memo
@item billMemo
The operational bill that deduplicates reusable bare questions between two
effects and retains every effect occurrence.

@cindex binding
@item binding
A typed answer made available to the remainder of a workflow block through a
Haskell handle and a Plan context entry.

@cindex branch rung
@item branch
The analysis rung at which finite cases are present and a finite cost multiset
contains every world's bill.

@cindex closed question
@item closed question
A request whose complete prompt is literal in the represented term.  It uses the
@code{askC} Plan form.

@cindex conformance
@item conformance
Versioned comparison between Haskell's emitted representation and observations
and the Lean oracle or its committed replies.  It establishes agreement only on
the compared fields.

@cindex consult intent
@item consult
Execution intent for an ordinary value question.  Its answer is reusable by bare
question identity.

@cindex context
@item context
The type-level list of answer codes live at a point in a Plan, written
@code{Γ}.

@cindex cost tree
@item cost tree
The finite multiset of syntactic path bills computed for a branch-level Plan
under a shape-based price.

@cindex decider
@item decider
A member of the closed pure classification vocabulary that turns bound text into
a flag without asking an addressee.

@cindex denotation
@item denotation
The meaning function from a typed Plan and environment to a dialogue.  It erases
execution intent.

@cindex dialogue
@item dialogue
A well-founded typed question-and-continuation tree.  A run in a world
produces a result, and a trace produces semantic events.

@cindex dispatched question
@item dispatched question
The operationally relabeled question actually sent after routing or fail-over.
The authored question remains in the execution event.

@cindex draw
@item draw
The question field used for deliberate resampling.  Distinct draws form distinct
questions and memo keys.

@cindex effect intent
@item effect
Occurrence-sensitive execution intent for a statement-position act.  It uses
the receipt code, bypasses reusable memo state and clears it, reserves the
effect lane, and can request permission.  It starts after every earlier occurrence in Plan order
has completed, and every later occurrence starts after it completes.

@cindex environment
@item environment
A typed product @code{Env Γ} containing one answer value for each code in the
Plan context.

@cindex evidence ceiling
@item evidence ceiling
The strongest conclusion licensed by a proof, trace, conformance record, or
transport report.  Physical effects and faithful external observations require
operational evidence beyond denotation.

@cindex first-order representation
@item first-order
A representation made only of data constructors and values, with no Haskell or
Lean closures.  @code{RawProgram} is first-order.

@cindex fresh bill
@item fresh bill
See @code{billFresh}.

@cindex function, workflow
@item function
A named straight-line open Plan over typed parameters.  Calls substitute
arguments and graft the body rather than adding runtime dispatch.

@cindex intent
@item intent
The consult, observe, or effect annotation on one Plan request occurrence.  It
guides execution and is erased by denotation.

@cindex memo table
@item memo table
The typed runtime table that is keyed by bare questions and the number of
earlier effects.  Consult and observe use it, and effects bypass it and begin
a new stretch of it.

@cindex observe intent
@item observe
Execution intent for an executable tool in value position.  Its answer is
reusable.  The tag alone does not establish that the argv is read-only.

@cindex open question
@item open question
A request whose prompt expression reads earlier answers while its shape remains
fixed.  It uses the @code{ask} Plan form and forces at least the pipeline rung.

@cindex panel
@item panel
A nonempty fan-out of questions folded in member order by the verdict algebra or
the named text-block algebra.

@cindex pipeline rung
@item pipeline
The analysis rung at which prompt words can depend on earlier answers while
question shapes and path structure stay fixed.

@cindex Plan
@item Plan
The intrinsically typed five-form workflow representation over a context and
result type.

@cindex Program
@item Program
The Haskell builder result pairing an emitted @code{RawProgram} with the typed
Haskell Plan constructed from the same authoring operations.

@cindex prompt
@item prompt
The authored words of a question.  A prompt expression can combine literals
and textual renderings of earlier answers.

@cindex question
@item question
A typed value @code{Q c} containing addressee, scope, prompt, and draw, and
requiring an answer in @code{El c}.

@cindex RawProgram
@item RawProgram
The first-order function table and main workflow emitted by Haskell and accepted
by the Lean checker on the conformance boundary.

@cindex receipt
@item receipt
The author-facing name for the unit acknowledgement code.  The first-order code
constructor and some wire positions use the name @code{ack}.

@cindex route
@item route
A live-run mapping from a serving-model pin to an ACP or agent-deck backend.
Routing is execution policy and does not alter Plan folds.

@cindex run fact
@item run fact
One of four input texts supplied by the runner about its resolved run: backends,
engine policy, routes, or sentinel.

@cindex schema
@item schema
The promoted algebra describing a structured answer as primitives, arrays, and
named product fields.

@cindex semantic event
@item semantic event
A dependent record of answer code, authored bare question, and semantic answer.

@cindex semantic trace
@item semantic trace
The ordered list of semantic events produced by dialogue trace or by erasing an
annotated execution trace.

@cindex serving model
@item serving model
The model pin that is authored on a model question.  The primary participates
in scope, and the alternates form a fail-over chain.

@cindex shape
@item shape
The part of a question known before prompt words are evaluated: addressee, scope,
and draw.  Shape-based prices support exact pipeline cost.

@cindex scope
@item scope
The authored policy axes that a question carries, among them the serving-model
axis.  The word is also used informally for the typed set of live bindings
where the context is clear.

@cindex structured answer
@item structured answer
An answer whose code carries a schema and whose semantic value is interpreted by
@code{SchemaEl}.  JSON is one validated boundary representation.

@cindex terminal
@item terminal
A workflow construction after which no statement follows in that block, such as
@code{stop}, @code{ask_}, or a complete branch.

@cindex verdict
@item verdict
The ordered algebra in which approval leaves another verdict unchanged, refusal
determines the result, and objection reason lists are concatenated.

@cindex world
@item world
A total typed answer sheet @code{Ω = (c : Code) -> Q c -> El c}.  It is the
mathematical environment in which a dialogue is observed.
@end table

